"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Monitoring systems, applications, and infrastructure': {
        'body': 'A clinic can look calm on one screen and be close to an outage on another. The job is to watch the host, the application on that host, and the network path under both, because each layer can fail alone. A check that only asks the application whether it is up stays green while the disk fills. That check never asked the host. A full disk can take a healthy application down minutes later. People call this three-layer watch monitoring systems, applications, and infrastructure.\n\nHost readings cover disk space, memory, processor load, and whether a required service is actually running. Application readings cover errors, slow requests, and the checks the program itself offers. Infrastructure readings cover link state, device health, name lookup, and whether the path through the firewall still reaches the service. Each reading needs a clock and a baseline for that hour of the day. A Tuesday morning is not judged against a Sunday night. One green light hides the layer you forgot to ask.\n\nMaya opens the clinic website on a Tuesday and the home page loads. The application log shows no new errors. On the host, the disk metric reads 96 percent and is still rising. The site files and the database share that disk. She writes a ticket with the host name, the 96 percent reading, the time in UTC, and the fact that the page still answers. The ticket records that the application view and the host view disagreed. That disagreement is the finding.\n\nA defender sets a disk threshold at 85 percent so the warning fires while there is still room to act. A separate check watches application errors, and another check confirms that the network path still answers. Maya does not restart the server as a first move. A restart can hide which job is filling the disk, and it can throw away volatile clues about that job. She leaves the three readings in the ticket so the next person does not tune the application when the host is the part that is failing. The useful response is to free space or grow the disk before the site falls over, and to keep the three checks after the crisis.\n\nStudents fold this watch into log aggregation or into alerting. Aggregation copies logs to a central store so a later wipe does not erase them. Alerting is the step that reaches a named person when a rule trips. You can copy every log and still miss a full disk if nobody measures the host. You can measure the host and still have no page if no rule calls anyone. The separating fact is the question being asked, which is whether the machine, the program, and the path are healthy right now.',
        'r0': 'The clinic web host can still answer on TCP port 443 while the disk metric reads 96 percent. Set the host warning at 85 percent so there is room to act before the disk fills. Record three numbers for the same minute: disk use, application error count, and whether the path check succeeded. Compare each one with the baseline for that hour, not with a quieter day of the week. Write the host name and the UTC time in the ticket before anyone restarts the box. A restart hides which job filled the disk and can drop volatile clues. A copied log would not by itself show the disk percentage. A page to a person comes only after a rule exists. This control is the three readings, taken together, while the site still looks up.',
        'r1': 'Watch the machine, the program, and the path.\nEach one can fail while the others look fine.\nA green page can hide a full disk.\nRead disk space, memory, and processor load.\nRead errors and slow requests from the program.\nRead whether the path still answers.\nCompare a reading with the usual hour.\nDo not restart the box as a first move.\nA restart can hide what filled the disk.\nFree space before the site falls over.\nKeep a separate check for each layer.\nA stored copy of logs is another job.\nA page sent to a person is another job.',
        'r2': 'A night nurse checks the person in the bed, the chart on the door, and the power serving that room before she calls the room fine. A calm patient and a tidy chart can still sit in a room whose power lead is loose. She writes which of the three is wrong instead of treating every trouble as a fever. The night reading is compared with other nights, not with a crowded morning. Nobody throws the chart away to make the panel look calm. In the real work the patient stands for the application, the room stands for the host, and the power lead stands for the path. A single calm chart is not proof that all three are fine.',
        'r4': 'Open the host view, the application view, and the path view for the same minute. Write down disk use, the error count, and whether the service still answers. Note the host name and the time. Do not restart the host yet. Do not delete logs just to free disk space until you know what grew. Compare the disk reading with the usual value for that hour. If only one layer is bad, say so in the ticket. Leave the other checks in place after you free space or grow the disk.',
        'exam': 'A quiet application log does not prove the host is healthy, and a healthy host does not prove the application is answering. Do not call this watch log aggregation. That work keeps a copy of logs somewhere else. Do not call it alerting. That work reaches a person when a rule trips. A green check that only asks the application if it is up will miss a filling disk. The fact that separates them is which layer you measured. Measure the host, the application, and the path, or you do not have this control.',
    },
    'Log aggregation': {
        'body': "Authentication events are useless after the server that held them is wiped, rebuilt, or stolen. The remedy is to copy those events to a central collector while the host is still alive. That copy is log aggregation. It runs on a short interval, about once a minute, rather than waiting for a month-end bundle that may never leave the disk. The central store keeps the lines after the source is gone. A quiet search later means you look at the copy, not at a machine that no longer exists.\n\nThe usual path for a Linux host or a network device is syslog. When the segment between the host and the collector is not fully trusted, syslog rides inside TLS on TCP port 6514. Classic syslog on UDP port 514 sends the same kind of line in the clear, so you turn that listener down after 6514 is proven. A device that cannot speak 6514 may use an authenticated agent instead. Each line should carry the host name, the clock on the source, and the clock on the collector. Two hosts with similar names must not merge into one story. Windows security events travel the same idea: success is event 4624 and failure is event 4625, and both should arrive at the collector within that same minute.\n\nJamal administers the clinic file server. Every minute the server ships its authentication lines to the collector. On Friday an attacker wipes the server after a burst of failed logons. The local security log is gone with the disk. The collector still has the 4625 lines from the burst and the 4624 line that succeeded just before the wipe. Jamal reads those lines from the collector and writes the times, the account name, and the source address into the ticket. The wipe did not get a vote on the copy.\n\nA defender treats a silent forwarder as an incident of its own. An empty search is not the same as a quiet host. If the collector received nothing for several minutes, the page should say the ship stopped. The accounts that administer the source hosts should not have the right to delete the collector's copy. Otherwise the person who wipes the server can wipe the story too. Aggregation is the hot copy you search this week. It is not the cheaper locked store where older years sit, and it is not a judgment about guilt.\n\nStudents call the central copy the same thing as monitoring or as alerting. Monitoring asks whether the host, the application, and the path are healthy now. Alerting reaches a person when a rule trips. A full disk can be watched and paged even when no log line has been copied. A copied 4625 line can sit unread if nobody is paged. The separating fact is survival of the record: the collector still has the line after the source disk is gone.",
        'r0': "Ship authentication lines about once a minute so a same-day wipe still leaves the central copy. Prefer syslog inside TLS on TCP port 6514 when the path to the collector is not fully trusted, and turn down cleartext syslog on UDP port 514 after 6514 is proven. A device that cannot speak 6514 can use an authenticated agent. Stamp each line with the host name, the source clock, and the collector clock so two similar names do not merge. Windows success is event 4624 and Windows failure is event 4625, and both belong in that minute's ship. Alarm a forwarder that goes silent. Deny delete rights on the collector to the accounts that administer the source hosts. The hot copy is not the older locked archive.",
        'r1': "Copy the logs to another machine.\nDo it often, not once a month.\nKeep that copy after the first machine is wiped.\nPut the machine's name on every line.\nPut the time on every line too.\nDo not let two machines share one name.\nA quiet search is not a quiet machine.\nIf the copy stops arriving, tell someone.\nDo not let the same admin delete the copy.\nThe recent copy is not the old locked store.\nThe copy does not decide who is guilty.\nA health reading is a different job.\nA page to a person is a different job.",
        'r2': "A shop keeps a second ledger in another building and copies each sale the same hour. If the shop burns, the other building still shows the day's lines. Each line names the shop so two shops do not become one. The clerk in the shop cannot scratch out yesterday in the other building. A bell rings there when a shop stops sending lines. Recent pages stay on the desk, and older seasons go to a high shelf that is a different decision. That second ledger is log aggregation. It is the copy that remains after the source is gone, not the health check and not the bell itself.",
        'r4': 'Find where the host sends its logs and how often. Write down the collector name, the host name on a sample line, and both clocks. Confirm a recent authentication line is on the collector, then note what a wipe of the source would still leave. Do not delete the collector copy. Do not grant the source admins delete rights there. Check whether a forwarder that goes silent would page anyone. Leave the older locked store for a separate decision. Do not rebuild the host until the copy is proven.',
        'exam': 'The central copy is what remains after the source disk is wiped, rebuilt, or stolen. Do not call that copy monitoring. Monitoring asks whether the host, the application, and the path are healthy now. Do not call it alerting. Alerting reaches a person when a rule trips. Do not call the hot copy the long archive. The archive is the older locked store. A silent forwarder is not a quiet host. An empty search can mean the ship stopped. Survival of the record is the fact that separates this work.',
    },
    'Alerting': {
        'body': 'A rule that changes color on a dashboard has not yet told anyone. Alerting is the step that turns a tripped rule into a message a person receives, with an owner and a written first step. If the message has no owner, it is a log line with extra machinery. The person on call needs to know what to open first, not merely that something beeped. Without that, the spike waits until morning while the account is already in use.\n\nThe rule watches a count, a threshold, or a missing heartbeat. One failed-logon rule trips when event 4625 arrives fifteen times in five minutes for one account. The action is a page, a ticket, or both, sent to the on-call rotation rather than to a mailbox nobody opens. The message names the host, the account, the count, and the first step. That first step says to call the user at the number already on file and, if the user did not log on, disable the account. Disabling the account is containment. It is not eradication, which would mean removing the cause after the evidence is kept. The collector clock is the one the five-minute window should trust, because a skewed source clock can make the window lie.\n\nNora is on call Tuesday night for the clinic. At 02:14 the pager shows twenty-two event 4625 lines for the account jsmith on the file server, then one event 4624 from an address the clinic does not use. The written first step says to call the user at the number on file. The user is asleep and did not log on, so Nora disables the account. She opens a ticket with the counts, the address, and the time. The disable is containment. How the password left the clinic is a later question.\n\nA defender tests the page with a planned drill so the on-call phone actually rings. The rule, the owner, and the first step are written where the next person can find them at night. A dashboard tile with no route to a person is monitoring, not this step. A log line sitting in the collector is aggregation, not this step. If the same page fires all night because a backup reports success as a warning, that noise is a tuning problem. You do not solve tuning by throwing the pager away.\n\nStudents treat any visible alarm as this kind of notice. A graph that nobody watches does not reach a person. A stored event 4625 line does not reach a person either. The separating fact is delivery to an owner who has a first step. Disabling the stolen account is containment, not eradication. It is also not a substitute for keeping the log copy in the collector.',
        'r0': 'Page a named owner when event 4625 hits fifteen times in five minutes for one account, and include any following event 4624 from an address the clinic does not use. The message must name the host, the account, the count, and the first step. That step is a callback to the number already on file, then a disable if the person did not log on. The disable is containment, not eradication. Trust the collector clock for the five-minute window so a skewed source clock cannot hide the burst. Send the page to the on-call rotation, not to a mailbox nobody opens. Drill the phone until it rings. A dashboard color change with no route to a person is not this control.',
        'r1': 'A color change on a screen is not enough.\nSomeone has to receive the message.\nName the person who owns that message.\nWrite the first step that person should take.\nA message with no owner is only noise.\nFailed attempts to sign in should reach the person on call.\nThe first step can be to disable the account.\nThat disable holds the line.\nIt does not remove the cause.\nTest that the phone actually rings.\nA stored log is not this step.\nA health chart is not this step.\nIf the phone path fails, have a second path.',
        'r2': 'A clinic bell rings in a room where a named nurse is awake, and a card beside the bell says the first action. A bell in an empty room changes nothing. A note in a drawer is not a bell. If the bell wire breaks, a second wire should still reach someone. The card might say to call the person and, if they are not the cause, take their key away for now. Taking the key is a pause, not a rebuilt lock. That bell, with an owner and a first action, is alerting. A chart on the wall and a stored diary are not the same thing.',
        'r4': 'Open the rule that should page on a failed-logon spike. Write down the owner, the threshold, and the first step. Confirm the page goes to a person on call, not only to a mailbox. Do not change the threshold yet. Do not disable the account until you have followed the written step and called the number on file. Note whether the collector clock is the clock the window uses. Schedule a drill that proves the phone rings. Leave noisy backup pages for a tuning change, and do not throw the pager away.',
        'exam': 'A notice counts only when it reaches an owner who has a written first step. A dashboard color with nobody attached is monitoring, not this step. A line sitting in a collector is aggregation, not this step. Disabling the account after a failed-logon spike is containment, not eradication. Eradication removes the cause after evidence is kept. Do not throw the pager away because a backup page is noisy. That noise is a tuning problem. No owner means the message is still just a log line with extra machinery.',
    },
    'Alert tuning': {
        'body': 'Too many pages teach people to ignore all of them. The careful change is called alert tuning, and it reduces noise so real events are not lost. It writes down why the change was safe. It is not a quiet delete of a detection you still need. A backup job that pages on every success is noise. A burst of event 4625 is not noise. The work is to tell those apart and to leave a note the next analyst can read.\n\nTuning can raise a threshold, add a short exception, or match a known maintenance window. The exception needs an owner and an end date. A permanent mute is how a fire bell gets taped over. The change record should say what was firing, how often, and what still pages. If the backup alert fired forty times because the job reports success as a warning, change the condition so success is only a log. Failure of that backup must still page. Do not fold the failed-logon rule into that same quieting. The event 4625 rule stays loud.\n\nSamir reviews the clinic night pages on a Wednesday. The backup alert fired forty times, and each line said the copy succeeded. He changes that rule so a success is recorded and only a failed backup pages. He writes the note on the rule: success was the noise, and failure stays. The same night the failed-logon rule is left at fifteen event 4625 lines in five minutes. He does not raise that number to make the queue look empty. In the ticket he records the old threshold, the new one, and the date.\n\nA defender reviews tuned rules on a schedule, because a temporary exception becomes a hole if nobody ends it. Before a change, count how many true cases the old rule caught last month. After the change, confirm those cases would still match. If you cannot explain the note, do not ship the tune. Suppression that hides a whole host is not tuning. That is blindness. The page for a real intrusion has to stay able to ring. Review the note again at the end date and either restore the old rule or extend it in writing.\n\nStudents confuse this work with quarantine and with archiving. Quarantine isolates a suspect file or host so it cannot spread. Archiving moves old logs into a locked store. Neither choice decides how loud a live rule should be. The separating fact is the detection you keep. The chatty success page may be narrowed, and the failed-logon page stays loud, with a written note.',
        'r0': 'A backup rule that fired forty successes in one night should record success and page only on failure, with that reason written on the rule. Leave the failed-logon rule at fifteen event 4625 lines in five minutes. Do not raise it to empty the queue. Any exception needs an owner and an end date, and the note is reviewed when that date arrives. Before the change, count true cases from the last month. After the change, confirm those cases would still match. Suppression of a whole host is not a tune. The event 4625 page stays loud while the chatty success page is narrowed.',
        'r1': 'Too many warnings teach people to ignore all of them.\nQuiet the noise.\nDo not silence the real warning.\nA backup that always says success is noise.\nA burst of failed sign in attempts is not noise.\nWrite down why you changed a rule.\nGive an exception an owner and an end.\nDo not make a mute last forever.\nLeave the failed sign in warning loud.\nCount how often the old rule was right.\nAfter the change, those cases should still match.\nA taped warning is not a tune.\nHiding a whole machine is not a tune.',
        'r2': 'A kitchen has a fire bell and a dozen timers. One timer rings every time a loaf finishes, even when the loaf is fine, and cooks start ignoring every ring. The cook rewrites that timer so only a burned loaf rings, and writes the reason on the timer. The fire bell is left loud. A piece of tape over the fire bell is not a repair. Old recipe cards in a locked drawer are not this task either. Rewriting the noisy timer, while the fire bell stays loud, is alert tuning. The note is what keeps the change from becoming a hidden mute.',
        'r4': "List the pages from last night and mark which ones were success noise. Read the failed-logon rule and write down its threshold. Do not raise that threshold. Do not mute it. For the noisy success rule, write the new condition and the reason on the rule itself. Add an owner and an end date if you must make an exception. Count last month's true cases before you ship the change. Confirm those cases still match. Do not hide a whole host to make the queue look empty.",
        'exam': 'Tuning narrows noise and leaves a note. It does not delete a detection you still need. The chatty backup success may be logged instead of paged. The failed-logon rule stays loud. A permanent mute is not a tune. Hiding a whole host is not a tune. Do not confuse this with quarantine, which isolates a suspect file or host. Do not confuse it with archiving, which moves old logs into a locked store. An exception without an owner and an end date becomes a hole.',
    },
    'Quarantine': {
        'body': 'A suspect file that can still run, or a suspect host that can still talk, keeps spreading while you read about it. Quarantine separates that file or that host so the spread stops and the evidence stays. For a host, you remove it from the network and you leave the power on. Memory is more volatile than disk. A casual power-off throws the memory away before anyone can look. The machine clock should keep running so later notes match a system that is still alive.\n\nA file move puts the suspect where it cannot execute, and you record the path and a SHA-256 hash before anyone deletes a copy. You do not destroy the only copy as your first act. A host cut blocks ordinary user traffic. If an analyst must still reach the machine, that path is narrow and logged. This pause is containment in the incident handling order. It is not eradication. Eradication deletes the malware and closes the hole after the evidence you need has been preserved.\n\nElena finds a clinic workstation that has started to encrypt a folder of shared files. She pulls the network cable, or she applies the control that drops the workstation off the staff network, and she leaves the power on. She writes the host name, the time, and the SHA-256 of the suspicious file before anyone deletes a copy. The user is told not to reboot. Memory on that live machine may still hold the process that started the encryption. The ticket says containment, not cleanup.\n\nA defender does not confuse this pause with a finished case. The host stays isolated until a named person says the look is done. Notes record who isolated it and when. A chain of custody form, if the case needs one, is a separate record of every hand that held an item. This pause does not fill that form by itself. It also does not by itself create a legal hold on ordinary deletion elsewhere. What it does is stop the spread while the live evidence is still there.\n\nStudents treat the pause as alert tuning or as archiving, or they call a power-off a cure. Tuning only changes how loud a rule is. Archiving only moves old logs. Turning the workstation off, or deleting the file before the hash is written, destroys evidence and is not the isolation step. The separating fact is containment with the evidence kept: off the network, still running, hash recorded, and removal of the malware left for eradication after that.',
        'r0': 'Pull the suspect workstation off the network and leave the power on so memory is not thrown away. Record the host name, the time, and a SHA-256 of the suspect file before anyone deletes a copy. Tell the user not to reboot. If an analyst still needs a path in, keep it narrow and logged. This pause is containment, not eradication. Eradication deletes the malware and closes the hole only after the evidence is preserved. A chain of custody form is a separate record and is not filled by the pause itself. Do not call a power-off a cure. The clock on the live machine should keep running.',
        'r1': 'Separate the suspect file or the suspect machine.\nStop it from spreading while you look.\nPull the machine off the network.\nLeave the power on.\nMemory disappears if you turn it off.\nWrite down the file identity before you delete anything.\nDo not restart yet.\nTell the user not to restart.\nRecord the time while the machine is still running.\nThis pause is not the final cleanup.\nCleaning out the cause comes later.\nA quieter alarm is a different job.\nOld locked logs are a different job.',
        'r2': "A clinic moves a suspect sample into a side room, shuts the public door, and leaves the lights on so the sample can still be studied. Nobody dumps the sample in the trash to feel done. A curator keeps a key and writes who moved the sample and at what hour. Quieting a noisy alarm clock in the hall is not this move. Putting last year's charts on a high shelf is not this move either. The side room with the lights on is quarantine. It stops the spread while the evidence is still there to study, and the later destruction of the sample is a different step.",
        'r4': 'Confirm the host is off the ordinary network and still powered. Write the host name, the time, and the hash of the suspect file. Tell the user not to restart. Do not delete the only copy. Do not power the machine off. If you keep a path for the analyst, write down how narrow it is. Mark the ticket as containment. Leave eradication, the removal of the malware and the close of the hole, until the evidence you need is preserved. Do not change alert rules or move old logs as a substitute.',
        'exam': 'Isolating the host or the file is containment. Leaving the power on keeps volatile memory. A power-off is not the cure, and deleting the file before the hash is written destroys evidence. Eradication comes later: remove the malware and close the hole after evidence is preserved. Do not call this pause alert tuning. Tuning only changes how loud a rule is. Do not call it archiving. Archiving moves old logs. A custody form is a separate record and is not created by the pause itself.',
    },
    'Archiving logs': {
        'body': 'Hot storage is for the logs you still search every day. Archiving logs is the later move: keep them for the full retention period in a store that costs less and is still protected from tampering. Ninety days may stay in the hot store. A year of older lines may sit in the archive. The archive is not a folder anyone can edit. If an attacker or a careless admin can rewrite last spring, you do not have an archive. You have a copy with no integrity.\n\nThe mechanism is a scheduled copy from the hot collector into the locked store, with a hash so you can see tampering later. Access to delete or alter that store should not belong to the same accounts that administer the source hosts. The archive can be slower to open. That is acceptable. What is not acceptable is a retention clock that deletes day ninety-one from the hot store and never landed those lines in the archive. Write the retention numbers down: how long the hot store keeps a line, how long the archive keeps it, who may read, and who may not delete.\n\nPriya checks the clinic collector in October. The hot store holds the last ninety days, and a search for event 4624 still returns quickly. Counsel asks for the authentication lines from eleven months ago. Those lines are not in the hot store. They are in the locked archive, and the hash recorded at archive time still matches. Priya writes the request, the date range, and the hash check into the ticket. She does not restore the old lines into the hot store just to look at them, because that would mix ages without need.\n\nA defender tests a restore of one old day into a side location, not over the hot store, so you know the archive can actually be read. The test result is written down. A store you have never read back is a hope, not a retention plan. Do not shorten the year because disk looks expensive until the policy owner agrees. Do not leave the archive open to every account to make the search easier. Protection from tampering is part of the definition, not an optional extra.\n\nStudents mix the archive with the hot aggregation copy, or with tuning, or with quarantine. Aggregation is the ongoing ship into the store you search this week. Tuning changes alert noise. Quarantine isolates a suspect system. The archive is older lines, kept for the required time, locked against change. The separating fact is retention with integrity: cheaper storage, still not editable by the people who run the source hosts.',
        'r0': 'Keep ninety days in the hot store, where a search for event 4624 still returns quickly, and keep a year of older lines in the locked archive. Copy on a schedule, and store a hash at archive time so tampering is visible. Day ninety-one must already be in the archive before the hot store deletes it. Deny delete and alter rights to the accounts that administer the source hosts. Test a restore of one old day into a side location, not over the hot store. Counsel asking for lines from eleven months ago should be answered from the archive, with the hash check written in the ticket. Do not shorten the year on cost alone.',
        'r1': 'Keep logs for the whole time the rule requires.\nRecent logs can stay where you search them fast.\nOlder logs can move to a cheaper place.\nThat cheaper place must still be locked.\nPeople who run the source machines should not edit it.\nDo not delete the recent copy until the old copy is safe.\nTest that you can read an old day back.\nRead it in a side place, not over the live copy.\nWrite down how long each store is kept.\nPulling a machine off the network is a different job.\nA store you have never read is only a hope.',
        'r2': "A records room keeps this month's folders on a desk where clerks can search them quickly. Older years go into locked boxes in a cheaper warehouse. The boxes are not open for anyone to rewrite a page. The people who wrote the pages do not hold the warehouse key. Once a year someone opens one box and reads a page, so the clinic knows the boxes are not just weight. Changing which bell rings in the hall is not this move. That warehouse of locked older folders is the log archive. It keeps the required years, cheaper than the desk, and still protected from tampering.",
        'r4': 'Write down the hot retention and the archive retention. Confirm day ninety-one is already in the archive before the hot store can drop it. Check who can delete or alter the archive, and do not leave that right with the source admins. Do not restore old lines over the hot store. Pull one old day into a side location and record whether the hash matches. Do not shorten the year yet, even if disk looks expensive. Leave alert noise and host isolation for their own decisions.',
        'exam': 'The archive keeps logs for the required time in cheaper storage that is still protected from tampering. Ninety hot days you can search are not the year in the locked store. Do not call the archive the live ship into the collector. That ongoing copy is aggregation. Do not call it tuning, which changes alert noise, or quarantine, which isolates a suspect system. A store nobody has read back is a hope. Deleting day ninety-one before it lands in the archive breaks retention. Source admins must not be able to rewrite the archive.',
    },
    'Security information and event management': {
        'body': "A pile of logs from many hosts is not yet a case. A SIEM collects those logs, normalizes the fields so a logon looks similar no matter the source, and correlates events that belong together. It does not decide guilt by itself. A person still judges the alert and opens the ticket. Correlation can show that a failed logon, a success, and a new process share a host and a short window of time. That join is the product. The verdict is not.\n\nCollectors feed the platform. Parsers map each source into common fields: time, host, account, source address, and result. A rule then looks across those fields. One useful pattern is event 4625 followed by event 4624 for the same account, then a process start on that host that the host has not run before. The platform can open an alert for that pattern. It should not disable accounts or isolate hosts merely because the rule matched, unless a separate playbook says a person has approved that action. Clocks need to be close, or the window joins events that did not really overlap. NTP keeps those clocks honest.\n\nLeah is the analyst on duty when an alert fires at 01:10 UTC. The alert ties eleven event 4625 lines and one event 4624 for a nurse account to a new process on the same file server two minutes later. Leah opens a ticket and writes the account, the host, the process name, and the two times. She does not mark the nurse guilty because the rule matched. She calls the number on file and asks whether that logon was expected. The platform gathered the clues. Leah decides what they mean.\n\nA defender keeps the parsers healthy, because a broken parser is a silent gap. If Windows security events stop arriving, the correlation rule cannot see 4624 or 4625, and an empty alert queue looks like a quiet night. Someone should page on a source that goes silent. This platform is also not a place to dump every packet. Flow summaries and device traps may be inputs. The judgment remains a person's. Content that must be blocked inside a mail message is a different control, even if the block event later arrives here to be joined with a logon.\n\nStudents expect the platform to be data loss prevention, endpoint detection, or a single device trap. Data loss prevention looks at sensitive content leaving by mail, web, or a device. Endpoint detection watches behavior on one host and can isolate that host. A device trap is one pushed event, such as a link down. The separating fact is correlation across sources, with a person still judging the alert. A match is not guilt.",
        'r0': 'Normalize time, host, account, address, and result, then correlate event 4625, a following event 4624 for the same account, and a process start on that host inside a short window. Eleven failures, one success, and a new process two minutes later are enough for Leah to open a ticket at 01:10 UTC. NTP keeps the clocks close so the window does not join strangers. The platform must not disable the account or isolate the host merely because the rule matched, unless a person approved that action in the playbook. Page when a source goes silent, because a broken parser looks like a quiet night. A person still judges. The match is not guilt.',
        'r1': "Many logs in one pile are not yet a case.\nPut them in one place and line up the fields.\nJoin events that share a machine and a short time.\nA failed sign in and a new program can be one story.\nThe tool does not decide guilt.\nA person still has to judge.\nOpen a ticket when the join looks real.\nCall the person before you blame them.\nA broken feed looks like a quiet night.\nPage someone if a source goes silent.\nBlocking a mail message is a different job.\nWatching one computer's behavior is a different job.",
        'r2': 'Detectives lay notes from many rooms on one table and line up the times. A failed knock, a successful entry, and a new tool in the same room can be one story when the times sit close. The table does not declare a verdict. A detective still calls the person and writes a case card. If one room stops sending notes, the empty table looks peaceful and is not. Blocking a letter at the door is a different job. That shared table is security information and event management. It gathers and joins the clues in time order, and a person still judges.',
        'r4': 'Pick one correlated alert and write the account, the host, the process, and the times. Check that the clocks used in the window agree. Do not mark the person guilty from the match alone. Do not disable the account or isolate the host unless the written steps say a person has approved that. Confirm the Windows security source is still arriving. If it has gone silent, note that gap before you trust an empty queue. Leave mail-content blocks and single-host isolation as other controls, even if their events show up here.',
        'exam': 'A SIEM collects, normalizes, and correlates. It does not decide guilt. A person opens the ticket and judges the alert. A match of event 4625, event 4624, and a new process is a clue, not a verdict. Do not call this data loss prevention, which inspects content leaving by mail, web, or a device. Do not call it endpoint detection, which can isolate one host. Do not call a single device trap this platform. A silent source looks like a quiet night, so a broken parser is part of the failure.',
    },
    'Data loss prevention': {
        'body': 'Secrets leave in ordinary mail more often than they leave through a clever channel. Data loss prevention watches for sensitive content on the way out, by mail, by the web, or by a removable device, and it blocks the transfer or raises an alert. The decision is about the content, not about whether a logon looked strange. A message full of card numbers is the kind of thing the control is built to stop. A person who is allowed to send mail is not thereby allowed to send that set.\n\nOn the mail path, the gateway inspects the message body and the attachments before the message is delivered. A rule can look for a full set of card numbers, for a file marked sensitive, or for a pattern the clinic has defined, such as a patient identifier next to a diagnosis. The action is block, or alert and deliver, and the choice should be written in policy. A block should tell the sender enough to fix an honest mistake without teaching anyone how to sneak the same file past the rule. Web uploads and a copy to a USB device are the same idea on other paths: the content is inspected at a point you control.\n\nOwen in billing attaches a spreadsheet of card numbers to a message meant for a partner bank. The mail gateway matches the full set, blocks the message, and shows Owen a notice that the message was not sent. A copy of the event, with the sender, the time, and the rule name, goes to the security queue. The spreadsheet itself is not pasted into a chat about the block. The ticket says the block worked and names the rule. Owen uses the approved transfer for that partner instead of mail.\n\nA defender tests the rule with a sample that should block and a sample that should pass, and writes both results down. A rule that never fires may be pointed at the wrong path. A rule that fires on every newsletter will be bypassed by staff who need to work. Narrow the noise without deleting the card-number rule. This control does not replace a SIEM. The SIEM may receive the block event and correlate it with a logon. The block itself happened because the content was inspected.\n\nStudents call this a SIEM, a firewall, or a device trap. A firewall can stop a port. It does not read a spreadsheet of card numbers inside a mail message. A SIEM correlates events a person will judge. A device trap says a switch link dropped. The separating fact is inspection of sensitive content on the way out, with a block or an alert. Stopping the message is the control. It is not a guess about guilt from a logon pattern.',
        'r0': 'Inspect mail body and attachments before delivery, and block a message that contains a full set of card numbers. The same idea covers a web upload and a copy onto a USB device. Policy chooses block, or alert and deliver, and the sender sees a notice that does not teach a bypass. Test one sample that must block and one that must pass. Keep the card-number rule even if you narrow a noisy newsletter match. Record sender, time, and rule name. Do not paste the spreadsheet into the ticket chat. A SIEM may later join the block event to a logon, but the block happened because the content was read, not because a port was closed.',
        'r1': 'Watch for secrets leaving the building.\nMail, the web, and a removable device are the paths.\nLook at what is inside the message.\nA full set of card numbers should not go out by mail.\nBlock that message or raise an alert.\nTell the sender the message was not sent.\nDo not paste the secret into the ticket.\nTest a sample that should block.\nTest a sample that should pass.\nDo not let a noisy rule get deleted.\nA strange sign in is a different problem.\nA closed network door is a different problem.',
        'r2': 'A door checker opens the bag, not merely the badge. A letter full of card numbers is stopped, and the sender is told it did not go. A harmless letter goes through. The checker does not decide the person is a thief from the badge alone. A log of who walked the hall is a different desk. A bell that says a pipe burst is a different desk too. Opening the bag for sensitive content, and blocking or warning, is data loss prevention. The badge alone never shows what is in the letter. The content on the way out is the thing being watched.',
        'r4': 'Find the mail rule that should catch card numbers. Send a test message that must block and a test message that must pass. Write both results, the rule name, and the time. Do not paste the sensitive file into the ticket. Read the notice the sender would see, and confirm it does not explain how to sneak the file through. Check whether web upload and USB copy are covered or still open. Do not delete the card rule to quiet a newsletter. Leave logon correlation to the SIEM.',
        'exam': 'This control inspects sensitive content leaving by mail, web, or a device, then blocks or alerts. A full set of card numbers in mail is the case. A firewall that closes a port does not read that spreadsheet. A SIEM may receive the block event, but the SIEM does not do the inspection. A device trap reports a link or a temperature, not the contents of a message. Do not paste the secret into the ticket. Narrow noise without deleting the card-number rule. The decision is about content, not about a strange logon.',
    },
    'SNMP traps': {
        'body': 'Some devices should speak when something breaks, instead of waiting to be asked. An SNMP trap is that push. The device sends a message to a listener when an event occurs, such as a trunk port dropping or a temperature alarm. The listener does not have to poll every interface every few seconds to learn about that event. Polling is the other direction: the manager asks, and the device answers. A trap is the device speaking first.\n\nTraps travel on UDP port 162. The poll, often called a get, uses UDP port 161. Version matters. SNMPv3 can authenticate the message and can encrypt it. Older versions that rely on a community string send a shared secret that is easy to reuse and often travel in the clear. A trap that anyone can forge is a way to hide a real outage under fake alarms, or to invent an outage that did not happen. Use SNMPv3 from the switch to the listener, and do not leave a default community string in place. The listener should know which device is allowed to send, so a random host cannot flood the console with traps.\n\nKim watches the clinic network listener. At 03:05 a switch sends a trap because a trunk port dropped. The trap names the switch, the interface, and the time. The listener opens a ticket assigned to the network on-call. Kim checks that the trunk is the uplink between the clinic floors and confirms the link light is out. She does not confuse this with a flow record. Nothing in the trap says which workstations were talking. It says a port on that switch went down. The ticket gets the switch name, the interface, and the trap time.\n\nA defender tests a trap by dropping a lab interface, or by using the device test action, and confirms the ticket opens. If traps stop arriving from a switch that used to send them, treat the silence as a fault. A listener that is down turns every later outage into a surprise. Keep the listener clock aligned so the trap time matches other logs. Do not use the trap as a reason to skip a later look at what the devices were carrying. The trap is the doorbell. It is not the conversation and it is not a verdict.\n\nStudents mix a trap with a poll, with a flow summary, or with a SIEM verdict. A poll is the manager asking on UDP port 161. A flow summary says who talked to whom and how much, without the payload. A SIEM may receive the trap and join it to other events, and a person still judges that join. The separating fact is the push: the device speaks first, on UDP port 162, about its own event, such as a trunk down or a temperature alarm.',
        'r0': 'A trap is a push to a listener on UDP port 162. A poll, or get, is the manager asking on UDP port 161. Use SNMPv3 so the message is authenticated and can be encrypted, and remove any default community string that travels in the clear. At 03:05 a clinic switch can send a trap because a trunk port dropped. The trap should name the switch, the interface, and the time, and the listener should open a ticket. Test by dropping a lab interface. If a switch that used to send traps goes silent, treat that silence as a fault. A forged trap from an unknown host is a reason to restrict who may send. The trap does not list who was talking.',
        'r1': 'Some devices should speak up when something breaks.\nThey push a message to a listener.\nThey do not wait to be asked.\nA dropped link is one reason to speak.\nA temperature alarm is another reason.\nAsking the device on a schedule is a different habit.\nUse the protected form of the message.\nA shared password in the clear is weak.\nTest that a real break opens a ticket.\nIf a device goes silent, treat that as a fault.\nA summary of who talked to whom is a different record.\nThe listener does not decide guilt.',
        'r2': 'A boiler calls the front desk when it overheats, instead of waiting for the hourly walk. The call names the boiler and the fault. A guard who walks over and asks each machine how it feels is doing the other job. A tally of which carts passed the loading dock is not this call. The front desk does not convict anyone. It opens a work card. That call the machine places on its own is the trap a device sends when something breaks. It is the device speaking first about its own fault, not a poll and not a tally of conversations.',
        'r4': 'Find the listener and write the switch name, interface, and time from one real trap. Note whether the message is the protected version or an old shared string in the clear. Do not change production strings until the protected path is tested. Drop a lab interface, or use the device test action, and confirm a ticket opens. Check what happens if that switch stops sending. Do not treat the trap as a list of conversations. Align the listener clock before you compare the trap time with other logs.',
        'exam': 'A trap is the device pushing an event, such as a trunk down or a heat alarm, to a listener on UDP port 162. A poll is the manager asking on UDP port 161. SNMPv3 authenticates and can encrypt. A community string in the clear is the weak form. Do not call a trap a flow summary of who talked to whom. Do not call it a guilt verdict. A SIEM may join the trap later, and a person still judges. Silence from a switch that used to speak is a fault.',
    },
    'NetFlow': {
        'body': "Packet captures are too heavy to keep for every link all week. NetFlow records a summary of a conversation instead of the payload, and IPFIX does the same kind of summary. A record can show the two addresses, the ports, the protocol, the start and end, and how many packets and bytes moved. You learn who talked to whom and how much. You do not learn what was said. That limit is the point. The summary is small enough to keep, and it is enough to spot a workstation sending far more than it usually does.\n\nA router or a switch exports the records to a collector. Classic export often uses UDP port 2055. IPFIX, the newer standard of the same idea, often uses UDP port 4739. Fast links are sometimes sampled, and the record should say the sample rate, or a byte count will look smaller than the wire. Clocks on the exporter and the collector need to agree before you join a flow to a Windows event 4624 or 4625. The collector should say which device exported the summary, so two sites do not blend. The export path and the stored records are sensitive, because the map of who talks to whom is itself worth protecting.\n\nNoah reviews flow records for the clinic after a night alarm. One workstation sent several gigabytes, starting near 01:00, to an address nobody on the team recognizes. The record shows the workstation address, the far address, TCP port 443 on the far side, and the byte count. It does not show a file name or any text. Noah writes those fields into the ticket and asks for a short packet capture only on that pair, if the content itself must be read. He also checks event 4624 on that workstation around 01:00, after the clocks are lined up, to see which account was logged on.\n\nA defender lists the segments that have no exporter, because those links are blind spots. A baseline for each host, by hour, keeps a busy backup server from looking like theft. Night volume that dwarfs that host's own week is the thing to page on. Do not treat the flow as proof of what was inside the bytes. When you need the words, capture packets for a short window and only for the flow you already named. Protect the collector. A stolen map of every conversation is a gift to the next intrusion.\n\nStudents treat the summary as a packet capture, as a device trap, or as an intrusion signature. A capture holds payload. A trap says a device had an event, such as a port down, and it does not list every conversation. A signature hunts a known attack trace inside the bytes, which this summary never stored. The separating fact is the summary itself: peers, ports, protocol, timing, and bytes, without the transcript, often exported on UDP port 2055 or, for IPFIX, on UDP port 4739.",
        'r0': 'Export conversation summaries, not payloads. Classic NetFlow often uses UDP port 2055, and IPFIX often uses UDP port 4739. Record both addresses, both ports, the protocol, the times, the packets, and the bytes. If the exporter samples a fast link, keep the sample rate on the record or the byte count will understate the wire. A clinic workstation sending several gigabytes near 01:00 to an unrecognized address, with TCP port 443 on the far side, is a ticket. Join that flow to event 4624 only after the exporter clock and the host clock agree. Capture packets only for that pair if you must read content. List segments with no exporter as blind spots.',
        'r1': "Keep a summary of who talked to whom.\nRecord how much was sent.\nDo not keep every word that was said.\nNote the two sides and the times.\nNote which device wrote the summary.\nA fast link may count only some of the talk.\nWrite that down so the size is not a lie.\nA huge send at night is worth a look.\nCompare it with that machine's normal week.\nRead the words only for the few talks that matter.\nA device crying that a link died is different.\nA known bad pattern in the words is different.",
        'r2': "A station clerk tallies which trains met, how long they stood, and how full they were. The tally has no transcript of any passenger talk. A night train far heavier than that platform's ordinary week stands out. The tally names the station that wrote it so two lines are not confused. Platforms nobody tallies are written down as blind. A boiler calling about heat is a different message. A wanted poster of a known thief is a different message too. That tally is a conversation summary. It records who met and how much moved between them, not the words inside.",
        'r4': 'Open one night of flow records and pick a host whose volume looks high. Write the two addresses, the ports, the protocol, the times, and the byte count. Note which exporter produced the record and whether it samples. Do not assume the bytes prove a file name. Do not start a full packet capture of every link. If you need content, capture only that pair for a short window. Check the host logon only after the clocks match. List any segment that has no exporter. Do not leave the collector open to every account.',
        'exam': 'A flow record summarizes peers, ports, protocol, timing, and bytes. It does not store the payload, so it is not a packet capture. It is not a device trap, which reports an event such as a port down. It is not an intrusion signature, which matches a known bad pattern in content. Classic export is often UDP port 2055, and IPFIX is often UDP port 4739. A missing sample rate makes a byte count look too small. Clocks must agree before you join a flow to event 4624. Segments with no exporter are blind spots.',
    },
    'Endpoint detection and response': {
        'body': "Network summaries cannot name the process that touched a file. Endpoint detection and response puts an agent on the host to watch behavior there: processes, file changes, and other actions a flow record will never show. The same agent can isolate the host and can pull artifacts for a look. Isolation cuts the host off the network while leaving it running. That is containment. Removing the malware, after the evidence is preserved, is eradication, and it is a later step.\n\nThe agent records process starts, parent processes, file writes, and network connections the host itself made. A burst of file encryption across many user files is a behavior the agent can flag even when no signature has a name for that exact program. The response actions are deliberate. Isolate means the host can no longer talk to peers, or can talk only on a narrow logged path. Pulling the process list, the file hash, and a memory image is collection, not cleanup. A person decides which action to take. An automatic isolate can be allowed for a narrow, tested behavior, and it should still open a ticket.\n\nRenee's laptop at the clinic starts encrypting files in a shared project folder just after 16:40. The agent isolates the laptop and opens an alert. Jordan pulls the process list and sees a process name the laptop has not run before, plus the SHA-256 of that program. The laptop stays powered and off the staff network. Jordan writes the host name, the process, the hash, and the time into the ticket, and labels the action containment. Nobody reimages the laptop in the same hour. The memory and the process list are still needed.\n\nA defender checks that isolation actually blocks the laptop from reaching file servers, and that the analyst can still collect artifacts through the allowed path. Test that path before a real case. If the agent is missing on a host, say so, because you cannot watch behavior you do not have an agent for. Some devices cannot run the agent. Those are checked another way, and that gap should be written down. Do not call a flow record a substitute for the process list. The flow can show bytes. The agent shows the process.\n\nStudents call this a SIEM or a flow summary. A SIEM correlates logs from many sources and a person judges the alert. It does not, by itself, sit on the laptop and cut that laptop off. A flow summary has no process name and no file hash. The separating fact is host behavior plus the ability to isolate and to pull artifacts. Isolation is containment. Deleting the program and patching the hole, after the evidence is kept, is eradication.",
        'r0': "An agent on the host records process starts, parent processes, file writes, and the connections that host made. When Renee's laptop starts encrypting a shared folder at 16:40, the agent isolates the laptop and Jordan pulls the process list plus the SHA-256 of the new program. Isolation is containment: the laptop stays powered and off the staff network. Reimaging in that same hour would throw away memory. Eradication, deleting the program and patching the hole, waits until the evidence is preserved. Test the analyst path before a real case. Hosts that cannot run the agent are a written gap. A flow record still cannot name the process.",
        'r1': 'Watch what programs do on the computer itself.\nA network summary cannot name the program.\nThe watcher can cut the computer off the network.\nLeave the power on when you cut it off.\nThat cut is a pause, not the final cleanup.\nPull the list of programs that were running.\nPull the identity of a file you suspect.\nA person should decide the next action.\nTest the cut before a real case.\nWrite down computers that have no watcher.\nA central pile of logs is a different tool.\nA talk summary has no program name.',
        'r2': 'A floor guard watches what people do in the rooms, not merely who passed in the street. When someone starts locking every cabinet in a rush, the guard can seal that floor and leave the lights on. The guard writes the names of the tools in the room before anyone hauls them away. Sealing the floor is a pause so the harm stops. Hauling the tools out and fixing the broken latch comes later. A book that only joins notes from many buildings cannot see the tool in the hand. That guard on the computer, able to seal it and still collect what was running, is endpoint detection and response.',
        'r4': 'Confirm the agent is installed on the host you care about. If it is missing, write that gap down and do not pretend a flow record replaces it. When an isolate fires, check that the host cannot reach file servers and that it is still powered. Pull the process list and the file hash into the ticket. Do not reimage yet. Do not delete the program yet. Label the action containment. Leave eradication until the evidence is preserved. Test the collection path on a lab host before you trust it in a real case.',
        'exam': 'The agent watches host behavior and can isolate the host or pull artifacts such as a process list and a file hash. Isolation, with the power left on, is containment. Eradication is later: delete the malware and patch the hole after evidence is preserved. Reimaging in the same hour destroys memory. Do not call this a SIEM. A SIEM correlates many sources and does not cut one laptop off. Do not call a flow summary this control. A flow has bytes and ports, not the process name. Hosts with no agent are a written gap.',
    },
    'Firewall rules and screened subnets': {
        'body': 'Anything you have not explicitly allowed should be denied. That is the stance of a firewall rule set worth trusting: default deny, then a short list of allows. A screened subnet is the buffer between the internet and the inside network, for systems that must be reachable from outside and must not sit beside the data you most want to protect. The public website belongs in that buffer. The database belongs inside. The firewall between them allows only the application path the website actually needs.\n\nRules match source, destination, port, and protocol, and they are read in order. A later allow does not repair a messy earlier allow that was too wide. From the internet, TCP port 443 may reach the website in the screened subnet, and nothing else on that host should be open. From the website to the database inside, the firewall allows only the database port, for this clinic TCP port 1433, and only from the website address. The internet must not reach 1433 at all. Management access does not belong on the public side. Put that access on a path that is itself restricted.\n\nHelen builds the clinic public site this way. The website sits in the screened subnet. The patient database stays on the inside network. The rule from the internet allows TCP port 443 to the website and denies the rest. The rule from the website to the database allows TCP port 1433 from that one address and denies every other source. Helen writes both rules, with the reason, into the change ticket. A test from the internet confirms that 443 answers and that 1433 does not. A test from the website confirms that the database port answers.\n\nA defender reviews rules that allow anyone to anywhere, rules that allow a port from the whole internet to an inside host, and rules nobody can explain. Those get removed or narrowed after the review, not in a panic if you can help it. The screened subnet is not a place to park the database because the website needs the files close. Need is met by a narrow allow, not by moving the protected data into the buffer. Log the denies. A sudden rise in denies against 1433 from the internet is a ticket, because that port should never have been reachable.\n\nStudents treat a firewall rule like an intrusion signature, or they treat the screened subnet like the inside network. A signature matches a known bad pattern in content. A firewall rule matches who may talk to which port, and the safe stance is default deny. Web filtering is a later policy about which sites a browser may open, and it is not this rule set. The separating fact is the buffer plus default deny: the public site may be reached, and the database stays inside behind an allow that names one port and one source.',
        'r0': 'Default deny, then allow only what you can explain. Place the public website in a screened subnet and keep the database on the inside network. From the internet, allow TCP port 443 to that website and nothing else. From the website address only, allow TCP port 1433 to the database, and deny every other source including the internet. Test from outside that 443 answers and 1433 does not. Test from the website that 1433 answers. Write both rules and the reason in the change ticket. A rise in denies against 1433 from the internet is a ticket, because that port should never be reachable from outside. Do not park the database in the buffer to make the path feel shorter.',
        'r1': 'Deny anything you did not choose to allow.\nKeep the allow list short.\nPut public systems in a buffer room.\nDo not put the private data in that room.\nLet the public site be reached.\nLet only the needed door reach the private data.\nDo not open every door from the outside.\nWrite the reason next to each allow.\nTest from outside that the private door stays shut.\nTest that the public door still opens.\nA known attack pattern is a different control.\nA rule about which sites people may browse is different.',
        'r2': 'A clinic builds a front lobby for visitors and keeps the records room behind a second door. The lobby may be entered from the street. The records room may be entered only from the lobby, and only through the one door the desk needs. Every other door is locked unless a written line allows it. Visitors are not invited to sit in the records room because the desk wants the files close. A wanted poster in the lobby is a different control. A rule about which newspapers visitors may read is different too. That lobby is a screened subnet, and the locked doors are firewall rules with a default of deny.',
        'r4': 'Read the rules in order and write down every allow from the internet. Confirm the public site is in the buffer and the database is inside. Do not move the database. Check that only the web port is open from the internet, and that the database port is allowed only from the website address. Test both paths before you call the change done. Do not delete unexplained rules in the middle of an outage. Log denies, and note any deny aimed at the database port from outside.',
        'exam': 'Firewall rules should default to deny. A screened subnet is a buffer for systems that must be reached from the internet, not the inside network where the database lives. Allow the web port to the public site, and allow the database port only from that site, not from the internet. Do not treat a rule as an intrusion signature. A signature matches a known bad pattern. Do not treat it as web filtering. An allow from anyone to anywhere is the mistake. Need is a narrow allow, not a database parked in the buffer.',
    },
    'IDS and IPS signatures': {
        'body': "Known attacks leave known traces. A signature is a pattern written to match one of those traces in traffic or in a file. An intrusion detection system raises an alert when a signature matches. An intrusion prevention system sits inline and can drop the matching traffic as well as alert. Both depend on the pattern being current. A trace they have never been given will not match. Updates are part of the control, not a courtesy. One hit matters less than a sudden rise in the same signature. IDS and IPS signatures are those patterns, read as a trend and kept current.\n\nYou apply a signature pack only after a test, because a bad pack can alert on normal clinic traffic or, on an inline system, can drop that traffic. The test compares the new pack with the old one on a sample of yesterday's traffic or in a lab. After the pack is live, you watch counts. A signature that matched a handful of times a day and then matches hundreds is a ticket, even if each single hit might be explained. Trends are part of the reading. A signature can also be wrong for your network. That is a false positive, and you tune it with a note rather than deleting the whole pack.\n\nLuis runs the clinic sensors, which use Suricata. He applies a new signature pack on Tuesday after the lab replay stays quiet on the clinic backup traffic. On Wednesday one signature that had matched three times on Monday matches four hundred times before noon. Luis opens a ticket with the signature identifier, the count, the top source addresses, and the time the rise began. He does not call four hundred hits four hundred intrusions. He does not disable the whole pack to make the chart pretty. The rise itself is the thing to explain.\n\nA defender separates the alert from the block. On a detection system, a match is a ticket. On a prevention system, a match can also drop real care traffic if the signature is too broad, so those signatures are promoted to drop only after the test. Keep the pack current. A pack from last quarter will miss a pattern published since. Record the version you are running. Do not treat a quiet week as health if updates have been failing. Misses of unseen attacks are expected. That is why this control is not the only one you run.\n\nStudents treat a signature as a firewall rule or as a web filter, or they think one hit proves an intrusion. A firewall rule allows or denies by address and port, usually with default deny, and it does not describe an attack trace. A web filter applies a browsing policy to sites. Anomaly detection is the neighbor that flags a break from normal without a named pattern. The separating fact is the known pattern, kept current, read as a trend: detection alerts, prevention can drop, and what the pack has never seen will not match.",
        'r0': 'Signatures match known bad patterns and miss what they have never been given. Suricata at the clinic gets a new pack only after a lab replay stays quiet on backup traffic. Detection alerts. Prevention sits inline and can drop, so a drop rule is enabled only after that test. On Wednesday a signature that matched three times on Monday matches four hundred times before noon. Open a ticket with the signature identifier, the count, the top sources, and when the rise began. Do not call each hit a separate proven intrusion, and do not disable the whole pack to flatten the chart. A false positive is tuned with a note. Record the pack version. A quiet week while updates fail is not health.',
        'r1': 'Match traffic against patterns you already know.\nA new attack with no pattern will be missed.\nKeep the patterns up to date.\nTest a new set before you trust it.\nOne beep is weaker than a sudden rise.\nA rise in one pattern should become a ticket.\nA system that only warns does not stop the traffic.\nA system in the path can also drop it.\nDo not drop real work with an untested pattern.\nA door rule about who may enter is different.\nA browsing policy is different.\nOdd behavior with no named pattern is different.',
        'r2': 'The lobby keeps wanted posters of known thieves. A guard who only shouts when a poster matches is the warning kind. A guard who also stops the person is the blocking kind. A brand new thief with no poster walks through. Posters must be replaced when new faces are published, and you try the new set before you let a guard start grabbing people. One match is less interesting than a poster that suddenly matches a crowd. A door list of who may enter is not a poster. Shouting or stopping from a known poster is signature detection and prevention. What has never been drawn on a poster will not be matched.',
        'r4': 'Write down the signature pack version and the date it was applied. Confirm it was tested before any inline drop was enabled. Pick the signature whose count jumped and record the old count, the new count, and the top sources. Do not disable the whole pack. Do not treat each hit as a proven intrusion. Note whether this sensor only alerts or can also drop. If you tune a false positive, write the reason and do not delete the pack. Check that updates are succeeding. Leave firewall allows and site categories in their own reviews.',
        'exam': 'Signatures match known patterns. They miss attacks they have never seen, so updates are part of the control. Detection alerts. Prevention can drop, and a drop belongs after a test. A sudden rise in one signature is a ticket. One hit is not proof of an intrusion. Do not confuse a signature with a firewall rule, which allows or denies by address and port. Do not confuse it with web filtering, which applies a browsing policy. Anomaly detection has no named pattern. A false positive is tuned with a note, not by deleting the pack.',
    },
    'Web filtering': {
        'body': 'Browsers can reach almost anything once a firewall has allowed the web ports. Web filtering is the policy that still says which sites are allowed or blocked, whether that policy sits on the device or on a central proxy. The firewall may permit TCP port 443 to the internet. The filter then allows a clinical site and blocks a category the clinic has forbidden, such as malware sites. A block page should be clear enough that a nurse knows it was policy, not a broken network.\n\nOn a proxy, the browser is pointed at the proxy, or traffic is steered there, and the proxy applies categories and custom allows. On a device, an agent applies the same policy even when the laptop is off the clinic network. Both have to match, or staff learn the path that is weaker. A bypass is drift: a laptop set to skip the proxy, a local change, or a second network path that never sees the filter. Drift is not a personal exception. It is a hole in the policy. The allowed categories and the blocked ones should be written down, with an owner, so a block is not a surprise argument at the desk.\n\nThe clinic blocks a malware category at the proxy. A nurse laptop still reaches a site in that category because the browser was set to connect directly and to ignore the proxy. The proxy log is quiet for that laptop, and the firewall log shows the laptop address going straight out on TCP port 443. Samir treats that as drift, not as a new allow. He puts the laptop back on the proxy, confirms the category block returns, and writes the host name and the bypass into the ticket. He does not add the site to a global allow to make one laptop happy.\n\nA defender samples a few laptops and compares their effective policy with the proxy policy. Differences are tickets. Test a site that must work and a site in a blocked category, from both a clinic desktop and a laptop that sometimes leaves. Do not rely on a filter that staff can turn off without a trace. Log the blocks. A sudden stop in block logs can mean the proxy is being skipped, which is worse than a noisy block. Filtering is not encryption of the web session, and it is not a signature of an exploit inside an allowed site.\n\nStudents think the firewall already did this job, or they think an intrusion signature is the same control. A firewall rule that allows TCP port 443 does not know the category of the site. A signature looks for an attack pattern, not for a browsing policy. The separating fact is the policy on destinations: allow or block by category or by name, enforced on the proxy or on the device, with a bypass treated as drift rather than as permission.',
        'r0': 'Allow TCP port 443 at the firewall and still apply a site policy. The clinic proxy blocks a malware category and allows clinical sites. The same policy should sit on the device for laptops that leave the network. A nurse laptop that ignores the proxy and goes straight out on TCP port 443 is drift, visible because the proxy log is quiet while the firewall log shows the laptop. Put the laptop back on the proxy and confirm the category block returns. Do not add the site to a global allow to quiet one user. Test one site that must work and one in a blocked category. A sudden stop in block logs can mean the proxy is being skipped. Name an owner for the category list.',
        'r1': 'Decide which sites browsers may open.\nThe decision can sit on the device.\nThe decision can sit on a central checker.\nA door that allows the web is not this decision.\nBlock a forbidden group of sites.\nAllow the sites the clinic must use.\nA laptop that skips the checker is drift.\nPut that laptop back on the checker.\nDo not bless the bad site to save one laptop.\nTest a site that must work.\nTest a site that must fail.\nWrite down who owns the allow and the block.\nA quiet block log can mean the checker is being skipped.',
        'r2': 'A library lets people into the reading room and still refuses some shelves. The refusal can be a librarian at the door or a rule on each desk, and both should refuse the same shelves. A reader who props a side door and walks past the librarian is not a special permission. Staff put that reader back through the door and do not relabel the bad shelf as allowed. The street door being unlocked is not the shelf rule. A poster of a known thief is not the shelf rule either. The shelf rule is web filtering. It allows or blocks destinations by policy, on the device or at the central door.',
        'r4': 'Write down where the policy is enforced, on the proxy or on the device. List the blocked categories and the owner. Test a site that must open and a site in a blocked category. Find any laptop whose browser skips the proxy. Do not add a global allow for that one laptop. Put it back on the policy and confirm the block returns. Compare a sample of laptops with the central policy and ticket the differences. Watch for a stop in block logs. Do not treat a firewall allow of web traffic as this policy.',
        'exam': 'Web filtering allows or blocks sites by policy, on the device or on a central proxy. A firewall allow of TCP port 443 does not know the category. An intrusion signature looks for an attack pattern, not for a browsing policy. A laptop that skips the proxy is drift, not permission. The proxy log going quiet while the laptop still reaches the site is the clue. Device policy and proxy policy have to match. Filtering is not encryption of the session, and a stop in block logs can mean the control is being bypassed.',
    },
    'URL scanning and categorization': {
        'body': "A category list is a set of buckets, such as news, shopping, or malware, and a policy can allow or block a bucket. Categorization is how a site gets into a bucket. URL scanning checks that link at the moment someone is about to open it, or at the moment a message arrives. The two work together and they are not the same age. A category list can be hours or days old. A scan can look at a link that was created this morning and is not in any bucket yet.\n\nScanning may fetch the link, or score it from features such as a brand-new name, a misleading path, or a page that asks for a password. The result can be block, warn, or allow, and it should be kept only for a short time so a changed page is not trusted all week. Categorization is broader and slower. A vendor or a local admin sorts known sites into buckets, and the web filter applies the policy to the bucket. A site that was news yesterday and is malware today will be wrong until the bucket is updated or a scan overrides it. That lag is why a fresh phishing link needs the scan even when the category list is well kept.\n\nA nurse receives mail that looks like the clinic bank and contains a link created overnight. Yesterday's category list has no bucket for that name, because the name did not exist. The gateway scans the link, sees a new name and a page asking for a password, and blocks it. The category list is unchanged. The block is from the scan. Chen writes the sender, the link domain, the scan result, and the time into the ticket. He does not add the domain to a permanent malware bucket until the scan result is reviewed, and he does not tell the nurse to open it just to check.\n\nA defender tests with a link that should be blocked and a link the clinic uses every day. Confirm the daily link still opens. Confirm the new bad link is blocked even when it is absent from the category list. Watch for a scanner that fails open and allows every link when the scan service is down. That failure should alert, and the policy for that outage should be written before the outage. Do not treat a green category as a promise about a page that changed an hour ago. Run the scan again when the risk is a live link in mail.\n\nStudents treat the category bucket as a reputation list, or they treat either control as Group Policy. A reputation list refuses addresses or domains that many others have already marked bad. It will miss a name nobody has reported yet. Group Policy pushes Windows settings from a central place, and it does not fetch a link. The separating fact is time: categorization sorts known sites into buckets such as news or malware, and scanning checks this link now, which is how a phishing link too new for yesterday's list still gets blocked.",
        'r0': "Categorization sorts a site into a bucket such as news or malware so a policy can allow or block the bucket. URL scanning checks the link at arrival. A phishing link created overnight has no bucket yet. The gateway still blocks it because the scan sees a new name and a page that asks for a password. The category list stays unchanged. That block is the scan, not yesterday's buckets. Keep a scan result only briefly so a changed page is not trusted all week. Test a daily clinic link, which must open, and a fresh bad link, which must block even though it is absent from the category list. If the scanner fails open when the service is down, alert on that failure. Do not tell the nurse to open the link just to check.",
        'r1': "Sort known sites into buckets such as news or malware.\nA policy can allow or block a whole bucket.\nThat sort can be a day old.\nAlso check the link at the moment it arrives.\nA brand new link may have no bucket yet.\nThe live check can still block it.\nA fresh trick link is the case to test.\nA normal daily link should still open.\nDo not trust yesterday's bucket alone.\nIf the live check is down, have a written plan.\nA list of already bad names is a different list.\nCentral settings for a desktop are a different tool.",
        'r2': "A diner prints a menu that sorts dishes into soup, bread, and dishes the house will not serve. The menu was printed yesterday. Today a new bowl arrives that is not on the menu. The cook tastes that bowl before it is served, and can refuse it even though yesterday's menu is silent. A regular bowl the house serves every day should still pass the taste. A list of suppliers already thrown out of the market is a different list, and a posted work rule for the kitchen staff is a different paper. That taste of today's bowl is a live check of the link. The printed groups are the buckets a policy can allow or block.",
        'r4': "Pick one mail link that is too new for yesterday's category list. Record the domain, the scan result, and whether it was blocked. Do not open it from a nurse mailbox. Confirm a daily clinic link still opens. Write down how old the category list is. Do not add the new domain to a permanent malware bucket until the scan is reviewed. Find out what the gateway does if the scan service is down, and write that failure mode. Do not treat a green category as a promise if the page changed an hour ago.",
        'exam': "Categorization puts known sites in buckets such as news or malware. Scanning checks this link now, so a phishing link too new for yesterday's list can still be blocked. A green category is not a promise. Do not call the bucket a reputation list. That list refuses names many others have marked bad, and it misses a name nobody has reported. Do not call either control Group Policy. Group Policy pushes Windows settings and does not fetch a link. A scanner that fails open when the service is down should alert, not allow every link.",
    },
    'Reputation block lists': {
        'body': "A reputation block list is a shared record of addresses or domain names that other operators have already tied to spam, phishing, or malware. A mail gateway can consult that record and refuse the sender before a nurse ever sees an inbox. That refusal is a historical judgment: the identifier already has a bad reputation among many sites. The list does not read the letter for a secret, and it does not sort websites into buckets such as news or shopping.\n\nThe gateway takes the sending address, or the domain that handed the message over, and asks whether the list already names it. A hit supports a permanent reject during the mail conversation on port 25, so the body is never stored for the recipient. Operators add a name after they see abuse, and they drop a name that has gone quiet and been repaired. The feed has to stay current, because a list from last month misses a domain registered this morning and may still blame a shared mail host for one bad customer. A refusal should name the list that fired. An exception needs an owner and an end date, or the hole becomes permanent.\n\nElena reviews the clinic gateway on a Tuesday morning. A message claims to come from a supplier the purchasing desk really uses. The sending domain sits on the reputation list the gateway is set to honor. The log at 08:14 records a reject, the list name, and that domain. The nurse never sees a subject line. Real mail from the same supplier, sent from a different address that is not listed, still arrives an hour later. The evidence of the control is that reject line. The evidence of a gap would be a delivered message with no list check recorded at all.\n\nDefenders keep the feed updating and read a sample of rejects each day. They write a short exception when a real partner is listed by mistake, and they put a date on it. They do not switch the whole list off because one good sender was caught. A clean result is also not proof the message is safe. A brand-new phishing domain has no reputation yet, so content checks and domain authentication still have work to do.\n\nStudents mix this list up with two neighbors. URL scanning inspects a link at the moment someone would open it, which can stop a site too new for yesterday's list. Categorization places a site in a bucket so a browsing policy can allow news and refuse malware. A reputation list only refuses identifiers others have already marked as bad. Group Policy is a third neighbor and a different machine entirely. It pushes Windows settings such as a screen lock. It does not judge a mail domain.",
        'r0': 'A reputation block list is consulted during the SMTP conversation on port 25, and a hit should end in a permanent reject before the message body is stored. Match the connecting address or the sending domain, and write the list name into the log next to the timestamp. Keep the feed on a short update cycle so a domain registered this morning is not invisible for a week. Treat a shared mail host as a special case: one bad customer can list an address that also sends real mail. An exception needs a named owner and an end date. Sample rejects daily so a legitimate supplier is not stuck unseen. Leave content inspection and domain authentication in place for senders the list has never met. A brand-new phishing name is the gap this control does not close by itself.',
        'r1': 'A shared list names senders others already saw as bad. Mail can be refused before anyone opens it. The list is built from reports of abuse. A brand new site may not be listed yet. Update the feed or the names go stale. One bad customer can taint a shared mail host. Read which list rejected the message. Give each exception an owner and an end date. Do not turn the whole list off for one good sender. A miss on the list is not a safe message. Checking a link at click time is different. Sorting sites into buckets is different. Pushing a screen lock to laptops is different.',
        'r2': "A lobby guard keeps a notebook of visitors that other buildings have already turned away. The guard checks the name at the door and does not wait for a long interview when the notebook already says no. A stranger who has never visited any building is absent from the notebook, so the bag still gets a look. One bad visit can put a whole company on the page, and a manager may write a short pass with a date on it. If nobody adds new names, last month's thief walks in during a quiet week. The notebook is the reputation list, and the door is the mail gateway. The bag check is the live inspection a list does not perform.",
        'r4': "Open yesterday's reject log on the mail gateway. Write down the sender, the list name, and the time. Confirm the list feed has updated since yesterday. Do not disable the list yet. Sample one refusal and decide whether that sender is legitimate. If you allow an exception, write the owner and the end date on the same line. Leave content checks and domain checks running. Note whether the refused message never reached an inbox.",
        'exam': 'A reputation block list refuses a name or address that others have already seen as bad. It is not URL scanning, which checks a link at the moment of use and can catch a site too new for the list. It is not categorization, which sorts sites into buckets such as news or malware. It is not Group Policy, which writes Windows settings. A clean list result does not mean the message is safe. Turning the list off is not the fix for one false reject. A dated exception is the fix.',
    },
    'Group Policy': {
        'body': 'Windows can push configuration from a central store so staff laptops pick up the same screen lock, the same host firewall setting, and the same restriction on removable storage. That push is Group Policy. A local edit on one laptop is not the final word. The central result arrives again at the next refresh, which is why a quiet local change does not drift forever.\n\nWindows applies the settings in a fixed order: local policy on the computer, then the site, then the domain, then the organizational unit. When two policies write the same setting, the one applied later replaces the earlier value. The last writer wins. The exception is a policy marked enforced. An enforced setting is not replaced by a later writer, so a domain policy that is enforced still beats an organizational unit that tries to loosen it. Computers refresh in the background about every ninety minutes, plus a random offset of up to thirty minutes, and they also apply policy at startup and sign-in. Until that refresh, a local edit can look successful on the screen even though it will not last.\n\nMaya wants a chart to stay up, so she sets the local screen lock to sixty minutes. The domain policy sets five minutes and is marked enforced. At the next refresh the lock returns to five minutes because the domain policy is applied after the local policy. The applied result names the domain policy as the winning source. A child organizational unit that asks for sixty minutes still loses, because the domain policy is enforced and a later writer does not override it. What an administrator reads is the winning source, not the slider Maya just moved.\n\nDefenders read the applied result before they argue with the console that edits the policy. They mark the settings that must not be loosened, such as the screen lock and the host firewall, as enforced. They leave ordinary preferences free for an organizational unit to specialize. They do not stop the refresh service to make a local fix stick. A laptop whose applied result does not match the domain is drift, and the note should say which policy won.\n\nStudents remember only the last half of the order and insist the closest organizational unit always wins. That is true only when no earlier policy is enforced. An enforced domain setting beats a later, looser one. Students also confuse this push with a reputation block list. A block list refuses a mail name that already has a bad history. The Windows push writes settings onto machines. It does not accept or reject a message.',
        'r0': 'Apply Windows settings in order: local, site, domain, then organizational unit. The later writer replaces the earlier value on the same setting unless the earlier policy is marked enforced. An enforced domain screen lock of five minutes beats both a local sixty-minute edit and a looser organizational unit. Background refresh is about ninety minutes plus a random offset of up to thirty minutes, and policy also applies at startup and sign-in. Read the applied result and record which policy won. Do not stop the refresh service to preserve a local edit. Treat a mismatch between the laptop and the domain result as drift. Leave removable-storage and host-firewall settings enforced when the standard requires them.',
        'r1': 'Central Windows settings land on each laptop. Local policy is applied first. Site policy comes next, then the domain. The organizational unit comes after the domain. A later writer replaces an earlier value. An enforced setting is not replaced later. A local screen lock can look changed until refresh. Refresh returns on a regular background cycle. Also apply policy at startup and at sign-in. Read which policy won before you argue. Do not stop refresh to save a local edit. A mail block list is a different control. A block list does not set the screen lock.',
        'r2': 'A building owner posts house rules in a set order: the apartment door, the floor, the whole building, then the wing. A later notice replaces an earlier one on the same subject, such as how fast the hallway lights must dim. A notice stamped so it cannot be overridden still stands after a wing tries to dim the lights later. Residents who tape a private note to their own door see that note until the next inspection, and then the building notice is back. The inspector writes down which paper won. The ordered notices are Windows policy layers, and the stamp is the enforced flag. The inspection is the refresh that puts the winning setting back.',
        'r4': 'Open the applied result on the laptop, not only the local editor. Write down the winning source for the screen lock. Write down whether that source is marked enforced. Note the last time policy refreshed. Do not disable the refresh service yet. Do not loosen the domain setting to match a local edit. Compare a child organizational unit only after the enforced flag is recorded. Leave the host firewall setting as it is until the note is done.',
        'exam': 'Windows applies Group Policy in order: local, site, domain, then organizational unit. The last writer of a setting wins unless an earlier policy is enforced. An enforced domain setting beats a later organizational unit that tries to loosen it. The closest unit does not always win. A local slider can look successful until the next refresh. A reputation block list refuses mail names and does not push a screen lock. URL categorization sorts websites and does not write Windows settings either.',
    },
    'SELinux': {
        'body': 'SELinux is mandatory access control on a Linux host. The kernel allows or denies a process action by comparing labels, and the person who owns the file cannot simply give that decision away. This is not a firewall. A firewall filters packets by address and port. A label decision can still deny a process that is already running, after the packet was accepted and the service is handling the request.\n\nEach process runs in a domain, and each file carries a type. Policy says which domain may read, write, or execute which type. If the web service is confined to its own domain, a flaw in that service does not become permission to read the payroll files. Ordinary Unix mode bits may allow the read. The process may even be tricked into asking. The label still refuses. Two modes matter in operations. Enforcing mode applies the denial. Permissive mode records the denial and then allows the action, which is useful on a test host and useless as protection. A production server left in permissive mode is logging a control it is not actually using.\n\nNoah investigates the clinic web server. Payroll files sit on the same machine for an old, bad reason. The web service is confined, and enforcing mode is on. An attacker uses a flaw in the service and tries to read payroll. The audit log records a denial that names the web service domain and the payroll file. The file contents are not returned. Ordinary pages on the site can still answer. The line an analyst keeps is that denial, with the process and the file both named, timestamped while the service stayed up.\n\nDefenders leave enforcing mode on once the policy is tuned for the real workload. They read a denial before they flip the host to permissive just to quiet an error. A denial is either a policy that is too tight for a legitimate change, or a process that tried something it should never do. They do not make the payroll file readable by everyone to silence the log. They also do not confuse a closed port with a label. Opening the service port on the firewall does not grant the web domain the right to read payroll.\n\nStudents call this a firewall because both controls can stop something. The packet filter never sees the payroll read that happens inside the host after the request is already in. Students also mix the labels up with secure protocol selection, which chooses a protected port and turns a clear-text listener off. Labels do not choose SSH instead of a clear file transfer. They also mix it up with DNS filtering, which refuses a name at the resolver. A resolver decision is not a process domain on one Linux host.',
        'r0': 'SELinux is mandatory access control on Linux, enforced by labels on processes and files, not by packet addresses. Keep production in enforcing mode so a denial is actually applied. Permissive mode logs the same denial and then allows it, which is a test mode and not a control. A confined web service domain should be unable to read payroll even when Unix mode bits allow the read. The audit line should name the domain and the file. Do not clear a denial by making the file world readable. A host firewall on the service port is a separate control and can be open while the label still denies the file. AppArmor is another mandatory system on some Linux hosts and is not this policy language.',
        'r1': 'Labels decide what a program may touch. The owner cannot simply give the label away. This is mandatory control on Linux. A firewall filters packets instead. A packet can be allowed and the file still denied. A confined web service should not read payroll. Enforcing mode applies the denial. Permissive mode only writes it down. Do not leave production in permissive mode. Read the denial before you change mode. Do not open the file to everyone to quiet the log. Choosing a protected protocol is a different job. Refusing a bad name is a different job.',
        'r2': 'A hospital kitchen gives each cook a station and a marked set of cupboards. The pastry cook may open the sugar tins and may not open the drug safe, even after someone tricks that cook into asking. A note from the cook does not retitle the safe. During a drill the supervisor can write the refusal in a book and then allow the door, which teaches nothing if the drill never ends. On a real shift the refusal stands and the safe stays shut. The stations and the marked cupboards are label rules on a Linux host. The street door that keeps strangers out is the firewall, a different lock entirely.',
        'r4': 'Read the audit denial and write down the process domain and the file. Write down whether the host is in enforcing mode or permissive mode. Do not switch the host to permissive yet. Do not change the file mode to silence the line. Check whether the same read is legitimate new work or an unexpected path. Leave the host firewall rules unchanged while you read the denial. Note the time and whether the service stayed up. Only then decide whether the policy needs a narrow allow.',
        'exam': 'SELinux is mandatory access control on Linux. It is not a firewall, and a firewall allow does not grant a confined process the right to read a labeled file. Permissive mode logs a denial and still allows the action. Enforcing mode applies the denial. Unix mode bits are discretionary and can allow a read the label still refuses. Secure protocol selection chooses a protected listener and turns the clear one off. DNS filtering refuses names at a resolver. Neither one is a process label on a single host.',
    },
    'Secure protocol selection': {
        'body': 'Secure protocol selection means using the protected form of a service, on the port that form actually uses, and turning the clear-text form off so it is not left listening. The protected form encrypts the session. A proper setup also lets the client check that it reached the real server. The clear form sends the same kind of work where anyone on the path can read names, passwords, and file contents.\n\nAdmin file transfer shows the choice cleanly. The protected path is SSH on port 22, which can carry a shell and a file copy. The old clear-text transfer service listens on port 21 and sends the session in the open. Telnet on port 23 is the clear-text shell. Selection is not finished when port 22 is turned on. It is finished when a scan shows 21 and 23 closed. Directory lookups have the same shape. LDAP, the directory protocol, uses port 389 in the clear and port 636 when the session is wrapped in TLS. Leaving 389 open to a wide network keeps every lookup readable. A firewall rule that hides 21 from the internet is incomplete if the clear service still answers for anyone already inside.\n\nPriya builds file transfer for clinic administrators. SSH answers on port 22. A probe of port 21 finds no listener. A probe of port 23 finds no listener. An administrator copies a configuration file inside the SSH session and checks the server key that was recorded earlier. The successful evidence is the listener on 22 and silence on 21. The failure evidence would be a banner on 21 that still offers a login. A forgotten script that still calls port 21 is the same class of miss, even when humans have switched.\n\nDefenders inventory listeners after every build. They write down the protected port they expect and remove the package that opens the clear port. They do not accept a sentence that says the protected client is in use while the clear service still answers. They test again after the next image, because a golden image can put Telnet back. They also confirm the directory clients use 636, and they do not leave 389 exposed as a convenience.\n\nStudents mix this habit up with SELinux. Labels confine a process after a connection exists. They do not choose port 22 over port 21, and they do not turn a listener off. Students also mix it up with DNS filtering. Filtering refuses to resolve a name the clinic does not want reached. It does not encrypt an admin file copy, and it does not close a clear-text port. Choosing a protected protocol and actually disabling the clear one are the whole task.',
        'r0': 'Use SSH on port 22 for admin shells and file copy, and leave port 21 and port 23 with no listener. LDAP, the directory protocol, is clear on 389 and protected with TLS on 636. Do not treat a wide-open 389 as acceptable once 636 works. Confirm with a listener scan after every image, not only with a statement that the protected client is installed. A firewall deny from the internet does not finish the job if the clear port still answers inside. Record the expected server key for SSH and investigate a change. Retire scripts that still call the clear port. Selection fails if the old service is merely unused but still listening.',
        'r1': 'Pick the protected form of the service. Turn the clear form off. A protected client is not enough. The old listener must be gone. Admin file copy belongs on the protected path. A clear transfer service must not answer. A clear shell must not answer. Directory lookups need the protected form too. Hiding the clear port from the outside is incomplete. Someone inside could still reach it. Labels on a process are a different control. Refusing a bad name is a different control. Scan listeners again after each new image.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse ships valuables in a sealed truck and used to leave an open loading dock for the same crates. Workers can prefer the truck and still forget the dock, so anyone in the yard can read the labels and take a box. The job is done when the dock door is actually shut, not when the truck is parked beside it. A side gate that blocks strangers from the street does not help if staff inside can still walk up to the open dock. A forgotten cart path that still rolls crates to the dock is the same mistake. The sealed truck is the protected protocol. The shut dock is the clear service turned off.',
        'r4': 'Scan the host for listening services and write down each port. Confirm the protected admin path is the one in use. Confirm the clear transfer service has no listener. Confirm the clear shell has no listener. Do not open a temporary clear port yet. Check scripts and scheduled jobs for the old path. Write down any directory client still using the clear lookup. Leave firewall rules unchanged until the listener list is written.',
        'exam': 'Secure protocol selection uses the protected form and turns the clear-text listener off. SSH for admin file transfer is the protected choice. A clear transfer service or a clear shell left listening is a failed selection, even if a firewall hides it from the internet. LDAP is the directory protocol: 389 is clear and 636 is the TLS form. SELinux confines a process by labels and does not choose the port. DNS filtering refuses names and does not encrypt the session. Installing the protected service without closing the clear one is not done.',
    },
    'DNS filtering': {
        'body': 'DNS filtering refuses to resolve names the organization does not want people or programs to reach. The clinic resolver makes that decision, not a category list inside one browser. If the name is refused, the workstation often never opens a connection to the bad server, so a beacon fails before it can speak. The control is a deliberate non-answer, or a useless answer, for a name on a policy list.\n\nA client asks the clinic resolver for an address. The resolver compares the name with a list of malware domains or with a category the clinic has chosen to refuse. On a match, it does not return the real address. It may answer that the name does not exist, or it may return a local sinkhole address that leads nowhere useful. The later connection fails or hits the sinkhole, and the resolver log shows the client, the name, and the block. Filtering works only for clients that use that resolver. A laptop pointed at a public resolver such as 8.8.8.8, or a program that talks straight to an outside resolver on port 53, walks around the filter. DNSSEC is a different control. It checks that a DNS answer is authentic. It does not refuse a name you dislike, and it does not stop a flood of queries.\n\nA clinic workstation tries to resolve a domain already named on the malware list. The resolver log shows the query, the workstation address, and a block at 11:06. No session to the real server is completed, and the beacon fails. The host firewall may show nothing further, because the conversation never started. What the analyst keeps is that blocked query line. A second host that succeeds in resolving the same name is a bypass, not a quiet success, and its configured resolver is the fact to write down.\n\nDefenders send clients to the clinic resolvers and alarm when a host sends queries somewhere else. They keep the refusal list updated and sample blocks so a partner the clinic still needs is not stuck on a stale entry. They do not treat a quiet beacon as proof the host is clean. Malware can wait until it finds a resolver that will answer. They also do not describe this refusal as protection against a flood. A flood is a volume problem. Filtering is a name decision.\n\nStudents call the refusal DNSSEC because both ideas live in DNS. DNSSEC will happily authenticate a correct answer for a bad name. It checks origin and integrity of the answer, not whether the clinic wants that name reached. Students also mix filtering up with secure protocol selection. Choosing a protected file transfer encrypts a session you intend to have. Filtering stops the lookup for a destination you do not want at all. SELinux labels on one host are a third neighbor and do not decide which names resolve.',
        'r0': 'Refuse malware and other disallowed names at the clinic resolver, and log the client address with the name and the time. Return a non-answer or a sinkhole address rather than the real host address. Port 53 queries that go to a public resolver such as 8.8.8.8 bypass the filter, so alarm those clients. DNSSEC checks that an answer is authentic. It does not refuse a bad name, and it does not stop a flood. Sample blocks so a needed partner is not stuck on a stale entry. A failed beacon is evidence the lookup was refused, not evidence the host is clean. Re-check resolver settings after each laptop image.',
        'r1': 'Refuse names you do not want reached. Do the refusal at the clinic resolver. A refused name often means no connection at all. The beacon can fail before it speaks. Log the client, the name, and the time. A laptop can point somewhere else and walk around you. Alarm hosts that ask a different resolver. A stale entry can block a real partner. A quiet beacon does not mean the host is clean. Checking that an answer is real is different. Encrypting a session you want is different. Labels on one host are different.',
        'r2': "A reception desk has a book of room numbers it will not give out. When someone asks for a banned room, the desk says there is no such room, or it points down a hall that ends at a blank wall. Guests who already know a side entrance never ask the desk, so the book does not touch them. A clerk who memorizes yesterday's book will turn away a doctor who belongs here today. The desk writes who asked and which name was refused. The desk is the resolver, and the banned room is the disallowed name. The side entrance is a client that uses some other resolver.",
        'r4': 'Open the resolver log and write down the blocked name, the client, and the time. Confirm that client is set to use the clinic resolver. Do not delete the block yet. Check whether any host asked a public resolver for the same name. Sample one block and decide whether the name is still supposed to be refused. Leave host firewall rules unchanged while you read the log. Note whether a real session to that name ever started. Write the bypass hosts on a separate line.',
        'exam': 'DNS filtering refuses to resolve names the organization does not want reached. DNSSEC checks that a DNS answer is authentic. It does not refuse a bad name, and it does not stop a flood. A flood is not a defense. A client that uses a public resolver bypasses the clinic filter. Secure protocol selection encrypts a session you intend to have and does not refuse a lookup. SELinux labels confine a process on one Linux host and do not decide which names resolve. A failed beacon is not proof the workstation is clean.',
    },
    'SPF, DKIM, and DMARC': {
        'body': 'A domain that sends mail can publish three DNS records so receivers can test messages that claim to come from it. SPF checks the sending IP in DNS. DKIM checks a signature. DMARC says what receivers should do when those checks fail, and it asks for reports. None of the three encrypts the message. A person can still read the body. The checks answer whether the sender was allowed and whether the message was signed, not whether the words are secret.\n\nThe domain publishes an SPF record that lists the addresses allowed to send for it. The receiver compares the IP address of the server that connected with that list. A listed address passes. An address that is not listed fails. The check is about the sending server, not about a cryptographic signature, and it follows the domain used on the mail envelope rather than every visible header. DKIM is the signature. The sender signs selected headers and the body with a private key. The receiver fetches the public key from DNS and checks that signature. If the message was altered, or the key does not verify, DKIM fails. DMARC tells receivers to do nothing special, to quarantine, or to reject when authentication fails for the visible sender domain. The visible domain should align with the domain that passed SPF or DKIM. Reports go back to the domain owner so forgotten senders show up before a strict policy drops real mail.\n\nThe clinic domain publishes a DMARC policy of reject. A lookalike message arrives at the receiving gateway. The sending IP is not in the clinic SPF record, and there is no valid DKIM signature aligned to the clinic domain. The gateway rejects the message because the published policy says reject. The mail log shows a DMARC failure, the reject action, the claimed domain, and the sending address. The nurse does not get the lookalike in the inbox. A billing host the clinic forgot to list would fail the same way, which is why reports come before a reject policy.\n\nDefenders list only servers that truly send, sign outbound mail with DKIM, and read aggregate reports while the policy is still soft. They move to reject only after the reports show their real senders pass. They do not publish a reject policy on a domain whose mail paths they have not inventoried. They keep the private DKIM key off the public web server. A signature without a protected private key is a stamp anyone can forge.\n\nStudents swap the three jobs on exams. SPF does not check a signature. DKIM does not look up whether the sending IP is allowed. DMARC does not replace either check. It states the action when authentication fails, and it requests reports. Students also think the three records are the email gateway. The gateway is a machine that can enforce a decision and inspect a file. The records are what the domain publishes in DNS.',
        'r0': 'SPF checks the sending IP against the addresses a domain publishes in DNS. DKIM checks a signature over selected headers and the body, using a public key also published in DNS. DMARC says whether a receiver should take no special action, quarantine, or reject when those checks fail for the visible sender, and it requests reports. Require alignment between the visible sender domain and the domain that passed SPF or DKIM. Publish reject only after reports show real senders pass. A forgotten billing host will fail a reject policy the same way a lookalike does. None of the three records encrypts the message body. Keep the DKIM private key off systems that do not sign mail.',
        'r1': 'One check asks whether the sending address is allowed. Another check asks whether a signature is valid. A third check says what to do when those fail. The failure rule can be to reject the message. The same rule can ask for reports. Read the reports before you choose reject. A forgotten sender fails the same way a fake does. These checks do not hide the message body. A signature is not the same as secrecy. The sending address check is not a signature. The signature check is not an address list. The mail machine that enforces this is separate.',
        'r2': "A front desk has three tools for packages that claim to come from a partner. One card lists the couriers allowed to deliver for that partner. One wax seal shows the package was closed by someone with the partner's stamp and was not opened on the way. One instruction sheet says to refuse the package, hold it, or accept it when the courier or the seal is wrong, and to send a note about what arrived. The sheet is useless if the desk has never written down the real couriers. The courier card is the check of the sending address. The seal is the signature, and the instruction sheet is the rule for failures.",
        'r4': 'Open the DNS records for the sending domain and write down the allowed addresses. Write down whether outbound mail is signed, and where the private key lives. Write down the published failure action: no special action, quarantine, or reject. Do not change that action to reject yet. Read recent reports for legitimate senders that would fail. Do not put the private key on a public web server. Leave the gateway file checks as they are. Note one lookalike reject only after the records are written down.',
        'exam': 'SPF checks the sending IP in DNS. DKIM checks a signature. DMARC says what receivers should do when those fail, and it asks for reports. SPF is not the signature. DKIM is not the address list. DMARC does not replace either check and does not encrypt the message. A reject policy will drop a forgotten legitimate sender the same way it drops a lookalike. The email gateway may enforce the result and may also strip a file. The gateway is not the DNS record. Reports come before a move to reject.',
    },
    'Email gateways': {
        'body': 'An email gateway filters mail before it reaches the inbox. It looks for spam, malware, and spoofing, and it can reject a message, hold it, strip a file, or tag what remains. The person should see a cleaned message or a clear warning, not the original dangerous attachment sitting under a normal subject line.\n\nMail for the domain has to arrive at the gateway first, or the filter never sees the message. The gateway accepts the message and then runs content checks: the attachment type, a malware inspection, a reputation check, and the domain authentication results. Those authentication results come from SPF, DKIM, and DMARC, which the sending domain publishes in DNS. The gateway can act on them, and it can also do work those records never do, such as removing a macro file. A dangerous attachment can be stripped while the text is delivered with a banner. A message that looks like malware can sit in quarantine until a reviewer decides. If senders can deliver straight to the mailbox server, none of that runs.\n\nA message to a clinic nurse carries a spreadsheet with a macro. The gateway strips that attachment, writes a warning banner at the top, and delivers the rest. The gateway log names the recipient, the sender, the file name, and the strip action at 09:40. The nurse sees the banner and does not see the macro file in the inbox. A reviewer who opens quarantine the same morning finds a second message held entirely, with a malware verdict and no delivery. The two outcomes are different on purpose. Strip-and-tag is for a removable danger. A full hold is for a message that should not be read yet.\n\nDefenders confirm that inbound mail actually arrives at the gateway before any mailbox server. They review quarantine on a schedule and stop users from releasing a malware verdict with one click. They tune false positives so a real supplier is not stuck for days, and they write the reason when a release is allowed. They do not disable the attachment rule because one person is in a hurry. They keep the banner wording plain enough that a nurse can tell a file was removed.\n\nStudents confuse the gateway with the DNS records it consults. SPF checks the sending IP, DKIM checks a signature, and DMARC says what to do when those fail. The gateway may enforce that decision, and it also inspects the file. Students also confuse the gateway with file integrity monitoring, which alerts when a stored fingerprint changes, and with behavior analytics, which flags an unusual volume of reads. Those controls do not strip a macro from a message on the way in.',
        'r0': 'Place the email gateway on the inbound mail path so messages are inspected before any inbox delivery. Strip a dangerous attachment, add a visible warning banner, and log the sender, recipient, file name, and action. Hold a malware verdict in quarantine instead of asking the recipient to release it with one click. Use SPF, DKIM, and DMARC results as one input, then still inspect the file, because those DNS checks do not open a spreadsheet. Review the quarantine queue on a schedule and record any manual release. Confirm no sender can deliver straight to the mailbox server and skip the gateway. A banner without a log line is incomplete. A log line without the banner leaves the nurse uninformed.',
        'r1': 'Check the mail before the person sees it. Look for spam, malware, and fake senders. A dangerous file can be removed. A warning can sit at the top of what remains. Some messages should be held for a reviewer. Do not let the recipient release malware alone. Make sure mail cannot skip this door. A fake sender check is only one input. The file still needs a look. A changed file on a server is a different alert. An odd download volume is a different alert. Write down every manual release.',
        'r2': 'A mail room opens packages before they reach a desk. A clerk pulls out a suspicious device, stamps a note on the letter, and sends the letter on without the device. A package that smells worse is locked in a cage until a supervisor looks, and the addressee does not get a key to that cage. If delivery trucks can roll straight to the desk, the mail room is theater. The clerk writes who it was for and what was removed. The mail room is the gateway, and the stamp is the warning a person can see. The cage is the hold for a message that should not be released by the recipient.',
        'r4': 'Confirm inbound mail arrives at the gateway before any mailbox. Open one stripped message and write down the file name and the banner text. Open the matching log line and write down the time and the action. Do not turn the attachment rule off yet. Check the quarantine queue for malware holds. Do not give users a one-click release. Note whether any path can deliver straight to the mailbox server. Leave DNS records unchanged while you inspect the gateway path.',
        'exam': 'An email gateway filters spam, malware, and spoofing before the inbox. Stripping a dangerous attachment and tagging the message is gateway work. SPF checks the sending IP, DKIM checks a signature, and DMARC says what to do when those fail. Those are DNS checks a gateway may use, not the gateway itself. File integrity monitoring watches a stored fingerprint. Behavior analytics flags a break from a normal pattern. Neither one removes a macro from inbound mail. A user release of a malware hold is not a successful filter.',
    },
    'File integrity monitoring': {
        'body': "File integrity monitoring alerts when an important file no longer matches a known-good fingerprint. The fingerprint is a hash of the bytes. For a file-integrity check, record SHA-256 rather than a retired digest. The alert does not mean the new file is malware. It means the bytes are not the bytes that were recorded when the system was trusted. MD5 and SHA-1 are the wrong choice here because they are broken for collisions, so two different files can be made to share one of those older fingerprints.\n\nA baseline is taken after a clean install or after a reviewed patch. The monitor stores the path, the SHA-256 value, and often the owner and the permissions. On a schedule, or continuously, it hashes the live file and compares. A mismatch opens an alert with the path, the old hash, and the new hash. A legitimate patch changes hashes too, so the alert is a lead that must be matched to a change ticket. No ticket means the change is unexplained. Login programs, service binaries, and the files that schedule work at boot matter more than a temporary folder that changes every hour. The baseline store itself should sit on a different host, or the person who edits the binary can edit the fingerprint to match.\n\nOvernight, the hash of a system binary on the clinic server changes. The monitor fires at 04:12, before the morning shift. The alert names the path and shows a SHA-256 value that does not match the baseline taken after last month's patch. No change ticket exists for that host. The file is still on disk. The monitor did not delete it, and it did not decide a virus name. The morning analyst has the two hashes, the path, and the time. That is the whole finding until someone compares it with approved work.\n\nDefenders rebuild the baseline after an approved change and not before the change is understood. They investigate an unmatched hash before they restore a copy, and they keep enough of the new file to know what arrived. They do not turn the monitor off to clear a queue. They exclude noisy temporary paths on purpose, in writing, so real alerts are not buried. They refuse a design that leaves the only fingerprint on the same disk as the file being watched.\n\nStudents mix this alert up with a malware signature scan. A signature looks for a known bad pattern and can miss a changed file it has never seen. A fingerprint fires on any change, including a bad patch and a good one. Students also mix it up with an email gateway, which strips a dangerous attachment from mail, and with behavior analytics, which notices an account reading far more records than usual. Those controls do not compare a stored hash of a system binary.",
        'r0': 'Baseline important files with SHA-256 after a trusted build or a reviewed patch. Alert on a mismatch with the path, the old hash, and the new hash, and do not treat the alert as a malware name. Avoid MD5 and SHA-1 for this fingerprint because they are broken for collisions. Match the change to a ticket before you call it approved, then rebuild the baseline. Store the baseline on a different host from the files being watched. Prefer login programs, service binaries, and boot-time task files over noisy temporary folders. Leave the changed file in place until the hash and the ticket are recorded. Do not disable the monitor to empty the queue.',
        'r1': 'Watch important files for any change. Compare them with a saved fingerprint. The alert means the bytes changed. It does not name a virus by itself. A good patch changes the fingerprint too. Match the change to a ticket. Keep the saved fingerprint on another machine. Watch programs and startup files first. Ignore noisy temporary folders on purpose. Do not turn the watcher off to clear noise. A mail filter does not do this job. A strange download count does not do this job. Write down both fingerprints before you restore.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse photographs the seal on every important crate after the crate is packed by a trusted crew. Each night a clerk compares the live seal with the photograph. A different seal rings a bell, even when the new seal looks approved. The bell does not say who changed it or whether the change was allowed. The clerk checks the work order before calling it a theft, and the photograph is kept in another building so a thief cannot swap the picture too. Temporary bins that are packed again all day are left out of the album on purpose. The photograph is the stored fingerprint, and the bell is the alert that the file changed. The work order is the change ticket that separates a patch from a surprise.',
        'r4': 'Open the alert and write down the path, the time, and both fingerprints. Check whether a change ticket covers that host and that file. Do not restore the file yet. Do not disable the monitor yet. Note whether the fingerprint store sits on a different host. Leave unrelated files out of the note. Record the owner and permissions if the alert includes them. Only after the ticket check should anyone plan a restore or a new baseline.',
        'exam': 'File integrity monitoring alerts when a file no longer matches a known-good fingerprint. SHA-256 is the hash to use. MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for collisions and are the wrong fingerprint. The alert is not a malware verdict and is not an email gateway stripping an attachment. A patch with a ticket is a legitimate hash change. A hash change with no ticket is unexplained. Behavior analytics flags an unusual user pattern and does not compare two hashes of a binary. Disabling the monitor is not the investigation.',
    },
    'Network access control': {
        'body': 'Network access control checks a device before it joins the real network. A device that fails the check, or a device nobody recognizes, can be placed on a limited network instead of the staff network. The decision is admission, made at connection time. It is not a guess about how the person will behave after they are already inside, and it is not a login prompt by itself.\n\nA common clinic setup uses 802.1X. The laptop identifies itself to the switch, and a RADIUS server on UDP port 1812 decides. A posture check can require disk encryption, a current patch level, or a management agent. If disk encryption is missing, the useful decision is not to dump the laptop onto the staff network and hope. The server can return a remediation network: enough access to turn encryption on, and no path to the chart system. If the check fails open, a sick laptop lands among healthy ones and the control is only a story. Unknown devices should not share the staff network with workstations that already passed. A printer port or a conference jack with the check turned off is a side door and belongs on a written exception list.\n\nA clinic laptop is missing disk encryption. The person plugs into a staff jack at 08:02. The switch does not place that port on the staff network. The RADIUS result names the remediation network. The laptop can open the encryption setup page and cannot open the chart server. The chart system shows no session from that machine. The evidence is the access decision in the RADIUS log, or the switch log that shows the port on the remediation network, timed at the moment of plug-in. A later success on the staff network should appear only after encryption is on and the device is checked again.\n\nDefenders test the fail path on purpose. They write down which ports are exempt and why, and they alarm a port whose check is disabled. They do not add a permanent exemption because one visit was inconvenient. They confirm a failed check does not fall open onto the staff network. They test again after a switch change, because a new closet can be installed with every port trusted.\n\nStudents confuse admission with a firewall rule that filters traffic after everyone is already joined. A firewall between networks does not decide whether this laptop may enter. Students also confuse the device check with behavior analytics, which watches an account after it is inside and might notice five thousand reads. Multifactor authentication is a third mix-up. It proves the person at sign-in. A laptop with encryption off can still belong to someone who knows the password. The device gate and the person gate are different.',
        'r0': "Check the device at join time with 802.1X and a RADIUS decision on UDP port 1812. If disk encryption is missing, place the laptop on a remediation network rather than the staff network. The remediation path should reach the fix and should not reach the chart system. A failed check must not fall open onto the staff network. Record exempt ports, such as a printer or a conference jack, with a reason. Alarm a port whose check is disabled. Test again after switch changes. Read the RADIUS result or the switch port assignment as the evidence, not the user's claim that the cable worked. A password prompt does not replace the device check.",
        'r1': 'Check the device before it joins the real network. A failed check belongs on a limited network. Missing disk encryption is a reason to limit it. The limited network should allow the fix only. It should not reach the charts. Do not let a failed check fall onto the staff network. Write down ports that skip the check. Alarm a port where the check is off. A filter between networks is a later decision. A strange user pattern is a later decision. A password is a person check, not a device check. Test again after a switch is added.',
        'r2': 'A clinic door checks each badge before the hallway. A visitor whose badge fails the health stamp is walked to a side room with a form and a pen, not into the ward. The side room has no door through to patient charts. A guard who waves everyone in when the stamp reader breaks has turned the door into a story. A loading dock that is always open is written on a list with a reason, and someone notices if a second dock appears overnight. The door check is admission of the device. The side room is the limited network for a machine that still needs a fix.',
        'r4': "Plug in a test laptop that fails the device check, or read a real failure from this morning. Write down the network it actually landed on. Confirm that network cannot reach the chart system. Do not move the port onto the staff network yet. List ports that are exempt and the reason for each. Do not add a new exemption yet. Check whether a failed decision falls open. Leave the person's sign-in policy unchanged while you record the device result.",
        'exam': 'Network access control checks a device before it joins and can place a failing or unknown device on a limited network. A laptop missing disk encryption belongs on a remediation network, not the staff network. A check that fails open is not a control. A firewall rule between networks is a later filter, not admission. Behavior analytics watches activity after the device is already inside. Multifactor authentication proves the person and does not prove disk encryption. An exempt printer port is a side door and should be written down, not forgotten.',
    },
    'User and entity behavior analytics': {
        'body': "Behavior analytics learns a normal pattern for a person or a system and flags a break from that pattern. The tool is often called a watch on user and entity behavior. The break is a lead for an analyst. It is not a verdict, and it is not a malware name. An account that suddenly pulls far more data than its own history is the kind of break the watch is built to notice.\n\nThe baseline comes from history: which records this account reads, at what hour, from which site, and how many. A peer group helps, so a nurse is compared with other nurses and not with a database administrator. When today's activity leaves the baseline, an alert opens with the account, the new count, the usual count, and sometimes a source address. The analyst still asks whether a ticket authorized a bulk export. Each individual chart read can be an ordinary action. A signature tool looking for a known bad string will not see the problem. The oddity is the volume and the break from habit. If a theft is folded into the baseline, the new normal hides the next theft, so incident days should not quietly retrain the model.\n\nOne clinic account usually reads five records a day. This afternoon it downloads five thousand, from an address the account has not used. The alert names the account, the old daily count, the new count, and that address, at 15:20. Nothing in the alert says the person is guilty. A scheduled research export can look the same until the ticket is read. The evidence on the screen is the pair of counts and the unfamiliar address. The judgment is a sentence a person writes after that look, not a color the tool paints by itself.\n\nDefenders put an owner on these alerts and write the first question that owner asks. They keep a known incident from becoming the baseline. They do not automatically disable every account that spikes, because a false spike locks a clinic out in the middle of a shift. If a later judgment says the account is stolen, disabling it is containment. That disable is an incident step a person chooses. It is not eradication, and it is not something the analytics tool finishes by raising the alert.\n\nStudents treat the spike as a signature detection. A signature matches a known bad pattern and misses a pile of ordinary reads. Students also mix this watch up with network access control, which decides whether a device may join and does not know that five reads became five thousand. An email gateway that strips a macro is another neighbor. It never sees how many charts an account opened after lunch.",
        'r0': "Build a baseline from an account's own history and from a sensible peer group, such as other nurses rather than administrators. Alert when five usual record reads become five thousand, and include the account, both counts, the time, and any unfamiliar source address. Treat the alert as a lead that still needs a ticket check for an authorized export. Do not retrain the baseline on days already judged to be incidents. Do not auto-disable every spike. Disabling a stolen account, after a person decides it is stolen, is containment, not eradication. Keep an owner and a first question on the queue. A signature match is a different detection and will miss a volume of ordinary reads.",
        'r1': 'Learn what normal looks like for an account. Flag a break from that pattern. Five reads versus a huge download is a break. The flag is a lead, not a guilty verdict. Compare a nurse with other nurses. Ask whether a ticket allowed the export. Do not teach the model that a theft is normal. Do not lock every spike out automatically. A known bad string is a different kind of detection. A device check at the door is different. A mail filter is different. Put a person on the alert with a first question.',
        'r2': 'A librarian knows that one reader usually takes five folders a day and sits at the same table. One afternoon that reader wheels out a cart of thousands and sits somewhere new. The librarian writes a note. The note does not say the reader stole anything. A research request on file would explain the cart, and the librarian checks the file before calling security. If the library then treats the cart as an ordinary day, the next cart will not look strange. The memory of five folders is the baseline. The note about the cart is the alert, and a person still has to decide what it means.',
        'r4': 'Open the alert and write down the account, the usual count, the new count, and the time. Write down any source address that is new for that account. Check for a ticket that authorized a bulk export. Do not disable the account yet. Do not mark the event as the new normal yet. Note who owns the alert and the first question they ask. Leave device-admission rules unchanged. Record the human judgment only after the ticket check.',
        'exam': 'Behavior analytics flags a break from a learned normal pattern, such as five record reads becoming five thousand. The alert is a lead, not a verdict and not a malware signature. A signature matches a known bad pattern and can miss ordinary reads done in huge volume. Network access control checks a device at join time and does not see that volume. An email gateway filters mail and does not count chart reads. Disabling a stolen account is containment, chosen by a person. It is not eradication, and the alert itself is not that disable.',
    },
    'Provisioning accounts': {
        'body': "Provisioning creates an account with the right access on the day the person starts. The source is a request, a ticket, or a trusted hiring record. It is not a hallway favor, and it is not a copy of someone else's powerful account. The new person should be able to work on Monday morning and should not inherit rights nobody reviewed.\n\nA ticket names the person, the start date, and the role. On that morning the account is created, placed in the group that matches the role, and left out of every group the role does not include. Copying an administrator is the failure mode. The new person inherits a pile of rights, the ticket no longer matches the directory, and the next review has to untangle a favor. The creation event, the group list, and the ticket number should agree. An account that appears before any ticket, or a group list that does not match the words on the ticket, means the create step did not follow the request. A disabled account with the password written in a chat is not a finished start either. The person needs a way to sign in that does not spread the secret.\n\nA new nurse starts Monday. Ticket 18421 asks for the nurse role and nothing else. At 07:10 the account exists, it is in the nurse group, and it is not in the domain administrator group. The creation event cites the same ticket. The evidence is that group list beside the ticket. A bad start would show the director's memberships copied across, with no line on 18421 that asked for them. The nurse can open the charts the job needs. The nurse cannot open the administrator consoles the job does not need.\n\nDefenders reconcile new accounts to tickets each day. They refuse a request that says to make the account like the director. They separate the right to create accounts from the right to grant administrator groups, so one hurried person cannot do both unnoticed. They do not leave the first password in a chat or a shared mailbox. They check the start date so an account is not live a month before the person arrives.\n\nStudents mix this start with the day someone leaves. Removing access on the last afternoon, including the badge and the remote token, is the leaving step, not the hiring step. Students also mix the original create with a later extra grant. Adding a right months afterward is a permission assignment and still needs its own request. Creating the account correctly on Monday does not excuse a quiet copy of the director's groups the same afternoon.",
        'r0': 'Create the account on the start date from a ticket that names the person and the role. Ticket 18421 for a new nurse should produce the nurse group and should not produce domain administrator. Tie the creation event to that ticket number and reconcile new accounts to open tickets the same day. Refuse a request to copy the director or another administrator. Separate account creation rights from rights that grant administrator groups. Deliver the first sign-in without placing the password in chat. An account that exists before any ticket is a miss. A later extra grant is a new request, not a silent edit to the original ticket.',
        'r1': 'Create the account on the day the person starts. Use a ticket, not a hallway favor. The ticket names the role. Put the person in that role only. Do not copy an administrator. Match the group list to the ticket. Do not create the account before the ticket exists. Keep the first password out of chat. Separate the right to create accounts from bigger grants. Adding a right months later needs a new request. Taking keys back on the last day is a different step. Check the start date against the directory. Write the ticket number on the create event.',
        'r2': "A hiring desk makes a name badge on Monday morning from a written request, not from a whisper in the hall. The request says nurse, so the badge opens the ward and does not open the director's office. Copying the director's key ring onto the new badge would let the new person into rooms nobody named. The desk stamps the request number on the badge log and refuses to cut a badge before the request exists. A spare key handed over in a crowded corridor is not how the first day should start. The written request is the ticket, and the badge cut on Monday is the account create. The director's key ring is the administrator access that must not be copied.",
        'r4': 'Open the start-day ticket and write down the person, the role, and the date. Open the new account and write down the groups actually present. Do not add groups yet. Confirm the account was not copied from an administrator. Confirm the creation event cites the ticket. Check that the account was not live long before the start date. Leave departure checks for a separate pass. Note where the first password was sent, and do not invent a new one yet.',
        'exam': "Provisioning creates an account on the start day from a request, with the role the job needs. Copying an administrator is not provisioning done well. It is an over-grant. Deprovisioning removes access when the person leaves, including remote access, badges, and tokens. A later extra right is a permission assignment and needs its own request. An account with no ticket is not a successful create. A password parked in chat is not a finished handoff. The nurse role on Monday matches the ticket. The director's groups do not.",
    },
    'Deprovisioning accounts': {
        'body': "Deprovisioning removes or disables access on the day the person leaves. The account, the remote access, the badge, and any token are one action, not four favors spread across a quarter. Waiting for a quarterly cleanup leaves a working key with someone who no longer works here. The leaving date is known in advance often enough that the removal can be ready before the afternoon ends.\n\nThe departure comes from human resources or from the contract end, and it has a date. On that date the account is disabled so the password no longer works. The badge is revoked at the door. Remote tokens are revoked so a laptop at home cannot keep a session. Disabling the directory account and forgetting the badge is a partial job. A shared mailbox or a service password the person knew is reviewed the same day, not left with the old secret because the mailbox is busy. The disable time should match the last afternoon. A report the next morning should show the account disabled, with no successful sign-in after the end time.\n\nContractor Samir finishes on Friday at 16:00. At 16:05 the directory shows the account disabled. At 16:10 the badge does not open the clinic door. The remote token is revoked in the same change window. The evidence is the disable event at 16:05, a badge denial at the door, and no successful remote sign-in over the weekend. A failure looks like a chart login on Monday from that same account, or a badge that still works because nobody opened a ticket with the door team. The directory and the door are both in scope.\n\nDefenders run the departure list every day and compare it with accounts that can still sign in. They put badges and tokens on the same ticket as the directory disable. They do not keep an account just in case past the last day. A shared password the person knew gets rotated as part of the same work. The next morning's report is the check that the afternoon action held.\n\nStudents mix a planned departure with an incident. If an account is stolen while the person still works here, disabling that stolen account is containment. Containment is not eradication. Eradication is removing the malware and closing the hole after evidence is preserved. A planned removal on the last afternoon is neither of those incident phases. It is the end of employment access. Students also think a quarterly review replaces same-day removal. The review can catch a miss. It is not the control that should take the keys back.",
        'r0': "Disable the account on the departure date, and revoke the badge and remote tokens in the same window. Samir's last afternoon at 16:00 should show a directory disable by 16:05 and a door denial immediately after, with no successful remote sign-in later. Include shared mailboxes and service passwords the person knew, and rotate those secrets the same day. Compare the human-resources departure list with accounts that can still authenticate. A quarterly review can catch a miss and does not replace same-day removal. Disabling a stolen account during an incident is containment, not eradication. Eradication removes malware and closes the hole after evidence is preserved. A planned departure is neither incident phase.",
        'r1': 'Take the keys back the day the person leaves. Disable the account that same afternoon. Revoke the badge the same afternoon. Revoke remote tokens the same afternoon. A directory disable alone can forget the door. Rotate shared secrets the person knew. Check the next morning for a later sign-in. Do not wait for a quarterly cleanup. A quarterly pass can only catch a miss. A stolen account is a different decision. Disabling a stolen account is containment. Removing malware comes later, after evidence is kept. Write the door result on the same ticket.',
        'r2': 'On the last afternoon, a departing contractor stands at the desk and hands back a badge, a gate fob, and a cabinet key. The desk tries each one and writes the time it stopped working. Keeping the cabinet key until a seasonal audit leaves the cabinet open to someone who has already left. A master key the contractor also knew gets changed the same day, not next season. If the person were still employed and the badge were stolen in the parking lot, cutting that badge off would be an emergency hold, not a goodbye. The goodbye that collects every key on the last afternoon is the planned removal of access. The emergency hold on a stolen badge is containment, a different decision.',
        'r4': "Open today's departure list and write down the person and the end time. Check the directory and write down whether the account is disabled. Check the door system and write down whether the badge still works. Check remote tokens the same way. Do not delete the account yet. Do not rotate unrelated secrets yet. Note any shared password the person knew. Leave incident containment notes off this ticket unless the account was actually stolen.",
        'exam': 'Deprovisioning removes or disables access on the day the person leaves, including the account, remote access, badges, and tokens. A quarterly cleanup is not a substitute. Disabling a stolen account while the person still works here is containment, not this planned leaving step. Containment is not eradication. Eradication removes malware and closes the hole after evidence is preserved. Provisioning is the create step on the start day. A directory disable that leaves the badge working is incomplete. A successful sign-in the next morning means the removal did not hold.',
    },
    'Permission assignments': {
        'body': "Permission assignments are the rights granted to an account, a group, or a role. The safe set is the set the job needs, written down, and nothing extra. A right added to be helpful is still an assignment. It is often the right an intruder uses later, because it was real access, not a bug. Least privilege is the name of the restraint: grant the work, and do not grant the neighboring work.\n\nRights should sit on a role when they can. The nurse role carries the chart rights, and people receive the role, so a review looks at one role instead of a unique pile on every person. A direct grant on a single account is easy to forget. Copying the director's groups onto a temp is an assignment of many rights nobody listed on a request. Inherited rights count too. An account can look limited on one folder and still reach the files through another group that contains the real power. The evidence of an assignment is the group list or the access entry, and it should match a request that names the job.\n\nA temp at the clinic needs mail. The ticket says the mail role. The account is placed in that group only. It is not a copy of the director's groups, and it is not in the finance group. The directory shows one role membership, and the ticket names mail. A bad assignment shows a long group list that matches the director, with no request that asked for those groups. The temp can read the shared mailbox the job needs. The temp cannot open payroll because nobody granted payroll, even if a helpful administrator almost did.\n\nDefenders review assignments against the role and remove extras. They write a reason and an end date for any exception. They do not grant administrator rights to finish a task that only needs mail. They expand nested groups on the review, because a friendly group can hold another group that holds the real authority. They sample access entries on sensitive folders so inheritance is visible and not only the direct names.\n\nStudents talk as if the file owner may share without it counting. The owner granting a teammate read on a project folder is an assignment too, and the owner can grant too much if nobody reviews it. Students also confuse the rights with the create step. Creating the account on the start date from a ticket is the hiring action. The assignment is which rights that account actually holds. You can create the account correctly and still attach the wrong groups the same morning.",
        'r0': "Grant the rights the job needs and record them against a request. A temp who needs mail receives the mail role and does not receive a copy of the director's groups or the finance group. Prefer a role over a one-off grant so the next review has a single place to look. Expand nested groups, because authority hides inside a group that looks harmless. Treat inherited folder rights as real rights. Put an end date on any exception, including a grant made to be helpful. Separate this review from account creation: the account can be correct while the groups are already wrong. Remove extras rather than adding administrator rights to finish a small task.",
        'r1': 'Grant only the rights the job needs. Write the rights down against a request. Put rights on a role when you can. Do not copy a director onto a temp. A helpful extra right is still a grant. That extra right can become the next incident. Look inside nested groups. Count rights inherited from a parent folder. Give every exception an end date. Do not hand over administrator rights for a mail task. Creating the account is a separate step. The owner of a file can still grant too much. Review that grant the same way.',
        'r2': "A temp on a ward is handed the key to the mail room and is not handed the director's ring or the pharmacy key. Someone who wants to be kind may add the pharmacy key because the temp is standing right there. That kindness is still a key, and it is the key a thief would be glad to find later. A ring can also hide a smaller ring inside it, so the clerk opens every ring during the check and writes down what falls out. A master key lent for one weekend needs a date to come back. The mail-room key is the role the job needs. The director's ring is the extra assignment that should never have been copied.",
        'r4': 'Open the account and write down every group, including groups inside groups. Open the ticket and write down the role it actually requested. Do not add a group yet. Do not remove a group yet. Check one sensitive folder for inherited rights. Note any exception and whether it has an end date. Leave the account enabled or disabled as you found it. Compare the list with the job, not with a neighboring director.',
        'exam': "Permission assignments are the rights you grant. Extra rights given to be helpful are still assignments, and they become the next incident. A temp gets the mail role, not a copy of the director's groups. Nested groups and inherited folder rights count. Provisioning creates the account on the start day. Deprovisioning takes access back on the last day. Neither one is a substitute for looking at the rights themselves. An owner granting read on a project folder is still an assignment and can grant too much. Least privilege means the job's rights, written down.",
    },
    'Identity proofing': {
        'body': 'Identity proofing checks that a person is who they claim to be before you give them an account or reset a password. The check uses facts the organization already trusts. It does not use facts the caller invented on this call. A confident voice, a correct job title, and an employee number read off a stolen badge are weak when the caller is the one offering them.\n\nA director calls the help desk and asks for a reset. The analyst does not change the password on that call. The analyst hangs up and dials the number already stored on the account, or meets the person at a desk that already knows them. If that callback reaches the director and the director confirms the request, the reset can proceed. If it does not, no new password is issued. The ticket records the fact that the stored number was used, the time, and the result. A number the caller just dictated is not the stored number, even if it looks local. Adding a new mail address on the same ticket and sending the password there repeats the same mistake in writing.\n\nThe clinic director calls at 14:05 and asks for a reset. The analyst calls the mobile number already on the account, reaches the director, and only then issues the reset. The ticket says the callback completed at 14:12 to the number on file. The reset event is timestamped after 14:12, not before. The evidence is that order: callback note first, password change second. A bad ticket says the caller was verified because they knew the employee number, and the password was changed at 14:06 while the first call was still up.\n\nDefenders script the callback and forbid the desk from trusting a number read aloud by the caller. They treat a pushy request to skip the callback as a reason to slow down, not a reason to help faster. They do not mail a password to an address the ticket just added. They keep the stored numbers accurate, because a callback to a stale number fails the honest director and teaches the desk to skip the step.\n\nStudents mix this check up with multifactor authentication. A password plus a prompt on a phone is two different kinds of proof at sign-in, after the account already exists. Proofing is how you know the human before you bind that account or replace its secret. Students also mix it up with federation, which trusts identities another organization has already checked, under an agreement. Federation does not replace the callback when your own director calls your own desk. Single sign-on is a third neighbor. It means one authentication is reused by many apps. It is not the check that happens before the reset.',
        'r0': "Reset a password only after a callback to the number already stored on the account, or after an in-person check at a desk that knows the person. A director's call at 14:05 should show the callback completed at 14:12 and the reset only after that time. Knowing an employee number is not proof, because a stolen badge carries that number. Never dial a number the caller just dictated, and never send the new secret to an address added on the same call. Keep stored numbers current so the desk is not tempted to skip a stale record. Multifactor authentication happens at sign-in and assumes the account is already bound to the right human. One login reused by many apps is not this check either.",
        'r1': 'Prove the person before you hand over the account. Use a fact you already stored. Call the number already on the account. Do not call a number the caller just gave you. An employee number on a badge is weak proof. Hang up before you dial the stored number. Change the password only after the person answers. Write the time of the callback on the ticket. Do not mail a secret to a brand new address. A sign-in prompt is a later check. Trusting another organization is a different agreement. One login for many apps is not this step.',
        'r2': "A stranger at the clinic window asks for the spare key to a director's office and recites the room number, which is printed on the door. The clerk does not hand over the key. The clerk calls the phone number written on the director's card in the drawer, not a number the stranger suggests. Only the voice on that stored number can approve the spare. A pushy plea to skip the call is a reason to keep the key in the drawer. Knowing the room number was never the proof. The card in the drawer is the stored number, and the spare key is the password reset. The call back to that card is the proofing step.",
        'r4': 'Open the reset ticket and write down the time of the request. Write down which number was called, and whether it was already on the account. Do not change the password yet. Do not add a new mail address yet. Confirm the callback reached the person and note that time. Compare the password-change time with the callback time. Leave sign-in factors and partner trusts unchanged. If the caller dictated the only number, stop and use the stored one.',
        'exam': "Identity proofing checks that a person is who they claim before an account is issued or a password is reset. The callback uses the number already on file, not a number the caller recites, and not an employee number from a badge. Multifactor authentication is two different kinds of proof at sign-in, after the account exists. Federation trusts another organization's identities under an agreement. Single sign-on means one authentication is reused by many apps. None of those three replaces the callback. A reset completed before the callback is not proofing.",
    },
    'Federation': {
        'body': "Federation lets one organization trust identities from another, under written rules, so people do not need a second password for every shared application. The trust is deliberate and limited. It is not a merge of the two directories. It is not a standing permission to every system you run. Your clinic still decides which application listens to the partner and which groups count.\n\nPartner staff authenticate at home. Their identity provider then issues a signed assertion that this person has authenticated, and the assertion can carry group names. SAML is a common way to carry that signed authentication assertion. The shared application checks the signature, checks that the issuer is the partner it was configured to trust, and checks the audience so the assertion was meant for this application and not for some other one. The application grants only the groups named in the agreement. A partner nurse group may open one shared scheduling app. It must not become a local administrator because a mapping rule was friendly. OAuth is a neighbor that people mix in here. OAuth delegates API access, such as permission for a helper to read a calendar, and it is not by itself proof of who the user is. Sign-in across organizations wants an authentication assertion, not only a delegated API grant.\n\nA partner clinic's nurses open one shared scheduling app. They sign in at the partner, with the partner password, and they do not get a second password at your clinic. The app log shows a signed assertion. The issuer is the partner identity provider. The audience is the scheduling app. The group claim is the partner nurse group named in the agreement, and it is not your local administrator group. The evidence is that trio in the log: issuer, audience, and the limited group. A bad trust would show the partner group mapped onto domain administrators, or an assertion accepted from an issuer nobody wrote into the agreement.\n\nDefenders write the agreement down to the group names. They restrict which applications will accept the partner. They review the claim rules so a wide group at the partner does not map to a powerful group at home. They disable the trust the day the partnership ends, rather than leaving the issuer accepted until someone remembers. They test a partner account that should be refused, not only one that should succeed.\n\nStudents call every shared login single sign-on and stop. Single sign-on means one authentication is reused by many apps, and that can happen entirely inside one organization. One clinic login can open mail and the chart with no partner involved. Federation crosses an organizational boundary and trusts another organization's proof. You can federate a single shared app and still keep every internal app local. Students also treat any token as this trust. A delegated API grant is not a signed authentication assertion, and identity proofing of your own director on the help desk is not a partner agreement.",
        'r0': 'Trust a partner identity only under a written agreement that names the groups and the applications. Accept a signed authentication assertion, which SAML carries, and check the issuer, the audience, and the signature. Map the partner nurse group to the shared scheduling app and do not map it to local administrators. OAuth delegates API access and is not by itself proof of who the user is, so do not treat an API grant as this sign-in. Single sign-on reuses one authentication across many apps and can live inside one organization with no partner. Disable the issuer the day the partnership ends. Test one account that must be refused. Log issuer, audience, and group on every acceptance.',
        'r1': "Trust another organization's identities on purpose. Write down which groups and which apps. Accept a signed note that the person already signed in. Check who sent the note. Check that the note was meant for this app. Grant only the groups in the agreement. Do not turn a partner nurse into a local administrator. End the trust the day the partnership ends. One login reused by many of your own apps is different. A delegated permission to an app is different. Proving your own director on the phone is different. Test a partner account that should fail.",
        'r2': "Two clinics write down that partner nurses may enter one shared class room with the badge from their home clinic, and may not enter the drug room. The door checks that the badge was issued by the partner, that it was meant for this room, and that the seal is intact. A friendly guard who waves the same badge into the director's office has broken the paper, not honored it. When the partnership ends, that badge stops working the same day. Staff who already work here and open several internal rooms with one home badge are a different arrangement. The paper between the clinics is the cross-organization trust. The sealed note on the badge is the signed assertion, and the one internal badge for many rooms is a single login with no partner at all.",
        'r4': 'Open the trust agreement and write down the partner, the application, and the groups. Open a recent acceptance log and write down the issuer, the audience, and the group. Do not add a group mapping yet. Confirm the partner nurse group is not mapped to local administrators. Do not disable unrelated local accounts. Note the date the partnership is supposed to end. Leave API grants to helper applications on a separate list. Record one test account that must be refused.',
        'exam': 'Federation lets one organization trust identities from another, under rules, limited to the groups and applications in the agreement. SAML carries a signed authentication assertion. Check issuer, audience, and signature. OAuth delegates API access and is not by itself proof of who the user is. Single sign-on means one authentication is reused by many apps. Identity proofing is a callback or in-person check before your own account or reset. Mapping a partner group to local administrators is not a successful trust. Leaving the issuer accepted after the partnership ends is a failure.',
    },
    'Single sign-on': {
        'body': "Staff can open mail, the chart, and the schedule after proving who they are one time. That pattern is single sign-on. One authentication creates a session, and each participating application accepts the session instead of collecting its own password. The convenience is the point, and the risk rides with it. A weak proof no longer exposes one program. It exposes every program that trusts the same session. Because that login is shared, it needs two different factor types, or the whole set of doors opens together.\n\nThe mechanism is a session the applications can check, not a password copied into each program. The person authenticates to an identity service. That service records the success and hands each application a signal it already knows how to verify: a cookie on a shared domain, a short-lived ticket, or a signed statement. The chart trusts the signal and skips its own password box. When the session expires, or an administrator revokes it, those applications should stop opening. The design breaks when a session never expires, when clocks disagree about the expiry, or when one application quietly keeps a local password beside the shared login.\n\nElena reaches the clinic desk at 07:42. She authenticates once, and the identity log shows two different factor types on that attempt. Mail and the chart then open with no second password box. The line worth keeping says a session was issued for her account and names mail and the chart as applications that accepted it. Her password is not stored on the chart server. If a later line shows the chart demanding a local password, that application has drifted off the shared login. The drift, the session identifier, and the clock time belong on the ticket.\n\nDefenders treat the shared login as the key to the shift. They require two different factor types, keep the session short enough to match a workday, and revoke it the day the person leaves. They alert when a session is issued from an address the person has never used, and when any application starts accepting a local password again. They keep a written list of which applications trust the session, because an extra name on that list is a new door on the same key. Disabling the account and ending live sessions stops a stolen login from opening the rest of the day. That step is containment, done at the identity service, before anyone hunts through a single workstation.\n\nStudents mix this pattern up with federation and with identity proofing. Federation means you trust accounts that another organization already manages, so a partner enters one shared application with a home login and only the groups named in the agreement. A shared login can live entirely inside one clinic, with no outside partner. Identity proofing checks that a person is real before you create an account or reset one, often by a callback to a number already on file. Reusing one proof across several applications is the shared login. Trusting another organization's accounts is federation. Checking the person before the account exists is proofing.",
        'r0': "One authentication at the identity service should open mail and the chart, and the log should show a single session rather than two separate password checks. Require two different factor types on that shared login, because one stolen password now reaches every joined application. Keep the session lifetime inside the workday, and revoke the session when the person leaves or the laptop is reported missing. Alert when the session comes from an address that account has never used. Maintain a list of applications allowed to trust the session, and investigate any application that starts asking for a local password again. Disabling the account and killing live sessions is containment of the access. Federation, which trusts another organization's accounts, is a separate decision. The callback that proves a person before an account is created is identity proofing, not the shared session.",
        'r1': 'People sign in once for many programs. The identity service remembers that success. Other programs trust the session. They should not ask for another password. One weak login opens every joined door. Protect that login with two kinds of proof. Keep the session only for the shift. End it when the person leaves. Watch for a sign-in from a strange address. Watch for a program that demands its own password. Write down which programs trust the session. A stolen session is contained by cutting it off. Trusting another clinic is a different choice. Proving a new hire is a different check.',
        'r2': 'A clinic badge office stamps one pass in the morning, and the lab, the pharmacy, and the records room all honor that stamp. Nurses do not collect a fresh secret at every door. If the stamp is easy to forge, every room falls, so the office asks for a strong check before it stamps anything. The stamp expires at the end of the shift, and a lost badge is cancelled at the office rather than at each door. A partner hospital is a different matter: their staff bring a pass their own office already issued, under a written agreement. Proving a new hire with a phone call back to a known number happens before any pass exists. The morning stamp that many rooms honor is the shared login. The partner pass is federation, and the phone call is identity proofing.',
        'r4': 'Open the identity log for the account and the time. Write down the session identifier and which applications accepted it. Confirm the shared login asked for two different kinds of proof. Check that the session has an end time inside the workday. List every application allowed to trust that session. Do not add or remove an application yet. Note any application that still asks for its own password. Leave the account enabled until the notes are written, unless the session is already known to be stolen.',
        'exam': "One authentication that opens several applications is the shared login, even when no outside partner is involved. Trusting another organization's accounts is federation, and a callback before an account exists is identity proofing. A session with no expiry, or an application that quietly keeps a local password, breaks the pattern even if the morning sign-in looked normal. Disabling the account and ending live sessions is containment of a stolen login. It is not a claim that every trace on a workstation has already been removed.",
    },
    'LDAP': {
        'body': "A directory is the clinic phone book of accounts, groups, and attributes, and LDAP is the protocol programs use to read it and to check a name and a secret against it. The conversation has two common ports. Ordinary LDAP listens on 389 and can send the lookup in the clear. LDAPS wraps the same conversation in TLS and listens on 636. If the chart can still reach 389, anyone on that path can read names, group membership, and sometimes a password during a simple bind. The protected port is the one that should answer. Anonymous browsing of the whole directory should be off, so a stranger with no account cannot walk the tree.\n\nThe client first binds, which is how it authenticates to the directory, and then it searches. A search names a starting point, a filter, and the attributes it wants back, such as a mail address or group membership. A simple bind sends the distinguished name and the secret. A stronger setup uses its own application account, with rights to read only the attributes that application needs. The directory answers with entries or with a refusal. Injection shows up when an application pastes a person's input straight into the filter, so a crafted value changes the search. You stop that by escaping the input and by refusing a filter the application did not intend to run.\n\nChris supports the chart. The application server queries the directory on port 636, with certificate checking turned on. A capture on the chart host shows a TLS session to 636 and no conversation to 389. The directory log shows a bind by the chart service account, then a search for one person, returning only the groups the chart is allowed to see. An older test server still answers on 389 and allows an anonymous bind that lists every account. That open listener is the fact on the ticket: port 389, anonymous bind permitted, full listing returned.\n\nDefenders close 389 once 636 is proven, or they refuse any unprotected bind on 389. They disable anonymous and unauthenticated binds. They give each application a limited account and an access list that hides secrets and unnecessary attributes. They alert on a search that asks for the whole tree, on a bind by an unexpected account, and on a spike of failed binds against one name. A signed statement that a person already signed in elsewhere is a different message, and so is a limited token handed to a helper application.\n\nStudents confuse the directory protocol with SAML and with OAuth. SAML carries a signed assertion that an identity provider has already authenticated the person. OAuth is delegated authorization: a helper receives a limited token and never receives the password. LDAP answers questions about entries, and it can check a secret during a bind, on 389 if you leave the path careless or on 636 if you do not. The port and the kind of message separate them. A lookup on 636 is not an authentication assertion, and a search result is not permission for a third-party calendar tool to act later.",
        'r0': 'LDAP on port 389 can carry a bind and a search in the clear. LDAPS on port 636 is the same protocol inside TLS. Point the chart at 636, check the directory certificate, and stop listening on 389 after a test proves the protected path. Turn off anonymous and unauthenticated binds so a blank login cannot list the tree. Give the chart its own service account and an access list that returns only the attributes it needs. Escape user input before it enters a search filter. Alert on a search of the whole tree and on a burst of failed binds. A signed authentication assertion is a different protocol. A limited token for another application is delegated authorization. Neither one is a directory lookup on 636.',
        'r1': 'The directory is the account phone book. Programs ask it questions over the network. Ask over the protected form of the conversation. A clear conversation can be read on the path. Do not allow a stranger to list every name. Give each program its own limited account. A search should start from a narrow place. Do not paste raw input into a search. Watch for a search that wants the whole tree. Watch for many failed checks of one name. Close the clear listener after the locked path works. A signed note of a login is a different message. A limited token for a helper is a different message.',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps one staff phone book in a locked office. A clerk who needs a number knocks, shows a badge, and asks for one name, not the whole drawer. If the office door is left open, anyone in the hall can copy every page, including notes that were never meant to travel. The clerk writes the request on a form the office designed, rather than letting a visitor scribble a question that changes what the drawer returns. Shouting the question down an open hallway is the careless version. Carrying it inside a locked corridor is the version you keep. The phone book is the directory, and the knock plus the question is the protocol. A signed letter saying someone already proved who they are, or a coupon that lets a helper see one calendar, is a different paper entirely.',
        'r4': 'Confirm the chart queries the directory on port 636. Write down that port and whether the certificate check succeeded. From the same host, test port 389 and record whether it still accepts a connection. Attempt a bind with no account and save the result. Do not close the listener yet. Note the account the chart binds as, and the attributes the search returned. Find any search that walked the whole tree. Schedule the closure of port 389 only after those notes are in the ticket.',
        'exam': 'Port 389 is clear LDAP. Port 636 is LDAPS, the same lookups inside TLS. A directory search is not a SAML assertion, and it is not an OAuth token. SAML says a trusted identity provider already authenticated the person. OAuth gives a helper limited authority without the password. Anonymous bind on 389, with a full listing, is a directory exposure. Closing that listener is the fix. Calling the lookup an authentication assertion, or calling it delegated authorization, gives the wrong name to the message you actually captured.',
    },
    'OAuth': {
        'body': 'OAuth is delegated authorization. A person lets one application act in a limited way at another service, and the helper receives a token instead of the password. The token names what was granted, such as reading a calendar, and it does not include the rest of the mailbox. The person can revoke the grant without changing the password that still opens mail. That split is the whole idea. If the helper ever sees the password, the design has been abandoned, no matter what the login screen called the button.\n\nThe person is sent to an authorization server they already trust. They authenticate there, see the requested scope, and consent. The helper then receives an authorization code and trades it for an access token. The calendar checks who the token was meant for, when it expires, and which scope it carries. A refresh token can mint a new access token and is itself a secret. A stolen token, a scope wider than the task, or a redirect to the wrong place is the failure to watch.\n\nDana installs a calendar helper on a clinic laptop. The consent screen at 09:10 lists one scope, reading the calendar, and does not list mail. She approves it. The helper configuration shows an access token and no copy of her password. A test call to the calendar succeeds. A test call to the mailbox fails because the token scope does not include it. The authorization log shows the client name, the calendar scope, her account, and the grant time. Those four fields are what you write down. If the helper files also contain her password, the integration is not delegation. It is password collection with extra steps.\n\nDefenders register each client, keep the redirect list tight, and prefer a short access token, on the order of minutes, with a refresh token that can be revoked. They refuse a token whose audience is some other application. They review consented scopes the way they review group membership, and they remove grants the day the person or the vendor leaves. They watch for a client asking for every scope, and for a burst of refresh calls from a new address. They do not paste the token into a ticket. The directory can still answer who Dana is and which groups she has. This grant does not replace that phone book, and it does not replace a signed assertion that she authenticated to the chart.\n\nStudents call this authentication because a login screen appeared. The login proved Dana to the authorization server. The object the helper carried away is authorization: a limited right to call one service. A signed assertion is a different object. It tells a relying application that an identity provider has already authenticated the person, and that application then opens its own session. The fact that separates them is what the receiver may conclude. A calendar token lets the helper read the calendar. It does not, by itself, prove to the chart that Dana signed in.',
        'r0': 'Delegated authorization hands a helper an access token after the person consents at the authorization server. The token carries scope, audience, and expiry, and the calendar must check all three. A scope that only reads the calendar must not open mail. Prefer an authorization code exchange, and keep the redirect list to registered addresses. A ten-minute access token limits the value of a theft. A refresh token can mint the next access token and must be revocable. Record the client, the scope, the account, and the time, and do not paste the token into the ticket. A signed authentication assertion is a different object. A directory lookup on port 636 is a different object. This grant is the limited authority the helper holds.',
        'r1': 'A helper should not receive the password. The person approves a limited grant. The helper gets a token for that grant. The token should name a narrow task. Reading a calendar is not reading mail. The calendar checks the token before it answers. A stolen token should die quickly. A longer ticket can be cancelled. Register where the helper is allowed to return. Refuse a token meant for some other program. Review grants the way you review groups. Remove a grant when the person or the vendor leaves. A signed proof of login is a different object. A directory lookup is a different object.',
        'r2': 'A clinic lets a temp clerk fetch one shelf of charts for the afternoon and hands over a dated slip rather than the master key. The slip says which shelf and when it expires. The records room reads the slip and will not open the pharmacy cabinet just because the slip is real. If the clerk loses the slip, the office voids that slip without changing the lock on the front door. A letter that says the clerk already showed identification at the front desk is a different paper, and so is the staff phone book the office uses to look a name up. The dated slip is delegated authority for one task. The letter is proof the person authenticated, and the phone book is the directory.',
        'r4': "Open the consent record for the helper and the account. Write down the client name, the scope, the account, and the grant time. Confirm the scope does not include mail if the task is only the calendar. Check the token lifetime and the redirect addresses on the client registration. Do not revoke the grant yet. Confirm the stored configuration contains a token and not the person's password. Note whether a test call outside the scope was refused. Leave the client registration unchanged until those notes are in the ticket.",
        'exam': "Delegated authorization gives a helper a limited token and withholds the password. A signed assertion that the person already authenticated is not this grant, and a directory search is not this grant. A login screen at the authorization server does not turn the token into proof for every other application. A calendar scope that also opens mail is an overly wide grant, not a second factor and not a directory bind. Stealing the token is theft of that grant. Changing the person's password does not, by itself, revoke a refresh token you left active.",
    },
    'SAML': {
        'body': 'SAML carries a signed assertion that a trusted identity provider has already authenticated the person. The chart, acting as the relying party, checks that signature and then creates a local session. It does not collect a second password, and it does not receive the password used at the identity provider. The assertion is a statement about a completed authentication, with a subject, a time window, and an audience. If any of those checks are skipped, a captured or forged assertion becomes a login.\n\nThe person authenticates at the identity provider. That provider builds an assertion, signs it, and sends the person back to the chart with the assertion in hand. The chart validates the signature against a certificate it holds for that provider, confirms the audience is itself, and confirms the clock sits inside the window the assertion names. It should refuse a replay of an assertion it has already consumed. It then opens a session of its own. The certificate and the audience are the configuration that makes the trust real. A chart that accepts any signature, or that ignores the audience, will accept an assertion meant for a different application.\n\nAlex opens the chart at 08:05 and is sent to the clinic identity provider. The provider records a successful authentication with two different factor types. The browser returns to the chart, and the chart log shows an assertion accepted: subject Alex, audience the chart, issuer the clinic identity provider, and a validity window of a few minutes around 08:05. The chart session starts. There is no password field on the chart for this path, and the chart store does not contain the password. If the log shows the signature was not checked, or the audience was empty, the acceptance is the defect, even though Alex really did authenticate.\n\nDefenders hold the identity provider signing certificate, alert before it expires, and refuse assertions signed by anything else. They enforce audience and the time window, and they keep a short replay cache so the same assertion cannot mint a second session. They keep clocks synchronized, because a skewed clock either locks people out or widens the window an attacker can reuse. They limit which providers each application trusts. A stolen assertion is contained by ending the chart session and, if the provider session is still live, ending that too. The directory can still be queried for groups after the session exists. The assertion did not hand a helper a lasting token to call the calendar on its own.\n\nStudents swap this for delegated authorization because both involve a redirect and a trusted server. Delegated authorization ends in a token whose scope lets one helper call one resource, without proving to the chart that a person just authenticated. This assertion ends in authentication. A directory search on port 636 is a third neighbor. It is not a signed statement of a login that just happened. The object in the log is the separator. An accepted assertion with an issuer, a subject, an audience, and a signature check is this protocol. A scope on an access token is the other one.',
        'r0': "The chart should accept a signed SAML assertion and skip its own password. Check the issuer, the signature against the identity provider certificate, the audience, and the time window before creating a session. A window of about five minutes limits replay, and a replay cache should refuse an assertion already used. Synchronize clocks so the window means what it says. Record subject, issuer, audience, and the result of the signature check. Do not store the person's password on the chart. Ending the chart session, and the provider session if it is still live, contains a stolen assertion. A scoped access token for a helper would be delegated authorization. A directory bind and search on port 636 would be a lookup. Those are not this message.",
        'r1': 'A trusted office signs a note that login already happened. The chart checks the signature on that note. It checks who the note was written for. It checks that the note is still fresh. Then it opens a session of its own. It should not ask for a second password. It should not keep the password at all. Refuse a note signed by a stranger. Refuse a note aimed at a different program. Refuse the same note a second time. Keep the clocks close or the time check lies. A limited token for a helper is different. A directory search is different too.',
        'r2': 'At a clinic door, the front desk checks a person and writes a short signed note: this person was checked at this minute, and the note is for the records room only. The records room verifies the signature, the name of the room, and the minute, then lets the person in without asking for a second secret. A note for the pharmacy is useless at records, and a note from yesterday is useless today. A coupon that lets a courier open one cabinet is not that note, and neither is the staff phone book. The signed note is an authentication assertion the records room trusts. The coupon is delegated authority, and the phone book is the directory.',
        'r4': "Find the chart log line for the sign-in you are reviewing. Write down the subject, the issuer, the audience, and whether the signature check passed. Note the validity window and the clock time on the chart. Confirm the chart trusts only the clinic identity provider certificate. Do not rotate that certificate yet. Check whether the same assertion was accepted twice. Confirm the chart did not store the person's password. Leave the trust list unchanged until the ticket has those facts.",
        'exam': 'A signed assertion that the identity provider already authenticated the person is this protocol. A token that lets a helper read a calendar without the password is delegated authorization, not an authentication assertion. A directory bind and search on port 636 is a lookup, not a signed login. Skipping the audience check, or skipping the signature check, means the chart is not really relying on the assertion. Ending the local session contains a stolen assertion. It does not erase a directory entry, and it does not cancel a refresh token the chart never held.',
    },
    'Attestation': {
        'body': "Attestation is a signed statement that some fact is true, made by a party you already trust to measure that fact. The fact might be that a laptop disk is encrypted, that a person is still employed, or that a required control is still in place. The statement is evidence for a later decision. It is not the decision itself, and it is not an access model. A network can refuse entry when the statement is missing or fails to verify. The laptop does not get to grade its own homework with an unsigned box on a form.\n\nA useful statement is bound to the thing it describes and is hard to forge. A laptop with a TPM can quote measurements the chip has stored. The signature comes from a key sealed to that chip, so a different machine cannot copy the quote and present it as its own. An employment system can sign a statement that a badge is still active. The receiver checks the signature, the time, and that the quoted values match policy: encryption on, a known boot, a patch level you accept. A self-reported field in an agent, with no signature, is a rumor. Treat a rumor as a rumor.\n\nHelen brings a clinic laptop to the staff network at 07:55. Before the switch grants a full connection, the access service asks the laptop to attest. The log shows a quote signed by the platform key, disk encryption reported on, and a signature that validates. The port then opens onto the staff network. A personal laptop presents an unsigned field claiming the same encryption. The service leaves that machine on a limited network and records that the signature was absent. The quote, the key identifier, the encryption fact, and the network that was granted are the four things to keep.\n\nDefenders decide which facts must be attested, who may sign, and what happens on failure. Failure should leave the device off the staff network, not on it with a warning nobody reads. They protect the list of trusted signing keys, and they notice when a key is about to expire. They distinguish a fresh quote from a replay of yesterday's good quote. They do not let the user click past a failed check on a machine that will then see patient charts. When the statement passes, other controls still apply. A healthy device can still be forbidden to copy a secret file into a public folder.\n\nStudents hear that the system checked the laptop and call the check mandatory access control, or they hear that someone approved the laptop and call it discretionary access control. Mandatory access control is the system enforcing labels the owner cannot give away. Discretionary access control is the owner choosing who else may have a file. Attestation does neither job. It supplies a checked fact, such as encryption on a named device at 07:55. Some later control may use that fact. The separator is the object in the log: a signed quote about device state, versus a label the system enforced, versus an access entry the owner wrote.",
        'r0': 'Require a signed attestation before a laptop joins the staff network. A TPM quote, signed by a key sealed to that chip, should report disk encryption on and a boot measurement you accept. Check the signature, the key identity, and the time, and reject a replay of an older quote. An unsigned self-report is not evidence. On failure, leave the device on a limited network and log that the signature was absent, along with the key identifier if one was offered. Do not confuse this statement with a label the operating system enforces, and do not confuse it with an access entry the owner edits. The quote supports a decision about the network. It is not the access model.',
        'r1': 'A trusted chip can sign a statement about the laptop. The statement might say disk encryption is on. The network checks the signature before it trusts the claim. A box the user ticks is not that statement. A failed check should keep the laptop off the real network. Write down the key that signed the quote. Refuse an old quote presented again. Protect the list of keys you trust. A file label the system enforces is a different control. An owner choosing readers is a different control. The signed fact can feed a later decision. It does not replace that decision.',
        'r2': "A clinic will not let a courier into the drug room on a spoken claim that the case is locked. The case carries a seal applied by a meter the clinic trusts, and the door guard checks the seal, the time, and the meter number. A sticker the courier wrote by hand does not count, and the courier waits in the hall. Passing the seal check does not let the courier decide who may open which cabinet, and it does not let them scrape a colored band off a folder. The seal is a statement that a measured fact is true. The band the archive enforces even against the owner, and the owner's own choice of who may read a folder, are separate rules.",
        'r4': 'Open the access log for the laptop and the time it tried to join. Write down whether a signed quote was present and whether the signature validated. Record the signing key identifier and the facts inside the quote, including disk encryption. Note which network the device was placed on. Do not move it to the staff network yet. Compare the quote time with the join time, and mark the quote if it is old. Leave the list of trusted signing keys unchanged until the ticket records what you saw.',
        'exam': 'A signed statement that a fact is true, such as disk encryption on a named laptop, is attestation. The system enforcing a label the owner cannot override is a different control. The owner choosing who else may have a file is a different control. An unsigned self-report is not attestation. Using the statement to pick a network does not turn the statement into either access model. The log shows a quote and a signature, or it shows a label decision, or it shows an owner editing an access entry. Those are three different objects.',
    },
    'Mandatory access control': {
        'body': 'Mandatory access control means the system enforces labels, and the owner of a file cannot simply give the file away. A subject has a clearance. An object has a classification. The system compares them on every read or write and refuses the operation the label forbids. The person who created the file can still be denied. That is the point of calling the control mandatory. The policy lives with the system, not with whoever happens to own the object today.\n\nOn a host that implements this model, each process runs with a label, and each file or directory carries one too. A web service confined to a web label can read its own content and cannot read payroll, even if the service is tricked into trying, and even if an attacker influenced an account that owns the payroll files. Changing a label is itself a privileged operation, reserved for a role allowed to relabel, and that change is logged. Ordinary users do not get that right. If they did, the control would collapse into a suggestion the owner can ignore.\n\nImani owns a project file labeled secret on a clinic research server. She tries to copy it into a public folder so a vendor can pick it up. The copy fails. The audit line shows a denial: subject Imani, object the secret file, destination a public directory, result denied, reason the label. Her ownership is still in place, and a discretionary list on the file even names her as owner. The denial happened anyway. The line you keep is the label on the source, the label on the destination, and the component that refused. You would not see a successful permission edit by the owner as the event that decided the outcome.\n\nDefenders assign labels from the sensitivity of the data, not from a hallway request, and they keep relabel rights off ordinary accounts. They alert on denials against a secret label or a payroll label. They test that a confined service cannot read outside its label after every policy change. When a label must change, a named administrator does it, the ticket records the old label and the new one, and the change is rare. A signed note that a laptop disk is encrypted can feed some other decision. It does not enforce this label, and neither does an owner adding a teammate to a folder.\n\nStudents meet an owner who cannot copy their own file and assume the permissions are broken. Under the model where the owner chooses, that copy would succeed, because the owner decides who else may have the file, including a copy placed in a new folder. Here the system enforces the label even against the owner. A signed statement that some fact is true, such as encryption on a device, is the other neighbor. A quote about a laptop does not relabel a file, and a file label does not attest that a device is healthy. The separator is who can override. If the owner can hand the secret file to a public folder, you are not looking at this control.',
        'r0': 'The system compares a subject clearance with an object classification and denies the access the label forbids, even when the owner requests it. A web service confined to its own label must not read payroll after a flaw in the service is abused. Reserve the right to change labels for a named administrative role, and log every change. Alert on denials that name a secret label. Test the confinement after each policy edit. An owner editing an access list can succeed in a place where this control must fail. A signed device quote does not move a file label. The audit line should name the source label, the destination label, and the denial.',
        'r1': 'The system wears the label, not the owner. A secret file stays secret even if the owner objects. Clearance is compared with the file label. A copy into a public folder should fail. A tricked program should stay inside its own label. Ordinary accounts should not change labels. Log every label change a privileged role makes. Alert when a secret label produces a denial. Test a confined service after each policy change. An owner editing a share list is a different model. A signed note about a laptop is a different control. If the owner can give the file away, look elsewhere.',
        'r2': 'In a clinic archive, each folder wears a colored band, and each staff badge wears a matching band. The clerk will not hand a red-band folder to a badge that lacks red, even when the person who filed the folder asks nicely and even when that person created it. A clerk cannot peel the band off. Only a named supervisor, with a logged key, can change bands. A note from the door saying a laptop case was locked does not change any band. Letting the creator add a colleague to an ordinary reading list is a different rule, one the creator controls. The bands the archive enforces against the creator are labels the system controls. The reading list the creator edits is the owner choosing.',
        'r4': 'Read the denial for the copy you are investigating. Write down the subject, the source label, the destination label, and the result. Confirm the account is the owner and was still denied. Do not change the label yet. Check whether that account is allowed to change labels at all. Note the component that enforced the denial. Leave the owner-edited access list as it is while you record the label decision. If the owner also changed permissions, put that edit in a separate note and do not merge the two causes.',
        'exam': 'The system enforcing a label, even against the owner, is mandatory access control. The owner choosing who else may have the file is the other access model. A signed statement that disk encryption is on does not move a label. If the owner successfully copied a secret file into a public folder, this control did not stop them. A denial that names both labels is the evidence you want. A permission edit by the owner is evidence of the model where the owner chooses. Do not call a device quote a label decision.',
    },
    'Discretionary access control': {
        'body': "Discretionary access control lets the owner decide who else may have the file. The owner creates a project folder and grants read to a teammate, or grants more than read, and the system honors that choice. Rights sit on the object as an access list or as permission bits the owner can edit. There is no label the system will enforce against the owner's wishes. If the owner can hand the file to the whole department, the system allows it. Review exists because the model itself will not say no.\n\nThe mechanism is the access entry. An owner, or someone the owner has allowed to manage access, adds an account or a group and a set of actions such as read, write, or delete. The system evaluates that list when someone tries the action. Inheritance may copy the list onto new files in the folder, which is how one generous grant spreads further than the owner noticed. Transferring ownership moves the discretion to someone else. None of these steps consult a clearance. The question the system asks is only whether the list the owner wrote permits this subject to do this action.\n\nIdris owns the clinic project folder for a vendor review. On Tuesday he grants read to one teammate, which matches the task. On Wednesday he also grants write to a large department group, because the meeting is tomorrow. The access list shows his account as owner, the teammate with read, and the department group with write. The change log attributes both edits to him. Nobody else approved them. The concrete fact is those two entries and his identity as the editor. A secret label did not block the wider grant. There was no label decision in the log at all.\n\nDefenders accept that owners will share too much unless someone reviews the list. They schedule reviews on sensitive folders, and they alert when a grant goes to a large group or to an account outside the clinic. They keep the right to own a folder separate from the right to change a classification label, so a discretionary grant cannot quietly rewrite a label. They teach owners to grant a small group rather than a crowd, and they remove grants when the task ends. They do not pretend an owner-edited list is the same as a system-enforced label. A signed device statement can still decide which network a laptop joins. It does not choose the names on this list.\n\nStudents see a denial and assume every denial is a mandatory label. Under this model, a denial means the owner's list did not include the subject, or did not include the action they tried. The owner can add them a minute later, and the same action succeeds. Under the label model, the owner cannot override that way. A signed claim that a fact is true, such as encryption on a laptop, is not either choice. It does not write access entries. The separator is who is allowed to decide. If the owner can widen access and no policy engine refuses, you are looking at the owner's discretion.",
        'r0': 'The owner writes the access list, and the system enforces that list rather than a label. Record the editor, the subject who received the grant, and the actions granted. A read grant to one teammate fits a task. A write grant to an entire department group is the owner choosing too much, and this model will allow it. Review sensitive folders on a schedule, and alert on grants to large groups or to people outside the clinic. A label model would still deny an owner who tries to place a secret file in a public folder. A signed device fact is not an access entry. If the owner can reverse the decision alone, the control is discretionary.',
        'r1': 'The owner picks who else may open the file. The system follows that choice. A list on the folder names each person and each action. The owner can add a teammate. The owner can also add too many people. Nothing in this model blocks a generous owner. Review the list because the system will not. Alert when a grant hits a huge group. Remove the grant when the task ends. A label the owner cannot override is a different model. A signed note about a device is a different control. Look at who was allowed to make the change.',
        'r2': 'A researcher keeps a cabinet in a shared clinic office and may hand copies of a folder to colleagues. The building does not second-guess the researcher. If the researcher gives write access to everyone who has a key to the floor, the copies multiply, and only a later review notices. A colored band that the archive will not let the researcher remove is a stricter rule. A sealed meter reading about a laptop is not a decision about the cabinet at all. The cabinet key the researcher lends is the owner choosing who enters. The band the researcher cannot peel off is the system enforcing a label.',
        'r4': 'Open the access list on the folder and the change log beside it. Write down the owner, each new subject, and the actions granted. Note the time and that the owner made the edit. Do not remove the grant yet. Check whether a label also exists on the file and whether any label denial was logged. If there was no label denial, say so in the ticket. Leave both the list and any labels unchanged until the review. Bring the large-group grant to that review as the item to accept or reverse.',
        'exam': 'The owner choosing who else may have the file is discretionary access control. A system that denies the owner because a label forbids the copy is the mandatory model, not this one. A signed quote that disk encryption is on does not write the access list. A wide grant the owner made, with no label denial in the log, fits this model. Review is how you catch that grant. The model will not refuse the owner on sensitivity grounds. If the question says the owner could not override, you have left this model.',
    },
    'Role-based access control': {
        'body': "Role-based access control grants rights to a role, and people receive the role. You change the role when the job changes, instead of editing a private list on every person. Nurses hold the nurse role and therefore the chart rights that role carries. A nurse who becomes a manager loses the nurse role and gains the manager role, and the old rights leave with the old role. The account name is not where the rights live. If extra rights are still stuck to the person after the role change, the model has been bypassed.\n\nThe mechanism is membership. An administrator, or a process fed by a ticket, puts the account into a role group. Permissions on applications, shares, and mail are granted to that group. When the account leaves the group, those permissions stop on the next evaluation. A well-kept role has a written purpose, an owner, and a list of rights shorter than a copy of whatever the last person accumulated. Nested roles deserve a diagram, because a role inside a role can carry rights the ticket never mentioned. The question at access time should be which roles this account holds, not which favors were glued on beside them.\n\nChen works the clinic floor as a nurse through Friday. The directory shows the account in the nurse role and not in the manager role. On Monday the job changes. A ticket says to remove the nurse role and add the manager role, and the directory shows exactly that by 08:00. The chart then allows the manager functions and no longer allows a nurse-only task that was not part of the new job. The evidence is the ticket, the two membership changes, and a chart result that matches the new role rather than a personal exception. If the nurse role is still present on Tuesday, removal of the old job did not happen.\n\nDefenders tie role changes to the ticket that changes the job, on the same day. They forbid copying one person's roles onto another as a shortcut. They review roles that include administrative rights, and they split a role that has become a junk drawer of unrelated permissions. They remove direct permissions glued to the account beside the role, because those leftovers survive the job change. A condition about the hour or the source address can still deny Chen after the role is correct. Device attributes can still deny Chen. The role answer is not the whole access answer.\n\nStudents treat a role name as if it were a live rule about time and place, or as if it were a bundle of device facts. A condition rule can refuse payroll at 02:00 from a home address even though the payroll role is present. An attribute decision can refuse a chart on a personal laptop even though the doctor role is present. The role only answers which job the account is wearing. The separator is what you edit when the job changes. You edit membership. You do not rewrite a time rule, and you do not adjust a device attribute, to move a nurse into a manager job.",
        'r0': 'Grant rights to the nurse role and the manager role, then put people in those roles. When the job moves from nurse to manager, remove the nurse membership and add the manager membership on the same day as the ticket. Rights should follow that membership change with no personal exception left behind. Direct permissions stuck on the account survive the job change and defeat the model. A condition can still deny access at 02:00 or from the wrong network while the role stays correct. An attribute check can still deny a personal laptop to someone who holds the right role. Review administrative roles whose rights no longer match the written purpose. The edit for a job change is membership, not a clock and not a device attribute.',
        'r1': "Rights belong to the job, not the person's name. People receive a role for the job they have. Changing jobs means changing roles. Old rights should leave with the old role. Do not copy one person's roles onto another. Write down what each role is for. Keep powerful roles on a short list. Remove extra rights glued to the account. A clock rule can still say no. A device check can still say no. The role does not read the clock for you. Look at membership when the job changes.",
        'r2': "A clinic wardrobe issues a nurse coat or a manager coat, and the doors respond to the coat rather than to the wearer's name. When someone is promoted on Monday, the wardrobe takes the nurse coat back and issues the manager coat. Old keys left in the coat pockets still open the old rooms, which is why the wardrobe checks the pockets too. A door that opens only during the day, or only from the finance hall, is obeying a posted rule, not the coat. A door that inspects the laptop in the bag is looking at facts about that visit. The coat is the role. The posted hours and the bag inspection are other decisions, and changing coats does not rewrite them.",
        'r4': 'Open the account and list every role membership. Write down the job on the ticket beside that list. If the job changed, confirm the old role is gone and the new role is present. Do not add or remove a role yet. Look for permissions granted directly on the account rather than through a role. Note any role whose purpose you cannot find in writing. Leave device checks and time rules for a separate note. Record the membership evidence before you edit anything.',
        'exam': 'Rights that follow a role, so a job change is a membership change, are role-based access control. A deny at 02:00 or from the wrong network, while the role is still present, is a condition rule. A deny on a personal laptop for an account that holds the right role is an attribute decision. Extra rights glued to the person are leftovers, not the role model. Editing the clock, or editing a device policy, does not move a nurse into a manager job. Membership does.',
    },
    'Rule-based access control': {
        'body': 'Rule-based access control opens or closes a door when conditions match, such as the hour or the source address. An IPv4 address on the finance network during business hours can be allowed while the same account is refused from a home address at night. The account can hold the right role and still be refused because a condition failed. Payroll files open from the finance network during the workday. The same account at 02:00, or from home, is denied even though nobody removed the payroll role. The rule is the decision. The role did not change between the daytime success and the nighttime failure.\n\nEach rule names a condition and a result. A schedule can allow a sign-in from 07:00 to 19:00 and refuse it outside that window. A network condition can require the session to come from the finance range. Conditions combine: finance network and business hours, both required. Evaluation happens at the moment of access, against the request in front of you, not against the job title stored on the account. The log should say which condition failed. A denial that only says access was denied forces a guess among the clock, the address, and a missing role.\n\nCole holds the payroll role and opens the payroll share at 10:15 from a workstation on the finance network. The log shows an allow, with the role present and the time and source conditions met. At 02:14 the same account tries again from a home IPv4 address. The log shows a deny, role still present, time 02:14, source outside the finance network. The clock and the address both fail. The concrete facts are those two lines side by side: same account, same role, different result, and the conditions named on the denial. You would not fix this by adding another role. The role was never the missing piece.\n\nDefenders write the conditions in the ticket that justifies them, and they test both sides. A permitted call during business hours from the finance network should succeed. A call at 02:00, and a call from outside that network, should fail, and the log should name the condition. They watch for a rule disabled during a change and never restored. They keep the time zone explicit so 19:00 means the clinic clock, not a laptop set to another region. A role still answers what the job is. This control answers whether the request met the conditions.\n\nStudents see a nighttime denial and assume the person lost a role, or they assume a rich attribute policy weighed the laptop, the data label, and the place together. The role model would have allowed Cole, because the payroll role never left the account. An attribute policy can consider who is asking, which device this is, where the request comes from, and how sensitive the file is, as facts in one decision. A rule of this kind is narrower and explicit: this hour, this network. The separator is the reason on the log. If the reason is the hour or the IPv4 source, and the role is unchanged, you are looking at a condition rule.',
        'r0': 'Allow payroll only from the finance network during business hours, and deny it at 02:00 or from a home IPv4 address even when the payroll role remains. Log the condition that failed, not a bare denial. Test the allow path in the middle of the morning from the finance network, and test the deny path after hours and from outside. State the time zone so 19:00 is the clinic clock. A role check would look only at membership and would have allowed the nighttime attempt. An attribute policy can weigh device health and data sensitivity together with place. A condition rule is the hour, the network, or both. Do not remove the role to fix a denial the role did not cause.',
        'r1': 'A rule looks at conditions on the request. The hour can be one condition. The network can be another. Both can be required together. The person may still hold the right job. The rule can refuse them anyway. The log should name the failed condition. Test a call that should succeed. Test a call that should fail. Do not delete the job to fix the clock. A role only says what the job is. A richer device check is a different policy. Write the time zone next to the hours.',
        'r2': 'A clinic door to the payroll room opens only on weekdays between the posted hours, and only if the person came through the finance hall. The badge can be a genuine payroll badge and the door still stays shut at night, or when the person arrives from the public lobby. The guard writes down which posted condition failed, so the next shift does not reissue the badge for no reason. A wardrobe that only checks which coat you wear is doing a simpler job. A guard who also inspects the laptop and the marking on the papers is doing a richer job. The posted hours and the required hall are the conditions. The coat is the role, and the bag inspection is a decision from several facts at once.',
        'r4': 'Pull the allow line and the deny line for the same account. Write down the role, the time, the source network, and the condition the denial names. Confirm the role did not change between the two attempts. Do not edit membership yet. Check the time zone on the rule against the clock in the log. Note whether a device policy also ran, and keep that result separate. Leave the rule enabled while you record the evidence. Test a fresh attempt only after the ticket says which condition you believe failed.',
        'exam': 'Conditions such as the hour or the source address, applied even when the role is present, are rule-based access control. An IPv4 address outside the finance network is one such condition, and 02:00 is another. Membership in the payroll role would have allowed the night attempt. A decision that also weighs device health and data sensitivity is broader than this rule. Removing the role is the wrong fix when the role was never absent. A sign-in window is one condition of this kind, not a second authentication factor.',
    },
    'Attribute-based access control': {
        'body': "Attribute-based access control decides from attributes available at the moment of the request. The attributes describe who is asking, what they are using, where they are, and how sensitive the data is. A doctor on a healthy clinic device can read a chart. The same account on a personal laptop cannot, even though the role never changed. The decision combines facts, and a change in one fact changes the outcome without a rewrite of the job.\n\nA policy names the attributes and the combination it requires. Subject attributes can include the job, the department, and whether the account is still active. Object attributes can include the sensitivity of the chart and whether this doctor should see this patient. Device and place attributes can include a managed laptop, disk encryption on, a clinic network, and the hour. The engine evaluates them together and should say which attribute tipped the result. A device flag that is always marked healthy, or a sensitivity mark nobody maintains, turns the policy into theater.\n\nFran is a doctor. At 11:20, from a clinic laptop that reports as managed with encryption on, Fran opens a chart. The decision log shows an allow: role doctor, device managed, sensitivity of the chart, and a patient relationship that is present. At 21:05 the same account tries from a personal laptop on a home network. The decision log shows a deny: role doctor still, device personal, encryption unknown. The role matches both times. The device attribute does not. Those two lines are the evidence, and the attribute names in them belong in the ticket. The laptop is the problem the policy was written to catch.\n\nDefenders list which attributes the policy actually reads, and they alert when an attribute feed goes stale. They log the deny with the failing attribute, not with a generic code. They test the same account on a managed device and on a personal one after every policy change. They resist collapsing the policy back into a role check merely because a role check is easier to explain. The reason for this model is the case the role gets wrong. A simple condition about hours or a source network still has a place when that really is the whole rule, and it should be named as that simpler rule. A role can be one attribute. It is not the entire policy.\n\nStudents flatten this into roles because the doctor role appears in the log, or into a condition rule because the place and the hour also appear. A role check would have allowed both of Fran's attempts, since the doctor role was present both times. A condition rule might say the finance network and the business hours, and stop there, without asking whether the device is managed or how sensitive the chart is. Here the deny cites the device. The separator is whether the outcome can change while the role stays the same, for a reason other than one posted condition such as the clock. If the personal laptop is the reason, the decision used attributes.",
        'r0': 'Evaluate the doctor role, the device, the place, and the sensitivity of the chart together. Allow a managed clinic laptop with encryption on. Deny the same account on a personal laptop, and log the device attribute as the reason. Do not remove the doctor role to fix that deny. A role-only check would allow both attempts. A single condition such as the hour or the finance network does not, by itself, describe device health. Test both devices after you change a policy. Alert when the feed that marks laptops as managed goes quiet, because a stale healthy flag makes every laptop look acceptable. The outcome should be able to change while the role stays put.',
        'r1': 'Several facts combine into one decision. The job can be one fact. The device can be another. The place and the data can matter too. The same person can be allowed, then refused. The job did not have to change. Log which fact caused the refusal. Test a clinic laptop and a personal laptop. Do not delete the job to punish the laptop. A role alone would have allowed both tries. A single clock rule is a narrower control. Keep the facts fresh or the decision lies.',
        'r2': 'A clinic door does not look only at the coat. The guard also looks at the bag, the hall the person walked from, and the marking on the folder they asked for. A doctor coat, a clinic bag, and a folder the doctor should see will pass. The same coat with a personal bag from the street will not, and the guard writes the bag as the reason rather than taking the coat away. A wardrobe that only issues coats is simpler. A sign that only lists hours is simpler still. The guard reading coat, bag, hall, and folder together is a decision from several facts. The coat by itself is the role, and the sign with the hours is a single posted condition.',
        'r4': 'Open the decision log for the allow and the later deny. Write down every attribute each line records, and mark the one that changed. Confirm the role is the same on both lines. Do not remove the role. Note the device identifier and whether it is managed. Check when the device feed last updated. Leave the policy text unchanged until those attributes are in the ticket. If the feed is stale, write that gap down before you edit the rule.',
        'exam': 'A decision that combines attributes, such as role, device health, place, and data sensitivity, is attribute-based access control. The same account allowed on a clinic laptop and denied on a personal one, with the role unchanged, fits this model. A role check would have stopped at the doctor role and allowed both. A rule that only checks the hour or the source network is narrower than this policy. Deleting the role does not fix a device attribute. A log that hides which attribute failed is not enough evidence to name the model.',
    },
    'Time-of-day restrictions': {
        'body': 'A time-of-day restriction limits when an account may sign in or reach a service. The front-desk account works from 07:00 to 19:00 and is refused at 02:00, even when the password is correct. The control is a schedule on the account or on the service. It does not ask for a second kind of proof, and it does not care whether the person is holding a phone or standing in the building. The clock is the whole test. Outside the window, the attempt ends for a time reason. That reason is what the log should show.\n\nThe schedule needs a time zone, a start, and an end. A window that says 19:00 with no zone will be read against whatever clock the server uses, and a person still inside the clinic afternoon can look wrongly refused. Days of the week belong in the same policy when weekends are closed. The denial should include the account, the server time, and the window. A success one minute inside the window, and a failure one minute outside it, prove the boundary is real. Holiday closures are extra dates. They are not a reason to delete the daily window.\n\nGabe uses the front-desk account. It is allowed from 07:00 to 19:00 in the clinic zone. At 18:40 the sign-in is allowed, and the log shows the time inside the window. At 02:14 someone tries the same account, and the log shows a deny, reason time, server time 02:14, window 07:00 to 19:00. No prompt for a phone or a fingerprint appears. The line does not mention a building or a network. It mentions the hour. Those fields are the ticket. If the 02:14 line instead shows only a bad password, with no schedule reason, you have not yet proved this control stopped the attempt.\n\nDefenders set the window to the real shift, include the zone, and test one minute inside and one minute outside after every change. They alert on a success outside the window, because that means the restriction is not bound to the account. They resist a personal exception that never expires. They do not describe the control as a factor. Turning it on does not meet a requirement for two different kinds of proof. A broader condition policy can place this schedule next to a network rule. When the only condition is the clock on one account, name the restriction for what it is.\n\nStudents call the 02:00 refusal a second factor, or they call it the factor for where you are. Two different factor types, such as a password and a phone, are a different control. A password together with a PIN would still be one type, and a clock is not a type on that list at all. Where you are is a place: the building or the network, not the hour. A correct password refused at 02:14 with a time reason is a schedule. It is not a second factor, and it is not a location check. The log shows a schedule reason, or it shows factor types. It should not be forced to wear both names.',
        'r0': 'Bind the front-desk account to 07:00 through 19:00 in the clinic time zone, and expect a deny at 02:14 that cites the schedule. Record the account, the server time, and the window on that deny. A success at 18:40 shows the inside of the window. An allow at 02:14 means the restriction is not applied. No second factor type is requested or checked, so this is not a multi-kind sign-in. The log cites the hour rather than a building or a network, so it is not a check of where the person is. A broader rule can add a source condition beside the clock. Test one minute inside and one minute outside after you edit the window, and do not leave an exception without an end date.',
        'r1': 'The account only works during its hours. A correct password can still be refused. The log should say the time was the reason. Write the hours and the time zone down. Test a minute inside the window. Test a minute outside the window. A success at night means the limit is missing. Do not call the clock a second proof. A phone prompt is a different control. A fingerprint is a different control. A building or a network is a place, not an hour. Keep weekend dates in the same note. Remove exceptions that have no end date.',
        'r2': 'A clinic locks the front-desk drawer except during the posted shift. At two in the morning the key still fits the lock, and the drawer stays shut because the schedule says so. The guard writes the hour on the slip, not a story about a missing badge or a wrong hallway. A second lock that needs both a memorized code and a token is a different door. A lock that opens only inside the building is about place. The posted shift on the drawer is only a clock. The code plus the token is two different kinds of proof, and the building lock is about where someone is standing.',
        'r4': 'Open the sign-in denial and write down the account, the server time, and the reason. Compare that time with the window on the account, including the time zone. Find a success inside the same window for contrast. Do not widen the window yet. Note whether any second prompt was offered. If none was offered, record a schedule rather than a second factor. Leave holiday dates unchanged until the ticket lists them. Capture the outside-hour deny before you test a new attempt.',
        'exam': 'A window such as 07:00 to 19:00, with a deny at 02:14 that names the schedule, is a time-of-day restriction. Calling that deny a second factor is the trap, because no second factor type was checked. Where you are is a place, and the hour is not a place. A password that is correct and still refused for time has missed the window. It has not failed as a secret. If the log shows a phone prompt or a fingerprint, you are looking at a factor, not at this schedule.',
    },
    'Multifactor authentication': {
        'body': 'Multifactor authentication requires two or more different kinds of proof, not two copies of the same kind. The kinds are something you know, something you have, something you are, and where you are. A password plus a prompt on a phone is two kinds: one you know, and one you have. A password plus a PIN is still only something you know, and it does not meet the requirement no matter how many boxes were on the screen. The count that matters is the count of types, not the count of fields.\n\nThe sign-in collects one proof and then a proof of a different type. The identity service checks both before it issues a session. A phone prompt, a hardware key, or a code from a device you hold counts as something you have. A fingerprint counts as something you are. A clinic network, or presence in the building, can count as where you are when the policy truly checks place and not merely the hour. People approve prompts they did not start. Codes are phished next to the password. A help desk removes the second factor after a weak phone call. The log should name the types, not merely say the sign-in was strong.\n\nBlair signs in to the clinic identity service at 07:50. The log shows a password accepted, then a phone prompt accepted, and then a session. That pair is two types. A second account, built as a favor, shows a password and a PIN, both accepted, and a session with no other proof. Both secrets are things a person can memorize. Someone who reads both off a note on a monitor has not been stopped by a second type. The two log lines are the pair to teach from: a phone prompt versus a PIN, with the types written beside them. The PIN session should not be called strong.\n\nDefenders accept only combinations that cross types, and they reject a PIN or a second password as the other factor when the first factor is already something the person knows. They protect a reset with a callback to a number already on file, so the second factor cannot be removed by an unchecked call to the help desk. They enroll a backup of a different type before a phone is lost. A window that refuses 02:00 is not a substitute. The account can be inside its hours and still have only one type of proof.\n\nStudents see two boxes and mark the control present. Two boxes of the same type are still one type. The neighboring topic is the list of kinds itself: know, have, are, and where you are. Naming those kinds is not the same as requiring two of them on a given account. A schedule is the other neighbor. Refusing the front desk at night does not add a factor. The separator sits on the log. If the types are a password and a PIN, the requirement has not been met. If the types are a password and a phone, or a fingerprint and a key, it has. Count the types, then decide.',
        'r0': 'Require two different factor types before a session is issued. A password plus a phone prompt is something known plus something held. A password plus a PIN is two things known, and the policy should refuse that pair. Log the type of each factor rather than a single flag that says strong. Alert when several phone prompts are approved in a short span. Reset the second factor only after a callback to the number already on file. A schedule that denies 02:00 does not add a factor type. Enroll a backup of a different type, such as a hardware key beside the phone, before the phone is lost. The session line should show the two types that actually passed.',
        'r1': 'Ask for two different kinds of proof. A password is something you know. A PIN is also something you know. Those two together are still one kind. A phone prompt is something you have. A fingerprint is something you are. A building check is where you are. The log should name each kind. Refuse a pair that stays inside memory. Guard the reset of the second proof. A night lock on the account is not a second kind. Enroll a spare method before the phone is lost.',
        'r2': 'A clinic drug cabinet opens only when the nurse both remembers a code and taps a token the pharmacy issued. Two codes would not do, because both live in memory, and a note on the wall would defeat them together. The token can be lost without the code becoming useless, and the code can be glimpsed without the token becoming useless. A drawer that simply stays locked after hours is not this cabinet. It never asked for a second kind of proof. The code plus the token is two different kinds. The after-hours drawer is only a schedule, and remembering two codes is still one kind of proof.',
        'r4': 'Open the sign-in record and write down each factor type that passed. If both are things the person knows, mark the session as one type. Do not call it a pass against the policy. Note whether a phone, a key, a fingerprint, or a place was involved. Do not reset any factor yet. Check the recent prompts for a burst of approvals. Confirm a reset would require a callback to the number on file. Leave the policy in place while you capture the types from the log.',
        'exam': 'Two different factor types are required. A password and a phone prompt qualify, because one is known and one is held. A password and a PIN do not qualify, because both are something you know. The kinds are know, have, are, and where you are. A sign-in window that refuses 02:00 is a schedule, not a factor. Prompt approvals the person did not intend are a failure of the held factor in practice, even when the policy names two types. Count the types on the log before you accept the control.',
    },
    'Something you know, have, are, or where you are': {
        'body': 'Proofs used at sign-in fall into four kinds: something you know, something you have, something you are, and where you are. A secret you remember is the first kind. A password, a passphrase, and a PIN all sit there, and a second one does not create a new kind. An object you possess is the second kind, such as a phone that receives a prompt, a hardware key, or a badge. A biometric you present is the third kind, such as a fingerprint or a face a reader measures. A place you are actually in is the fourth kind, such as the clinic building or the clinic network, checked as a location rather than assumed from the hour.\n\nTwo of the same kind do not become two factors, even when the screen has two fields. Two passwords are the first kind, repeated. A password and a PIN are the first kind, repeated. A fingerprint and a password cross kinds, because one is something you are and the other is something you know. A badge and a fingerprint cross kinds as well. Where you are has to be a real location check. A schedule that shuts the front desk at night is not a place, and it is not any of the four kinds. It is a clock wrapped around the account. Calling that clock a location files the fourth kind in the wrong place.\n\nBrooks stands at the medication cabinet. The cabinet asks for a fingerprint and a password, and the access log records both kinds: something you are, and something you know. The drawer opens. A web form elsewhere asks for a password and then a PIN, and its log records the first kind twice. That form never left memory. A third system checks that the workstation sits on the clinic network and records where, together with a hardware key that records something held. The lines to keep are the kind names, not a vendor adjective. If a line says only that the sign-in was strong, you do not yet know which kinds were used.\n\nDefenders label every enrolled method with its kind before they let it satisfy a two-kind rule. They refuse to enroll a PIN as a second kind beside a password. They pair a place with another kind rather than using place alone on a sensitive drawer. They do not reset a lost badge and a fingerprint reader after one casual call. Schedules and roles stay in other notes so they are not smuggled into this list.\n\nA PIN looks like a partner to a password, and it is the same kind. A night restriction looks like where you are, and it is not, because a person can stand in the building at 02:00 and still be refused for the hour, or be outside at noon and still be inside the hours. The demand for two kinds is a separate policy. This topic is the list that policy must draw from. Naming a kind does not mean an account uses two of them. Requiring two kinds does not change what a PIN is.',
        'r0': 'Classify each proof as something you know, something you have, something you are, or where you are. A password, a passphrase, and a PIN are the first. A phone prompt, a hardware key, and a badge are the second. A fingerprint is the third. A checked clinic network or building is the fourth. Two passwords remain one kind. A password paired with a PIN remains one kind. A fingerprint paired with a password is two kinds. A clock that refuses 02:00 is not a kind on this list. The demand to present two kinds is a separate policy. Write the kind beside every enrolled method so a later review does not have to guess from a product label.',
        'r1': 'Secrets you remember are one kind. A password and a PIN share that kind. Objects you hold are another kind. A phone and a badge fit there. A fingerprint is a kind of its own. A checked place is the last kind. Two secrets are still the first kind. A night schedule is not a place. Name the kind in the log. Do not trust a sticker that only says strong. Demanding two kinds is a separate policy. Sort the proof before you count it.',
        'r2': 'A clinic pharmacy sorts every opener onto four hooks: a code in your head, a token in your pocket, a measure of your finger, and a mark that you are standing in the room. Hanging two codes on the first hook does not fill two hooks. The night cover on the window is not a hook at all. It does not ask what you remember, what you hold, or who you are. It only asks what the clock says. Staff who must use two hooks have to pick from two different ones. The four hooks are the kinds of proof. The demand to use two hooks is the sign-in policy, and the window cover is only a schedule.',
        'r4': 'List every method enrolled on the account. Next to each one, write which of the four kinds it is. If two methods are both things the person knows, say that in the first line. Do not enroll anything new yet. Check whether a schedule is being described as a place, and split it out if it is. Note the words the log actually stored. Leave the required count unchanged until the kinds are written down. Bring the sorted list to the review rather than a vendor adjective.',
        'exam': 'The kinds are something you know, something you have, something you are, and where you are. A password and a PIN are the first kind twice, so they are not two factors. A fingerprint and a password are two kinds. Where you are is a place, such as the clinic network, and a time window is not that kind. The demand for two kinds is a separate rule that depends on this sorting. A log that says only that a sign-in was strong has not shown the kinds. Sort first, then count.',
    },
    'Password length, complexity, reuse, and age': {
        'body': 'Length matters more than forced complexity, and reuse across sites is how one theft becomes many. A rule that demands a symbol or a capital letter pushes people toward short patterns that still satisfy the checklist. A rule that demands a new secret every few weeks, with no suspected theft, pushes them toward a tiny edit of the old one. A long passphrase a person can remember beats a short secret with clever substitutions. Refusing the last several passwords matters when there is a real reason to change. Changing for show does not.\n\nGuessing grows much harder as the secret grows, while a symbol rule is easy to meet with a predictable shape. Reuse matters because a password stolen from a weaker site will be tried on the clinic next. The directory should store a salted hash, not the password itself. SHA-256 is the hash to name when you mean a general hash, and a slow password hash is what belongs on an account store, because a fast hash makes guessing cheap. The policy a person feels is the length, the history, and whether you force a rotation. A short complex password is not stronger than a long simple one.\n\nAisha sets the clinic account policy. The minimum length is 15. Rules that demanded a symbol are off. History remembers the last 5 passwords. There is no maximum age of 14 days. A suspected theft still forces a change, and history blocks a quick return to the exposed value. Her own passphrase is several ordinary words and clears the length. A short pattern that includes a symbol and a digit fails the length check. The evidence is the policy screen: length 15, history 5, maximum age not set, and the rejected short secret in the test log. Sticky notes with a new final digit every two weeks were the old habit, and they left with the 14-day rule.\n\nDefenders set length high enough that people choose a passphrase, block reuse with history and with a check against passwords already known to be stolen, and rotate when there is a reason. A theft, a person leaving a shared account, or a real suspicion is a reason. Looking busy on a two-week timer is not. They offer a store of unique secrets so each site can have its own long value without asking anyone to memorize a pile. They do not pretend the store removes typed secrets. A device or a biometric can replace the typed secret.\n\nStudents answer with complexity when the question is about strength, or with a short expiry when the question is about care. Length beats a forced symbol. Age without a reason produces weaker secrets. Reuse is the bridge from a breach somewhere else into this clinic. A store of unique secrets makes a different secret per site practical. Authentication that uses a device or a biometric, instead of a typed secret, leaves nothing to lengthen, reuse, or age. The separator is whether a memorized secret still exists. If it does, make it long, do not reuse it, and do not rotate it for show.',
        'r0': 'Set a minimum length of 15 and prefer a passphrase over a short secret that only contains a symbol. Keep a history of the last 5 passwords so a change cannot hop straight back to an exposed value. Do not set a maximum age of 14 days. Change a password when there is a reason, such as a theft or someone leaving a shared account. Store a salted hash. Teach SHA-256 as the general hash, and use a slow password hash on the account store. Treat a password seen in a public breach list as already burned. A store of one unique secret per site addresses reuse. A device or a biometric, with no typed secret, is a different design and is not a length setting.',
        'r1': 'Long secrets resist guessing better than clever short ones. A symbol rule does not replace length. People write down secrets they must change too often. Change a secret when you suspect theft. Do not change it on a timer for show. Refuse the last few secrets so they cannot return. Do not reuse one secret on many sites. A theft elsewhere will be tried here next. Store a hash, not the secret itself. A locked book of unique secrets helps. A device or a biometric can replace a typed secret. That replacement is a different design.',
        'r2': 'A clinic chooses a long phrase for the drug-room door rather than a short code with an odd character in the middle. The long phrase is harder to guess, and the staff can remember it without a sticky note. Forcing a brand new phrase every other week made people change one letter and write the rest on the door frame. Using the same phrase on the drug room, the side door, and a personal locker meant one overheard phrase opened all three. A locked book with a different phrase for each door stops that repetition. A door that opens with a badge or a fingerprint, and no phrase at all, has left phrases behind. The long phrase is the length rule. The locked book stores unique secrets, and the badge or fingerprint replaces the typed secret.',
        'r4': 'Open the account policy and write down the minimum length, the history count, and whether a maximum age is set. Do not change the numbers yet. Note any rule that requires a symbol or a capital letter. Find one rejected attempt and record whether length or history caused it. Ask whether the last clinic-wide change had a reason or only a date. Leave the hash settings untouched while you record them. Say whether a secret store or a device sign-in is also in use. Bring the settings to review before you edit the policy.',
        'exam': 'Length matters more than forced complexity. A short secret with a symbol is still short. Reuse across sites turns one theft into many. A maximum age of two weeks, with no suspected theft, often produces weaker secrets. History that blocks the last few values helps when a change has a reason. A store of unique secrets does not, by itself, remove the typed secret. A device or a biometric, used instead of a typed secret, is a different design. If an answer praises frequent forced changes over length, it has the priority backward.',
    },
    'Password managers': {
        'body': 'A password manager stores unique secrets so a person does not reuse one password on the chart, on mail, and on a dozen outside sites. Each entry holds a site, an account name, and a secret long enough to meet a length rule. The person remembers how to unlock the store, not twenty site secrets. That unlock is now the valuable secret, so it should be a long passphrase plus a second factor of a different type. A spreadsheet on a shared drive is not this tool. It has no serious unlock boundary and no record of who copied a secret.\n\nThe store encrypts its contents. AES-256 is the shared-key cipher for that bulk protection, with the key derived from the unlock secret. Entries copy through an account of their own, and that account needs the same care as the store. The application fills the matching secret into the site. A generated secret should differ for every site. If two entries match, the store is being used as a notebook of a reused password, and the main benefit is gone.\n\nAhmed opens the clinic-approved store at the start of a shift. The unlock is a long passphrase plus a prompt on a phone. The store shows separate entries for the chart, for mail, and for a lab portal, and the secrets are not the same. A fill into the lab portal uses only the lab entry. The store log shows the unlock at 08:02 with two factor types, and it does not show the site secrets in the clear. An older shared sheet in the team folder lists one secret for three sites, with no second factor, and the file is readable by the whole department. The contrast belongs on the ticket: unique entries under a two-type unlock, versus one reused secret in an open sheet.\n\nDefenders approve one store, forbid the spreadsheet, and require the unlock to be long and backed by a different factor type. They revoke the store account the day the person leaves, and they rotate any secret that person could see. They watch for the store account signing in from a new address, because that account is the key to the rest. Length and reuse rules still matter, because the store is how unique secrets stay practical. A design with no typed secret, using a device or a biometric instead, removes the need to keep those site secrets. It does not describe this store.\n\nStudents call the store passwordless because the person no longer types each site secret. The person still has a typed unlock, and the sites still have secrets. Authentication with a device or a biometric, instead of a typed secret, leaves nothing in a store for an attacker to read. Length and reuse rules say how to choose a secret. This store makes a unique secret per site practical. The separator is whether unique typed secrets still exist. If they do, guard the store and the unlock. If the sign-in is a hardware key or a biometric, with no memorized site secret and no memorized unlock, you are in the other design.',
        'r0': 'Store a unique secret per site in a manager whose vault uses AES-256 and opens with a long passphrase plus a second factor type. Generate site secrets to a length of at least 15, and reject a vault where two sites share one secret. A shared spreadsheet is not a substitute. Revoke the manager account on the last day and rotate secrets that account could reveal. Watch unlocks from a new address. Length policy tells you how a secret should be chosen. A device or a biometric, used instead of a typed secret, leaves no vault of site secrets to steal. The manager still holds those secrets. Guard the unlock the way you would guard a master key.',
        'r1': 'Keep a different long secret for each site. Put those secrets in a locked store. Unlock the store with a long phrase. Add a second kind of proof on that unlock. Do not keep the secrets in a shared sheet. A sheet the whole team can open is not locked. Generate a secret instead of reusing one. Remove the store account when the person leaves. Change secrets that person was able to see. Watch for an unlock from a new address. Length rules still apply to the unlock. A device or a biometric can avoid a typed secret. That path keeps no site secrets in a store.',
        'r2': 'A clinic pharmacist keeps a locked book of different cupboard codes, one code per cupboard, and remembers only how to open the book. The book is useless to a stranger who lacks the phrase and the token on the phone. A paper list taped inside a shared drawer, with the same code on every line, is how the last incident started. Throwing the codes away and opening each cupboard with a badge or a fingerprint means there is no book to steal. The locked book is a store of unique secrets. The badge or the fingerprint, with no code left to type, is authentication that no longer depends on those secrets.',
        'r4': 'Open the store policy for the person, not the site secrets themselves. Write down whether the unlock uses a long passphrase and a second factor type. Confirm two sites do not share one secret, using the site names rather than by copying secrets into the ticket. Do not rotate anything yet. Note whether a shared sheet still holds the same accounts. Check the last unlock time and the address it came from. Leave the store intact until the ticket records the unlock method and the sheet.',
        'exam': 'A password manager stores unique secrets, and the unlock should be a long passphrase plus a second factor type. It is not authentication by a device or a biometric in place of a typed secret. That other design leaves no site secret to store. Length and reuse rules still explain how a secret should be chosen. The manager is where the unique copies live. A spreadsheet of one reused secret is not a manager. Theft of the vault is theft of every entry.',
    },
    'Passwordless authentication': {
        'body': 'Signing in without a memorized secret means the server never stores a password it can leak. Passwordless authentication does that with a device the person holds, or with a body trait that unlocks a key on that device. The authenticator creates a key pair at enrollment and keeps the private key. The server stores only the public key. At sign-in the server sends a fresh challenge, the private key signs that challenge, and the server accepts the signature when it matches the stored public key. A fingerprint or a short PIN may be required before the key will sign, but that check stays on the device.\n\nEnrollment binds the public key to the account, and that binding should happen only after staff have confirmed who the person is. A hardware key on a badge and a second key locked in a safe give two ways back in. If the badge key is lost, staff revoke that one public key the same day and the person uses the spare. Recovery that mails a temporary password puts a memorized secret back into a design built to remove one. A look-alike site has a harder time as well, because the key signs a challenge only for the site it was enrolled with.\n\nPriya signs in to the clinic chart system with the security key on her badge. The sign-in log at 08:12 names authenticator A4F2, records success, and shows no password check beside it. The spare for her account is item 18 on the safe log, present in the safe, not mailed. On Tuesday she leaves the badge in a taxi. The help desk revokes authenticator A4F2 at 09:40. A test with that key fails, and a test with the spare succeeds. The evidence on the ticket is a revoke event, a failed assertion from the old key, and a success from the spare.\n\nDefenders enroll two keys before they turn the password off for that account, and they practice the spare-key path before a real loss. They read recovery reports for any path that still issues a memorized secret. They revoke on the day of the loss and they test the refusal. They begin with the central login and with admin actions, where a reused password would open the most doors. A one-time code in a text message is a weaker cousin. A person can be tricked into reading that code aloud. The key that will not sign for the wrong site is the stronger object.\n\nStudents mix this control up with a password manager and with rules about length, reuse, and age. A manager stores a different memorized secret for each site. Length rules talk about those secrets. Sign-in without a password has no memorized secret to lengthen, reuse, or age out. What separates them is the check the server performs: a signature from a key it enrolled, not a password hash. If the question still has a stored secret a person could type, the topic is the manager or the password policy, even when another factor sits beside that secret.',
        'r0': 'Enroll two keys, one for daily use and a spare, before the password is turned off. FIDO2 is the usual standard. The authenticator keeps the private key, the server stores the public key, and sign-in is a signed challenge rather than a password hash. Keep the spare in a safe and record its identifier, such as safe item 18. Revoke a lost key the same day and test that an assertion from it fails. Do not mail a temporary password as recovery. Confirm the person before enrollment, for example with a callback to a number already on file. Prefer the key over a numeric code a person can read to an attacker. Watch recovery reports for any path that still issues a memorized secret. Start with the central login and with admin actions.',
        'r1': 'People can sign in without a memorized secret.\nA device holds the private key.\nThe server keeps only the public key.\nSign-in is a signed challenge.\nA fingerprint only unlocks the key on the device.\nEnroll a spare key and lock it in a safe.\nRevoke a lost key the same day.\nTest that the lost key is refused.\nDo not mail a password as the backup.\nA password manager still stores secrets.\nLength rules are about those stored secrets.\nThis server checks a signature instead.\nA fake page should not receive a valid signature.\nStart with the main login and with admin actions.',
        'r2': 'A clinic door opens when a person presents a carved token the door already knows. A second token for that person stays in the safe. There is no spoken phrase on a card that would also open the pharmacy down the street. If the badge token is lost, the desk voids that carving the same day and tests that the door stays shut. The spare from the safe still works. A book of different phrases, one per door, is still a book of phrases. Rules about how long a phrase must be are rules about phrases.\n\nThe carved token is the device key that signs the person in, and the spare in the safe is the recovery path. A password manager and a length rule still depend on a memorized secret, which this door does not use.',
        'r4': 'Look at the enrolled keys for one admin account. Write down how many keys are enrolled and where the spare is stored. Do not turn the password off yet. Confirm the person was checked before enrollment. Revoke a test key on a lab account and write down whether sign-in with it fails. Do not mail a backup password as part of the drill. Note whether a fake page could collect a typed code instead. Leave production recovery paths unchanged until the lab refusal works.',
        'exam': 'The trap is a password manager or a rule about length, reuse, and age. A manager stores unique memorized secrets. Length rules talk about those secrets. Passwordless sign-in stores a public key and checks a signature. There is no memorized secret to reuse or to age out. Recovery is a spare key in a safe, not a mailed password. A fingerprint that only unlocks a key on the device is not a second password. If the answer still depends on a secret a person can type, it is the wrong topic.',
    },
    'Just-in-time privilege': {
        'body': 'Admin that sits on an account all year is standing privilege, and a stolen account then has admin immediately. Just-in-time privilege grants the powerful right for a short window and then removes it. The right expires when the window ends, even if the person forgets to click anything and even if the ticket is still open. Expiry is the control. A promise to remove the right later is not expiry. Good intentions do not shorten the time an attacker has if that account is stolen tonight.\n\nThe grant names a right, a system, and a reason. A person who approves, or a written policy, allows it. The directory adds the account to the privileged group, or the platform opens a privileged session, and a timer starts. When the timer ends, membership is removed or the session is cut. The log should name who asked, who approved, which system, the start, and the end. A grant without that record is a quiet admin add. Scope belongs in the grant. Admin on one server is not admin on every server unless the request said so and someone accepted that wide reach.\n\nNora needs to replace a certificate on one clinic server. The ticket allows local administrator on that host for two hours. At 10:05 her account enters the administrators group on that host. At 12:05 automation removes the membership. A directory check at 12:10 shows the account gone. The evidence is two membership events, two hours apart, and an empty group afterward. If the account is still in the group the next morning, the control failed even though the certificate was fixed. Finished repair and a removed right are different events.\n\nDefenders make expiry automatic, and they alarm when a grant outlives its window. They keep an emergency account sealed, and they alarm any use of it, so nobody widens the everyday window just in case. They review these grants the way they review ordinary role membership. They do not leave Nora in the admin group between jobs to make the next job faster. The timed path is what makes the next job faster, because the right exists only while the work is approved. A standing membership left for convenience is the failure this control exists to prevent.\n\nStudents confuse an expiring admin right with a secret that dies soon, and with a vault that checks a shared password out and writes who took it. An expiring right, a short-lived token, and a checkout log are three different objects, and a shop may use all three on the same change. What separates them is the object that disappears. If the question says the right is gone when the window ends, the answer is the expiring privilege, not the token and not the checkout record.',
        'r0': "Grant local administrator on the named host for two hours, and remove it when the timer ends. Log who asked, who approved, the host, the start, and the end. A directory read ten minutes after the window should show the account gone from that host's administrators group. Alarm if the membership outlives the window. Do not widen a one-host grant to every server. Keep a sealed emergency account, and alarm its use, instead of leaving standing admin in place. The privilege expires even if the person forgets. This is not a fifteen-minute token, and it is not a vault checkout of a shared password. Standing admin left for speed is the failure mode.",
        'r1': 'Give admin rights for a short window only.\nTake the right away when the window ends.\nThe right expires even if someone forgets.\nDo not wait for the ticket to close.\nLimit the right to the one system named.\nWrite down who asked and who approved.\nWrite the start time and the end time.\nCheck the group after the window closes.\nAlarm if the person is still an admin.\nDo not leave admin rights on between jobs.\nKeep a sealed account for real emergencies.\nA secret that dies quickly is different.\nA checkout log for a shared password is different.',
        'r2': "A warehouse issues a visitor badge that opens one cage and goes dark at noon, whether or not the visitor remembers to hand it back. Yesterday's badge does not open the cage this morning. The desk writes who asked, who agreed, which cage, and both times. A badge that opens every cage, because copying the master was faster, is a wider and worse choice. A sealed badge in a breakable box waits for a fire, and opening the box rings a bell. Finishing the repair does not keep the badge alive past noon.\n\nIn the directory, that badge is the temporary admin right and noon is the moment the right expires. A secret that simply stops working soon is a different object, and a log of who borrowed a shared combination is a different object again.",
        'r4': 'Look at one admin grant that should already have ended. Write down the host, the start time, and the end time. Check whether the account is still in the administrators group. Do not remove a different role yet. If the account is still present, record that expiry failed. Note whether the grant covered one host or every host. Leave the sealed emergency account untouched. Do not turn the timed right into a standing membership to save time.',
        'exam': 'Just-in-time privilege expires. Admin is granted for a window, such as two hours on one server, and then removed even if the person forgets and even if the ticket is still open. A right still present the next morning means expiry failed. This is not a short-lived token. This is not a vault checkout that records who took a shared password. Standing membership left in place for convenience is the trap. One server in the request is not every server. A sealed emergency account is not the daily path.',
    },
    'Password vaulting': {
        'body': "Shared admin secrets do not belong in a chat thread, a spreadsheet, or a script someone will copy later. Password vaulting puts those secrets in a vault that checks them out to a named person, writes an audit log, and can rotate them afterward. The vault is the source of truth for the database password, the firewall admin password, and any other secret more than one person must use. A personal password manager is a locked book of one person's website passwords. The vault in this topic is a controlled store of shared privileged secrets, with a record of who took each one.\n\nCheckout is the working step. Jordan opens a change ticket for the clinic database. The vault requires that ticket, then reveals the database password or places it into the session so Jordan does not paste it into chat. The audit line records Jordan, the secret name, the ticket number, and the time. When the change ends, the vault rotates the password and the application picks up the new value. A copy written on a sticky note dies at rotation. Failed attempts to open the vault are logged too, because guessing at the vault matters as much as a successful checkout.\n\nThe strongest secrets need a second person to approve the checkout. A checkout should expire so the secret is not left on a workstation overnight. Where the tool can place the secret into the session, Jordan never sees the characters and cannot paste them into a message. Service accounts that still need a long-lived password belong in the same vault, checked out by a job rather than held in human memory. Changing the vault's own rules should take two people, so one insider cannot both rewrite the rules and hide the log.\n\nDefenders search for the same secret outside the vault, in old tickets, in scripts, and in mail. They alarm a secret that missed its rotation date. They remove a departed person's right to check anything out on the last day. They review every emergency retrieval, because a break-glass path used every week is no longer an emergency path. They do not email the secret as the delivery method. They treat the vault host as a high value system, with its own backups and its own tight login list.\n\nStudents call any locked password store by this name. A manager that keeps unique secrets for one person does not record a shared checkout, and it belongs to a different topic. A token minted for a few minutes, never stored as a shared password, belongs to another topic again. What separates them is the shared admin secret, the audit log of who took it, and the rotation after use. If there is no shared secret and no checkout record, this control is not the one in front of you.",
        'r0': "Store shared admin secrets in a vault and write an audit log on every checkout. Jordan's checkout of the database password should name Jordan, the ticket, and the time. Rotate that secret when the change ends, and confirm the application picked up the new value. Expire the checkout so the secret does not remain on a workstation past the task. Require a second person for the strongest secrets. Alarm a secret that missed its rotation date. Remove checkout rights on the day a person leaves. Review every emergency retrieval. A personal manager of website passwords is not this vault. A token that lives fifteen minutes is not this vault either.",
        'r1': 'Keep shared admin secrets in a vault.\nWrite an audit log for each checkout.\nRecord who took the secret.\nRecord the ticket and the time.\nRotate the secret after the change.\nDo not leave the secret in a chat.\nLet the checkout session expire.\nHide the secret when the tool allows it.\nRequire two people for the strongest secrets.\nAlarm a rotation that is late.\nRemove access when a person leaves.\nReview every emergency retrieval.\nA personal password book is a different tool.\nA secret that dies in minutes is different too.\nSearch for copies that escaped the vault.',
        'r2': 'In an office building the master key to the utility rooms lives in a lockbox, not on a sticky note in the lobby. A worker signs that key out against a work order, and the page shows the name, the order, and the hour. When the job ends, the lock is changed so a copy made yesterday fails. The strongest rooms need a second person to agree before the box opens. If someone leaves the building, that name comes off the list the same day. A personal key ring in one pocket is not this box.\n\nThe lockbox is the vault for shared admin secrets, and the sign-out page is the audit log. A token that dies in minutes was never a master key kept for the next worker.',
        'r4': 'Look at the vault entry for one shared database password. Write down the last checkout, including who, which ticket, and when. Do not rotate the secret yet. Check whether the audit log shows failed opens as well as successes. Note any copy of that secret in a ticket or a script, and leave those copies in place until you have a plan. Confirm a departed person cannot check the secret out. Leave the emergency retrieval rules unchanged while you read them.',
        'exam': 'The trap is a personal password manager, or a token that lives only minutes. Password vaulting stores shared admin secrets and writes an audit log of who checked them out. Rotation after use kills a copied secret. A manager keeps unique secrets for one person and does not record a shared checkout. A short-lived credential is minted and then dies. It is not a shared password waiting in a vault. No audit line of a checkout means this control is not what you are looking at.',
    },
    'Ephemeral credentials': {
        'body': "A credential that lives for months is still useful to whoever stole it last week. Ephemeral credentials are short-lived, so a stolen copy dies when the clock runs out. A cloud job receives a token that lasts minutes, not a permanent key written into a file or a script. The job proves its identity to a token service. The service mints a token scoped to one action and one audience. The job presents that token. When the minutes end, the service rejects the token even if a thief still holds the string.\n\nScope keeps a short life from still being unlimited power. The token that restarts one queue should not also read every storage bucket and change every account. Minting should happen only after the caller is authenticated, and the mint record should name which job asked. The token string should stay out of the job log, because a log copy is a stolen copy with a wider audience. A test should present an expired token and record a refusal. If the expired token still works, the lifetime is only a label on a long-lived secret.\n\nOn Thursday the billing job asks for a token at 14:02. The token record shows audience billing-deploy, permission to update one service, and expiry at 14:17. The job log shows the work finished at 14:09 and does not contain the token string. At 14:20 a replay of that same token fails. The evidence is a fifteen-minute lifetime, a narrow permission, and a rejection after expiry. A permanent key that had been sitting in a config file is the object this pattern replaces.\n\nDefenders count permanent keys still sitting in scripts and config files, and they replace those keys with a mint-and-expire pattern, starting with build jobs and workload identities. They plan how to stop the signer that mints tokens, because a stolen signer can create new short tokens until it is stopped. They do not use this name for a password that expires in ninety days. Ninety days is a long life. Minutes, or a single task, is the range that makes a stolen copy die quickly. They also keep the token's permission as small as the task.\n\nStudents mix a short-lived secret with a vaulted password and with a timed admin right. A vault holds a shared secret until someone rotates it. A timed admin right removes a privilege and may leave the underlying password permanent. An ephemeral credential is minted for a short life and then fails on its own. What separates them is the lifetime of the secret itself. If the secret remains valid until a person rotates it, it is not short-lived, whatever window surrounds the privilege.",
        'r0': 'Mint a token for a cloud job that lasts fifteen minutes, scoped to one service and one action, and do not write a permanent key into a file. If the token was minted at 14:02, a test at 14:20 should show it rejected. Keep the token string out of the job log. Log which job asked for the mint. Replace permanent keys found in config files. Do not describe a ninety-day password as short-lived. Plan how to revoke the signer that mints tokens, because new tokens can appear until that signer is stopped. A vaulted password lives until someone rotates it. A two-hour admin right can end while the secret underneath stays valid. Here the credential itself dies.',
        'r1': 'A stolen secret should stop working quickly.\nThese credentials last only a short time.\nA job receives a token for minutes, not months.\nThat token is not a permanent key in a file.\nLimit the token to one small action.\nDo not print the token in the job log.\nReject the token after it expires.\nTest that refusal on purpose.\nReplace permanent keys found in files.\nA ninety-day password is not short-lived.\nA vaulted password waits until someone rotates it.\nA timed admin right is a different object.\nThe secret itself is what expires here.\nRecord which job asked for the token.',
        'r2': 'A kitchen hands out a tasting pass that is good for one dish and is torn up when that dish leaves the pass. A stolen pass cannot be used at dinner. The pass names one station, not every cooler in the building. The pass is not written on the menu where every guest can read it. A house key hidden in a jar is a different and worse idea, because it still works next month. A combination written in a book and changed only when someone remembers is different again.\n\nThe tasting pass is a short-lived credential, and tearing it up is expiry. The house key is a permanent secret, and the remembered combination is a shared password that lives until someone changes it.',
        'r4': 'Look at one cloud job that updates a service. Write down the token lifetime and the permission it carries. Do not replace a permanent key yet. Check whether the job log contains the token string. Note any permanent key still sitting in a config file. Test an expired token only in a lab, and write down the refusal. Leave the minting signer in place while you read. Do not label a ninety-day password as short-lived in your notes.',
        'exam': 'The trap is a vaulted password, or admin rights loaned for a window while the secret stays permanent. Ephemeral credentials are short-lived. A stolen copy dies when they expire, in minutes or at the end of one task. A password that remains until someone rotates it is not short-lived, even if a vault holds it. A privilege window can end while the underlying secret stays valid. A ninety-day password is a long life. If the secret is still accepted tomorrow, it was not ephemeral.',
    },
    'Automated user provisioning': {
        'body': "Accounts should appear when a person is hired and stop working when the person leaves, without a hallway favor in between. Automated user provisioning creates, changes, and disables accounts from a trusted source, usually the staffing system, so access follows hiring and leaving. The staffing record is the source of truth. A job code maps to a set of roles. When the staffing office marks someone departed, the directory account disables the same day, and the badge can follow the same feed. Nobody has to remember a separate cleanup at the end of the quarter.\n\nThe mechanism is a feed, not a mood. The staffing system sends a hire, a transfer, or a departure. An integration account, itself a valuable secret, applies that event to the directory and to the applications that accept the feed. Each directory change should record the source event that caused it. A rehire is a new decision, not a silent return of the old admin groups. If a required field is missing, the feed should wait and alarm rather than invent access. If the feed stops arriving, that silence is an incident, because departures are no longer being applied.\n\nLuis starts as a nurse on Monday. The staffing record is completed at 16:00 on Sunday. At 07:10 on Monday the directory shows the nurse role and not a copy of a manager's groups. The cause is the staffing event, not a chat note that said to copy Dana. Three months later the staffing office sets his status to departed at 15:00. By 15:20 the account is disabled, the badge is void, and remote access is refused. The evidence is a disable event whose cause field names the staffing record, on the same calendar day.\n\nDefenders practice three lab events before they trust the feed: a hire, a transfer, and a departure. They measure the lag from the staffing time to the directory change and alarm when it slips past the same day. They review the job-code map when roles change, so an old code does not keep granting a privilege the job no longer has. They keep the integration account in the vault. They write down which attributes the staffing system owns and which attributes stay local. They keep a manual path for the day the source record is wrong, and they log every use of that path.\n\nStudents confuse this feed with a ticket that only escalates an alert, and with a rule that refuses a bad configuration. A ticket tracks work that already happened. A guard rail blocks an unsafe shape before it is built. This control changes accounts because hiring and leaving changed. What separates them is the trusted source. If a person still types each account by hand from a one-time request, the provisioning is manual, even when that request has a perfectly good ticket number.",
        'r0': 'Drive account create, change, and disable from the staffing system. SCIM is one protocol that carries those events to the directory. When staffing marks a person departed, disable the directory account the same day and void the badge on the same feed. Map each job code to roles and review the map when jobs change. Treat a rehire as a new grant, not as a return of old admin groups. Alarm if the feed stops. Practice a hire, a transfer, and a departure in a lab. Log any downstream application that rejects the change. Keep the integration account in the vault. A hand-typed account from a chat request is manual work, not this feed.',
        'r1': "Let hiring and leaving drive the accounts.\nUse the staffing system as the source.\nCreate the account when the person starts.\nDisable the account the day they leave.\nMap the job to the right roles.\nDo not copy a manager's groups by habit.\nTreat a return to work as a new decision.\nAlarm if the staffing feed stops arriving.\nPractice a hire, a move, and a departure.\nProtect the account that applies the feed.\nLog any system that rejects a change.\nA hand-typed favor is not this feed.\nAn alert ticket does not hire or remove people.\nA rule that blocks a bad setup is different too.",
        'r2': "A clinic roster opens a locker on the morning a nurse is hired and closes that locker on the afternoon the roster says the nurse left. The charge nurse does not have to remember a separate cleanup next quarter. A move to a new ward changes the locker and does not keep the old ward's keys out of kindness. If the roster page fails to arrive, the office rings an alarm, because departures would sit undone. A name typed in from a hallway favor is not on this roster. A note about a broken freezer is not a hiring event.\n\nThe roster is the staffing feed that creates and disables accounts. A ticket about an alert, and a rule that refuses an unsafe build, do not hire people or take their keys away.",
        'r4': "Look at one departure recorded by the staffing system this month. Write down the staffing time and the directory disable time. Do not disable extra accounts yet. Check whether the badge stopped working on the same day. Note the role map for that job and whether a rehire would regain old groups. Leave the integration account's rights unchanged while you list them. If the feed has a gap, write the dates and do not fill the gap by hand yet.",
        'exam': 'The trap is a hand-typed account, a guard rail, or an alert ticket. Automated provisioning creates and disables accounts from the staffing source. A departure in that source disables access the same day, without a quarterly cleanup. A guard rail refuses an unsafe configuration. Ticket escalation records an alert and calls someone. A one-time chat request is manual provisioning even if it has a ticket number. The trusted hiring and leaving feed is the fact that separates this control.',
    },
    'Guard rails and security groups': {
        'body': "A template that will happily build an unsafe system will build that unsafe system every time, and it will do so at machine speed. Guard rails stop a configuration that breaks a rule before that configuration becomes real. A security group is a different control that often shows up in the same design. It limits which addresses and which ports may talk to a resource. The rail judges the shape of what someone is about to deploy. The security group is the network allow list on the running resource. Both belong in the design, and one word should not be used for both ideas.\n\nThe rail is usually a policy attached to the template or to the cloud account. Someone asks for a storage bucket. The template inspects the setting that would make the bucket readable by anyone on the internet. The apply fails, and the error names the rule. Nothing is created. A security group works later, when a connection is attempted. The database group allows traffic on port 5432 from the app tier only. A laptop on the guest network can try that port and gets no session. The group does not explain why a bucket was public. The rail does not, by itself, filter packets that are already in flight.\n\nOn Wednesday a new project tries to ship a bucket with public read turned on. The pipeline stops on rule number 12, and no bucket id appears in the inventory. The same afternoon the database security group is read line by line. It shows one inbound entry: port 5432, source the app tier, and no entry for every address on the internet. The evidence is a failed deploy that names the rule, beside a security group with a single narrow inbound line. A line that allows 0.0.0.0/0 on port 5432 means the group is wide open, whatever the template claims on paper.\n\nDefenders read both places. They read the template rules that refuse public storage, open admin ports, and logging that has been switched off. They read the live security groups for any line that allows the whole internet, and they write down the ticket that justified a wide line. They do not delete a live line in the middle of an outage until they know which service uses it. They do not weaken the template so today's deploy can succeed and the rail can be fixed later. Later is how the unsafe shape becomes the usual shape.\n\nThe exam trap treats a security group as a directory group of people, or treats a guard rail as if it were the packet filter itself. A directory group grants rights to accounts. A security group, in this topic, limits who or what can talk on the network. A guard rail refuses a configuration that breaks a written rule, at the moment the configuration is applied. A firewall rule is yet another object, because it filters traffic, often on a different device. The moment each control acts is the fact that separates them.",
        'r0': 'Make the template refuse a storage bucket that anyone on the internet could read, and fail the apply before the bucket exists. Rule number 12 should name public storage as refused. On the database, allow port 5432 only from the app tier security group. A source of 0.0.0.0/0 on that port means the group is open to the world. Write down any wide line and the ticket that justified it. Do not delete a live line during an outage until you know the service that uses it. A guard rail acts when configuration is applied. A security group acts when a connection is attempted. A directory group of people is neither control.',
        'r1': 'Stop a bad setup before it gets built.\nA template can refuse a public store.\nThe build should fail instead of creating it.\nName the rule that refused the setup.\nA security group limits who can connect.\nAllow only the app tier to reach the data.\nDo not open the data store to everyone.\nA directory group of people is different.\nThe rail acts when a setup is applied.\nThe group acts when traffic shows up.\nDo not delete a live line during an outage.\nWrite down why any wide line exists.\nFixing the rule later often means never.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse template refuses a crate stamped as open to the street, and the crane will not set that crate in the aisle. The refusal happens before the crate has a place on the floor. Separately, the bay gate lets trucks from the app bay through and turns other trucks away. The gate list is not the same object as the template refusal. A list of names on the break room door is a third object. During a traffic jam, nobody unscrews the gate until they know which delivery still needs to enter.\n\nThe template refusal is a guard rail on a bad configuration. The bay gate is a security group that limits which callers can reach the resource.',
        'r4': 'Look at the template rule that should refuse public storage. Write down the rule name and whether a test apply fails closed. Do not weaken the rule yet. Open the database security group and write down each inbound source. Note any line that allows the whole internet. Do not delete that line yet. Keep those lines separate from directory groups of people. Leave live traffic rules in place until you know which service uses each one.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to mix a guard rail, a security group, and a directory group of people. A guard rail stops a configuration that breaks a rule, such as a storage bucket the whole internet can read, and it acts when the setup is applied. A security group limits who or what can talk, such as only the app tier on the database port. A directory group grants rights to accounts. Neither object is a firewall merely because it can say no.',
    },
    'Ticket creation and escalation': {
        'body': 'An alert that lives only in a chat scroll can vanish while the person on duty is asleep. Ticket creation and escalation turn that alert into tracked work with an owner and a clock. Automation opens a ticket when the rule fires, copies the evidence into the ticket, and names a queue. If nobody accepts the ticket within the agreed time, escalation reaches the next person. The page is the signal. The ticket is the record. Escalation is the timer that moves the record when the first owner does not take it.\n\nThe mechanism is a rule on the alert, not a person copying text by hand. A high-severity event matches. Automation creates the ticket, sets the severity, pastes the relevant log lines, and assigns the queue. The accept clock starts at creation. At fifteen minutes with no accept, the rule pages the duty phone and writes that page into the ticket. Duplicate alerts for the same outage should attach to the open ticket rather than spawn a crowd of copies. The first action the responder should take is written on the ticket, so a tired person is not inventing a plan at 02:00.\n\nAt 02:14 a failed-login rule crosses fifty failures in five minutes on the remote access gateway. Ticket 4419 opens with those counts, the source addresses, and the identity queue. At 02:14 the owner field is empty. At 02:29 nobody has accepted it, and the ticket gains a line that the duty phone was paged. Samir accepts at 02:31 and follows the written first step, which is to confirm whether the source is the clinic remote-access pool. The evidence is a create time, an empty owner, a page fifteen minutes later, and then an accept. A chat line with no ticket number is the failure this control exists to stop.\n\nDefenders check that high-severity rules both open a ticket and carry an escalation time short enough to matter. Fifteen minutes is a common wait before the first page. They read a sample of tickets each week for missing evidence, missing owners, and automatic closure that hid a real event. They do not let automation close a high-severity ticket merely because the alert went quiet. Quiet can mean the attacker paused, or it can mean logging broke. A person remains the one who decides the incident is finished. Automation may open the ticket and escalate it. Automation should not declare the work done.\n\nStudents call the page itself a ticket, and they call any automation a guard rail or a provisioning job. A page can be missed and then it is gone. A ticket remains, with an owner field and a history. A guard rail stops a bad configuration before it is applied. Provisioning creates or disables accounts from the staffing feed. What separates them is that this control records an event that already happened and moves it to a person on a clock. If nobody can show a ticket number, an owner, and an escalation time, the alert is still only a line.',
        'r0': 'Open a ticket when a high-severity alert fires, and escalate if nobody accepts it in fifteen minutes. Ticket 4419 should contain the log lines, the severity, and the queue. The page at minute fifteen should be written on the ticket, not left only in chat. Attach later alerts for the same outage to the open ticket. Put the first response step in the ticket text. Do not let automation close a high-severity ticket only because the alert went quiet. Each week, sample tickets for missing owners. A page with no ticket number is only a signal. A guard rail and a staffing feed do not record this event.',
        'r1': 'Turn a serious alert into a ticket.\nPut the log lines into that ticket.\nName the queue that should own it.\nPage someone if nobody accepts the ticket.\nKeep the accept window short.\nWrite the page onto the ticket itself.\nKeep one ticket for one outage.\nPut the first action in the ticket text.\nDo not close it only because the noise stopped.\nA chat line is not a tracked ticket.\nA page with no number is easy to miss.\nA guard rail stops a bad setup instead.\nThis record is about an event that already happened.',
        'r2': 'A clinic bell does more than make a noise in the hall. The bell writes a line on a chart, names the ward, and includes the readings that caused the bell. If nobody accepts that chart line within a short wait, the bell rings the backup nurse and the chart records the second ring. Repeat bells for the same patient attach to the same line instead of starting a stack of new charts. The chart says what to do first. Silence after the first bell does not erase the line.\n\nThe chart line is the ticket, and the second ring is escalation when nobody accepts the work in time. A guard rail would have stopped a bad setup, and a roster change would have hired or removed a person, which this bell does not do.',
        'r4': 'Look at one high-severity alert from the last week. Write down the ticket number, the create time, and the accept time. If there is no ticket, write that down and do not build a new rule yet. Check whether a page was recorded on the ticket after the accept window. Note the written first step, if one is present. Do not close the ticket yet. See whether repeated alerts opened extra tickets for the same outage.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call a chat line or a page a ticket, or to call this control a guard rail. Ticket creation records the alert as work with an owner. Escalation reaches someone if nobody accepts the ticket within the clock, often fifteen minutes, and that page is written on the ticket. A guard rail prevents a bad configuration. Provisioning changes accounts from the staffing feed. A quiet alert does not erase the ticket. No ticket number means the control did not run.',
    },
    'Continuous integration and testing': {
        'body': "A change that reaches the live service without a build and a test is a bet the clinic should not be making. Continuous integration and testing means every proposed change is built and tested, including security checks, before that change is allowed to ship. The pipeline is the mechanism. It packages the change, runs the unit tests, scans the files for secrets, and checks libraries for known vulnerabilities. A failed stage blocks the path forward. The change stays off the live service while that stage is red.\n\nOrder matters inside the pipeline. The secret scan should see the files the person actually proposed, not an older copy. The unit tests should run against that same build. A library pinned to a version with a known CVE should fail the dependency stage even when the unit tests are green. Green tests do not mean the change is wise. They mean the checks you wrote did not fail. A person may still need to review the design. The pipeline's job is to make a skipped check visible and to stop the ship, not to replace judgment about whether the change should exist.\n\nMaya proposes a change to the billing service on Friday morning. The pipeline reaches the secret scan and fails. The log says an access-key pattern was found in a config file at line 40. Later stages do not matter until that scan is clean. The change does not ship. The live service does not contain that key. The evidence is a red stage named for the secret scan, the file name, the line number, and a blocked release. A green board with the key still in the file means the scan is not on the path that ships.\n\nDefenders treat a bypass of the pipeline as an incident, not as a favor to a hurried team. They keep the security stages on the required path, and they alarm when someone turns a required stage off. They repair unreliable tests rather than teaching people to ignore red. They do not leave a long-lived key in the build files just so the scan has something to find. They rotate any secret the scan does find, because the finding means the secret is already copied into the change history. They write down which stages are required before a ship is allowed.\n\nStudents hear the word automation and answer with the general benefits, or they hear about a messy script and talk about future cost. This topic is the gate on one change: build it, test it, scan it, and refuse to ship it when a stage fails. Benefits explain why repeating a safe step is worth the effort. The future cost of a shortcut is a different question, even when the shortcut lives inside a pipeline. What separates them is the failed stage that stops this particular change. If the question never mentions a build, a test, or a scan, a neighboring topic fits better.",
        'r0': "Build and test every proposed change, and include a secret scan plus a dependency check, before the change can ship. A known CVE in a pinned library fails the dependency stage even when unit tests pass. Maya's billing change should stop at the secret scan when an access-key pattern appears at line 40, and the release should stay blocked. Alarm if a required stage is turned off. Rotate any secret the scan finds, because that secret is already in the change history. A bypass of the pipeline is an incident, not a favor. This gate is not the general benefit of speed, and it is not the later cost of a shortcut. The failed stage is the fact that stops the ship.",
        'r1': 'Build every change before it reaches production.\nRun the tests on that same build.\nScan the proposed files for secrets.\nCheck libraries for known flaws.\nA failed stage blocks the release.\nDo not ship past a red stage.\nA green test is not a full design review.\nSkipping the pipeline is an incident.\nTurn a required stage back on if it was disabled.\nRotate any secret the scan finds.\nSpeed by itself is a different topic.\nThe later cost of a shortcut is different too.\nThe failed check is what stops this change.',
        'r2': 'Every plate stops at the kitchen pass. The cook finishes the plate, tastes the sauce, and looks for anything that does not belong, including a scrap of packaging that should never have entered the pan. If the taste fails or the scrap is found, the plate does not go to the dining room. A plate that tastes fine can still be a poor special, so a senior cook may still look. Turning the pass off to save a minute is how a bad plate reaches a guest. How fast the kitchen runs, and what a messy recipe will cost later, are real concerns, and they are not this stop.\n\nThe pass is the build and test that happen before a change ships. The failed taste is the stage that keeps this change off the live service.',
        'r4': 'Look at the latest failed change in the pipeline. Write down which stage failed and the log line that explains it. Do not bypass that stage yet. Check whether a secret scan and a library check sit on the required path. Note any required stage that someone turned off, and leave it off until you know why. Confirm that a found secret would be rotated, but do not rotate live secrets as part of this reading.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to answer with general speed, or with the later cost of a shortcut. Continuous integration builds and tests each change, including a secret scan and a check for known-flawed libraries, and a failed stage does not ship. A green test is not a design review. Bypassing the pipeline breaks the gate. Benefits describe the same safe steps repeated at a large scale. The future cost of a shortcut is a separate idea. If nothing is built or tested, the question is not this gate.',
    },
    'Benefits of automation': {
        'body': 'Doing a safe step once, by hand, proves that the step can work. Doing that same step the same way on every system, before the morning shift, is a different kind of gain. Benefits of automation are the consistency, the scale, and the speed you get when a correct step runs every time. One laptop is not locked while the next stays open because someone was tired. The work grows without hiring one person per machine. The time to react shrinks, because a job can start when the event happens, not when a person next sits down. Those gains exist only when the steps themselves are the right steps.\n\nThe mechanism is a job with a defined input and a defined result, run by an account that can reach the systems, on a schedule or on a trigger. A person writes the steps once, others review them, and then the job repeats them. The job writes a result for each system: success, failure, or skipped. A failure is visible. A skip is visible. A long list of quiet successes is visible too. Without that result line, speed is only a claim.\n\nThe clinic wants a five-minute screen lock on staff laptops. A person clicking through a hundred machines will not finish before morning, and a few machines will be missed. The job starts at 01:00. The log at 06:10 shows 100 laptops, 100 success lines, and a screen lock of five minutes. Spot checks on two laptops match the log. The evidence is a result per machine, a count, and a setting value, finished overnight. That page of output is the scale gain in a form a reviewer can check.\n\nDefenders keep the job aligned with the approved steps, and they spot-check a machine so the log is not the only witness. They measure how quickly an alert-triggered job actually starts, and they pause a job that is harming machines rather than letting speed finish the harm. They name a person who can stop the run. They do not treat a fast job as a reason to skip the test on the change that created the job. Speed multiplies a correct step, and it also multiplies a wrong one. A wrong step repeated on a hundred machines is not a benefit.\n\nStudents treat these gains as a promise that automation is simple, or they answer a question about later cost by saying the job was faster. Faster is a gain only for a correct repeated step. The future cost of a shortcut, including a fast script nobody else can repair, is a different topic. One account that can change every system is a failure risk, not a benefit, even though that account is fast. What separates them is whether the question asks what you gain from the same safe steps at a size people cannot click through by hand.',
        'r0': 'Run the same approved steps on every system and keep a result line for each one. A screen-lock job that starts at 01:00 should report 100 laptops and 100 successes, with the lock set to five minutes, finished by 06:10. That overnight run is the scale gain, because a person cannot click through a hundred machines before morning. Spot-check two hosts against the log. Name someone who can pause the job. Measure how fast an alert-triggered job starts. Do not skip tests on the change that created the job. Speed helps only when the step is the correct step. A shortcut nobody can repair is a later cost, not a benefit. One account that can change every system is a failure risk, not a benefit either.',
        'r1': 'Use the same safe steps every time.\nA tired click should not change the result.\nReach more machines than people can click by hand.\nStart the job when the event happens.\nWrite a result line for every machine.\nShow success, failure, and skip.\nA hundred machines can finish overnight.\nCheck a few machines against the log.\nName a person who can pause the job.\nFast helps when the step is right.\nFast hurts when the step is wrong.\nLater repair cost is not a benefit.\nOne account over every system is not a benefit either.',
        'r2': 'One worker can wrap a single crate with care. Wrapping a hundred crates the same way before dawn is a different kind of win. The wrap steps are written once and then repeated, so a tired hour does not skip the strap on crate forty. The sheet at dawn shows a line for each crate: done, failed, or skipped. Someone on duty can halt the line. A fast line that wraps the wrong goods has multiplied a mistake. A temporary brace left inside a wall, and a single key that opens every aisle, are not this win.\n\nThe repeated wrap is the gain from automation: the same safe steps, at a size people cannot finish by hand, done in time. The later cost of a shortcut, and one key that opens everything, belong to neighboring ideas.',
        'r4': 'Look at one job that ran overnight across many machines. Write down the counts of success, failure, and skip. Check one machine against the log and write down the setting you saw. Do not widen the job to more machines yet. Name the person who can pause the run. Note how long the run took. Do not treat a fast run as proof the step is correct until you compare it with the approved steps.',
        'exam': "The trap is to claim automation removes risk, or to call a shortcut's later cost a benefit. The gains are the same safe steps every time, scale past hand clicking, and a shorter time to react. A hundred laptops finished overnight is the picture, with a result line for each machine. A wrong step repeated at that scale is harm, not a gain. The future cost of a shortcut is a different topic. One account that can change every system is a failure risk. The question has to ask what you gain.",
    },
    'Complexity and technical debt': {
        'body': 'A shortcut that ships during a small incident can be the right call that afternoon, and it can also be a bill that arrives during the next incident. Technical debt is the future cost of a shortcut. Complexity and technical debt travel together when the shortcut grows branches that only its author can follow. Extra paths, unnamed scripts, and behavior nobody can explain without that author in the room all raise the bill. The cost shows up as hours, as risk, and as an outage that lasts until the one person who understands the shortcut is available. The expense was moved forward in time.\n\nThe way the bill grows is ordinary and easy to miss. Someone skips a cleaner design to meet a deadline. The script works. The next change takes longer because the reader must decode the shortcut before making a small fix. Sometimes the script has no owner, so during an incident it sits untouched. The future cost is the decoding, the failed repair, and the delay. That cost is the debt. The pile of branches is the complexity that makes the debt hard to pay.\n\nThe clinic deploy path was extended by a quick script during a holiday rush. The script has no comments. The last edit is eight months old, and the only author is Samir. Samir is on leave this week. At 03:40 the deploy fails on a certificate step inside that script. The duty notes say to ask Samir. The path sits untouched until morning, and the billing site stays on the old build. The evidence is a failing step nobody else will edit, a single author in the history, and a service waiting on a person who is not here. The hours until morning are part of the bill the shortcut deferred.\n\nDefenders name an owner for each piece of automation, write what it does in plain language, and schedule time to replace the shortcuts that only one person can fix. They record the debt where others can see it, as a ticket with a cost, not as a private worry. During a live incident they do not rewrite the only working path while the service is down. They stabilize first, then they pay the debt in a planned change. They resist adding another variant of the same script for one special case. Each new variant is more future cost.\n\nStudents call every piece of automation debt, and they also call one powerful account debt. A clear, owned job that sets a baseline on a hundred laptops is a gain, not a debt. One account that can change every system is a single failure point even when the code is easy to read. Debt is the later cost of a shortcut. What separates them is the unpaid later cost: extra time, extra risk, or an incident stalled because the shortcut has no owner. If the question is about one component whose loss or compromise hits every system, the failure-point topic fits better than debt.',
        'r0': 'Treat technical debt as the future cost of a shortcut. The clinic deploy script, with one author, no comments, and a failing certificate step at 03:40, is the picture. Samir is on leave, the path sits untouched, and the billing site stays on the old build until morning. Those hours are the bill the shortcut deferred. Name an owner, write what the script does, and schedule the replacement. Do not rewrite the only working path during the outage. Record the debt as a ticket with a cost. A clear job on a hundred laptops is a gain, not debt. One account that can change every server is a failure point, not debt. The unpaid later cost is the fact that decides this topic.',
        'r1': 'A shortcut can ship faster today.\nThe future cost of that shortcut is the debt.\nTangled branches make the bill hard to pay.\nScripts with no owner fail during an incident.\nOne author on leave can stall a deploy.\nWrite down what each automation does.\nName an owner for every job.\nSchedule time to replace the shortcut.\nDo not rewrite the only path during an outage.\nRecord the debt as a ticket others can see.\nA clear repeated job can be a gain.\nOne account over every system is another risk.\nAsk what the shortcut will cost later.',
        'r2': 'During a storm, a crew props a hallway with a temporary brace so people can pass. Months later the brace is still there, with no label, and the only carpenter who knows how it was tied is away. The next crew will not cut the brace, because they cannot see what it holds, and the hallway stays closed until morning. That delay is the bill the storm-day shortcut pushed into the future. A clean job that locks a hundred windows overnight is not this bill. One master key is not this bill either.\n\nTechnical debt is that future cost of the shortcut, and the unlabeled brace is the tangle that makes the cost hard to pay. The window job is a gain from repeating a safe step, and the master key is one object whose loss hits every room.',
        'r4': 'Look at one automation script that only one person will edit. Write down the author, the last change date, and what the duty notes say to do. Do not rewrite the script yet. Estimate the delay if that person is away, and record the delay as future cost. Open a ticket for the debt if none exists. Do not start the replacement during a live incident. Leave the working path in place until the service is stable.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call every automation debt, or to call one powerful account debt. Technical debt is the future cost of a shortcut. Complexity is what makes that cost hard to pay, such as a script only one person can fix while that person is on leave. A clear job on many machines is a gain. One automation account that can change every system is a failure point even when the code is easy to read. Ask what the shortcut will cost later, not merely whether a job exists.',
    },
    'Single points of failure in automation': {
        'body': "Automation concentrates reach so that one run can help a hundred systems, and that same concentration means one fault can hurt a hundred systems. Single points of failure in automation are the places where one fault stops every change or pushes one bad change to every system. A single automation account that can change every system is a single point of failure. The one orchestration host those jobs run on is a single point of failure as well. The one pipeline every change must pass through is another. If the account is stolen, the attacker inherits the right to change every system that account can change. If the host dies, no approved change can ship. If the pipeline learns a bad step, that bad step runs everywhere the pipeline reaches.\n\nReach is the mechanism. The account is a member of the admin group on every server, or it holds a role that can edit every configuration. The orchestration host stores the jobs and the credentials those jobs use. One login to that host is one login to the whole estate. A backup of the host matters, because a dead host with no restorable copy stops the clinic's changes. Restriction matters more, because a compromise of that host is a compromise of every system it touches. Use of that account outside the pipeline should raise an alarm.\n\nThe clinic service account sits in the local administrators group on 400 servers. The night job log shows that account pushing a configuration at 01:00, with 400 success lines from one source address. No second account can push a fix if this one is disabled. The jobs run on a single host, and the only people who can log in to it are listed in a small file that has not been reviewed this year. The evidence is one account name on every host, one source address for the pushes, and one host whose loss stops the pushes. That shape is the failure point, even while tonight's run looks healthy.\n\nDefenders split the reach. The account that configures laptops should not also be the account that edits the identity directory or the firewall. They back up the orchestration host and they test the restore. They limit who can log in to that host, and they alarm a login that is not the job itself. They keep an emergency path that stays sealed, so a dead pipeline does not force someone to hand admin to every engineer. They do not store the automation password in a chat or in a script on a laptop.\n\nStudents answer this topic with technical debt, because one person who understands a script feels similar. Debt is the future cost of a shortcut, and that cost can exist in code that touches only one system. A failure point is about reach and about the lack of a second path. A script can be clear, documented, and still be the only account that can change every server. What separates them is that one component's failure or compromise stops every change or poisons every system.",
        'r0': 'Treat one automation account in the administrators group on all 400 servers as a single point of failure. The night push from that account, with 400 success lines from one source address, shows the reach. Back up the single orchestration host and test that you can restore it. Limit who can log in to that host, and alarm a login that is not the job. Split accounts so laptop configuration cannot also edit the identity directory. Keep a sealed emergency path. Do not put the automation password in chat. A clear, commented script can still be this failure point if its account can change every system. Debt asks about the later cost of a shortcut. This topic asks what one component can reach.',
        'r1': 'One fault in automation can hit every system.\nOne account that can change everything is a risk.\nA stolen account hands that reach to the thief.\nOne dead host can stop every approved change.\nOne bad step can run on every system.\nSplit accounts by what they are allowed to change.\nBack up the host that runs the jobs.\nTest that the backup can be restored.\nLimit who can log in to that host.\nAlarm a login that is not the job itself.\nKeep a sealed path for real emergencies.\nDo not store the job password in chat.\nHow far one component reaches is the fact here.',
        'r2': 'One desk in a warehouse holds the only key that opens every aisle, and one clerk is the only person allowed to use it. If that key is stolen, the thief can open every aisle the key opens. If the clerk is gone and there is no second key, no scheduled move happens. If the clerk is taught a wrong route, that wrong route is driven in every aisle. A second key cut for a single aisle would limit the damage. A messy note only one clerk understands is a real problem, and it is about understanding rather than about this reach.\n\nThe all-aisle key is one automation account that can change every system. The messy note is the later cost of a shortcut, even when the note touches only one aisle.',
        'r4': 'Look at the automation account and list the groups it belongs to. Write down how many systems that account can change. Do not remove it from those groups yet. Find the host the jobs run on and note whether a tested backup exists. Write down who can log in to that host. Do not rotate the account password yet. Check whether a second account could ship an emergency fix. Leave the sealed emergency path sealed while you read.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to describe hard-to-read code instead of reach. A single automation account that can change every system is a single point of failure. One orchestration host can stop every change, or it can push a bad change everywhere. A compromise of that host is a compromise of every system it touches. The future cost of a shortcut is a different topic and can live in code that touches one system. A backup and a split of accounts address the failure point. Clear writing does not shrink how far the account reaches.',
    },
    'Security content automation protocol': {
        'body': "Scanners disagree when each one writes results in a private format, and a dashboard then needs a custom parser for every tool. Security content automation protocol files, usually shortened to SCAP, are a family of standards for describing checks, results, and configuration so tools can share them. SCAP is a language for checking configuration baselines. SCAP is not a firewall. Nothing in this language allows or denies a packet. A firewall sits on a network path and decides which traffic may pass. This language sits in files that say what setting was expected and what setting was found.\n\nThree pieces get collapsed into one product name, and that collapse causes the exam mistakes. A baseline says what good looks like for a class of system. A check language states each setting and the value you expect to find. OVAL is the check language inside this family. A result format records pass or fail, the evidence, and which baseline the check came from. A scanner reads the checks, looks at the host, and writes the result in that shared format. A second product can read the result without learning the first scanner's private database. The protocol does not, by itself, collect the facts. Software on the host, or a login from outside, does the collecting. The protocol is how the check and the result are written down.\n\nThe clinic scores new servers with two scanners from two vendors. Both emit a result file the same dashboard can import. Check 14 is password length, expected 14, found 8, result fail, on host chart-03. The dashboard did not grow a new parser when the second scanner arrived. The evidence is a standard result with a check id, an expected value, a found value, and a pass or fail, identical in shape across tools. Importing that file does not create a firewall rule. If someone points at a deny rule and calls the rule SCAP, they have named the wrong object.\n\nDefenders ask which baseline was expressed, which scanner produced the file, and whether the dashboard consumed the standard result or a one-off spreadsheet. They update the check content when the baseline changes. They do not treat a passing score as proof that an attacker who already has a valid password is harmless. A configuration baseline says the settings match a guide. It does not watch live misuse of a legitimate account. They also refuse to draw this protocol on a diagram in the spot where a firewall belongs.\n\nThe exam trap calls this protocol a firewall, or calls it the benchmark itself. A benchmark is the agreed set of settings you measure against. This protocol is the language those checks and results are written in so tools can share them. Software installed on the host may gather the facts, and that software is not the language. What separates them is the job the object does. If the object filters traffic, it is a firewall or a similar control. If it describes checks and results for a configuration baseline, it is this language.",
        'r0': 'Use SCAP as the shared language for configuration checks and results, not as a firewall. OVAL states the setting and the expected value inside that family. A result file should show check 14, password length, expected 14, found 8, fail, on host chart-03, in a shape a second scanner can also emit. The dashboard should import that file without a custom parser for each tool. Ask which baseline the checks came from. Do not read a passing score as proof that a stolen password is harmless. Do not draw this protocol where a deny rule belongs. The benchmark is the agreed settings. This protocol is how tools share the checks and the results. It does not allow or deny packets.',
        'r1': 'This is a shared language for check results.\nTools can trade results without a custom reader.\nThe language describes how baselines are checked.\nThe language is not a firewall.\nThe language does not allow or block packets.\nA baseline says what good looks like.\nA check states the value you expect.\nA result records what was actually found.\nA scanner still has to look at the host.\nA passing score is not proof the host is safe.\nThe agreed settings list is a different object.\nSoftware installed on the host is different too.\nAsk which baseline the checks came from.',
        'r2': "Two clinics trade checkup sheets. A shared form says which question was asked, what answer was expected, and what answer was found, so the second clinic can read the sheet without learning the first clinic's private notes. The form is not the lock on the pharmacy door. The lock allows or refuses entry. The form only describes a checkup. The list of questions a medical board agreed to ask is a related object, and it is not the form's grammar. A person standing in the room taking measurements is not the form either.\n\nThe shared form is the language for configuration checks and results. The pharmacy lock is a firewall, which this language is not, and the agreed questions are the benchmark you measure against.",
        'r4': 'Look at one imported result file. Write down the check id, the expected value, and the found value. Note whether a second tool could have produced the same shape of file. Do not add a firewall rule because of this file. Record which baseline the checks belong to. Do not treat a pass as proof a stolen password is harmless. Leave the scanner settings unchanged while you read. Confirm you are looking at a language for baseline results, not at a device in the packet path.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call this protocol a firewall, or to call it the benchmark. SCAP is a language for checking configuration baselines. It describes checks and results so tools can share them without a custom parser for each tool. It does not allow or deny packets. A benchmark is the agreed settings you measure against. Software on the host may collect facts, and that software is not the language. A passing score does not mean a stolen password is harmless. If the object filters traffic, it is not this language.',
    },
    'Benchmarks': {
        'body': "Two administrators can both believe a server is hardened and still disagree on every setting that matters. Benchmarks turn that argument into a list you can score. A benchmark is an agreed set of settings, such as a hardened build guide, that you measure systems against. The agreement is the point. Screen lock, disk encryption, which services stay off, and which remote logins are refused are written down as expected values. A live system either matches a line or it does not. Opinion moves to the day the clinic adopts the guide, not to the hour each person builds a host.\n\nMeasurement is a comparison. For each setting, the guide states the expected value and a check reads the live value. A match is a pass. A mismatch is a fail. The list of fails is the gap list. A score with no gap list is a number nobody can repair. Each gap should become a ticket with an owner, or an exception with a reason, an owner, and a date when the exception expires. An exception that never expires is a quiet choice to abandon that line of the guide. New servers are scored before they take traffic, and older servers are scored on a schedule so drift becomes visible.\n\nThe clinic adopts one published server benchmark for new builds. Server chart-03 is scored on delivery day: 186 pass and 22 fail. The first fail is remote login allowed from any address. Ticket 4420 is opened for that gap, assigned to the platform queue, and due before the server takes clinic traffic. Three other fails are exceptions, each with a reason and a review date. The evidence is a score, a gap list, and either a ticket or a dated exception for every gap. A score sent by mail with no tickets behind it is a report, not a control.\n\nDefenders pick one benchmark for a class of system and they stop mixing three guides in one sentence. They score the server again after the fix, and they keep both the before and the after. They do not edit the guide's expected value on the host that failed, just to turn the line green. They do not treat a high score as a substitute for logs, because a host can match a build guide and still be misused by a valid account. They review exceptions on a set calendar. They record who adopted the benchmark and which version, so next year's guide is a conscious change rather than a surprise.\n\nStudents call the scanner the benchmark, and they call the result language the benchmark as well. The scanner is the tool that collects a value. The shared result language carries checks and results between tools, and it is not a firewall. The benchmark is the agreed list you measure against. What separates them is the question each object answers. The list answers which settings were agreed. The language answers how tools share a check. The scanner answers how a live value was collected. If the question asks which settings were agreed, the benchmark is the topic.",
        'r0': 'Measure new servers against one published benchmark before they take traffic. Chart-03 scoring 186 pass and 22 fail, with remote login from any address as the first fail, should produce ticket 4420 or a dated exception. Record the benchmark name and the version. Score the host again after the fix and keep both results. Do not edit the expected value just to turn a line green. Review exceptions on a set date. A high score does not replace log review. The shared check language can carry these results between tools, and that language is not a firewall. The benchmark is the agreed set of settings you measure against, not the scanner and not the file format.',
        'r1': 'Agree on the settings before you measure a host.\nA benchmark is that agreed list.\nCompare each live setting with the list.\nA mismatch is a gap to repair.\nTurn gaps into tickets or dated exceptions.\nScore a new server before it takes traffic.\nScore older servers often enough to see drift.\nWrite down the guide name and the version.\nDo not edit the goal just to force a pass.\nA high score does not replace log review.\nThe scanner is the tool, not the list.\nA shared result language is not the list.\nThat language is also not a firewall.',
        'r2': 'Before a new wing of a building opens, inspectors carry an agreed list. The list says which doors must lock, which alarms must arm, and which closets must stay empty. Each line is a pass or a fail against the wing in front of them. Failures become work orders, or dated waivers with a name attached. Nobody rewrites a line on the clipboard just to make a failing door count as a pass. A high count of passes does not send the night guard home. The clipboard is not the grammar inspectors use when they trade sheets, and it is not the lock on the door.\n\nThe agreed list is the benchmark you measure systems against. The grammar for trading sheets is the check language, and the lock is a firewall, not the list.',
        'r4': 'Look at the newest server score. Write down the benchmark name, the version, the pass count, and the fail count. Open the first gap and see whether a ticket or a dated exception exists. Do not edit the expected value yet. Do not mark a fail as a pass to clear a dashboard. Note the review date on any exception. Leave the guide text unchanged. Keep this list separate from the language used to share results between tools.',
        'exam': 'The trap is to call the scanner or the result language the benchmark. A benchmark is an agreed set of settings you measure systems against. Gaps become tickets or dated exceptions. Editing the expected value to force a pass abandons the measure. The shared language carries checks and results between tools, and that language is not a firewall. Software on the host can collect a fact, and that software is not the list. A high score does not replace monitoring. If the question asks which settings were agreed, this is the topic.',
    },
    'Agents and agentless checks': {
        'body': "Some facts about a machine are visible only from inside it, and some devices will not let you install anything. Agents and remote checks are two ways to gather a fact, and they fail in different ways. An agent is software on the host. That software can see local files, processes, and settings, and it reports what it sees. A check that does not place software on the host stays outside. That outside check logs in remotely or queries an API. The outside check sees less, because it only learns what that login or that API is willing to show. The path it uses is a door, and the door has to stay locked.\n\nThe agent is installed, runs as a service, and sends results on a schedule or when an important file changes. A file integrity agent hashes selected programs and alerts when a hash moves. The outside check needs a credential and a service it can reach. For a server, that may be a remote login on port 22, used only from the management network, with every login recorded. For a cloud resource, the check calls an API and reads configuration. A switch often has nowhere to install your software, so the remote login or the API is the only practical check.\n\nPayroll servers run the file integrity agent. At 03:14 the agent on payroll-01 reports that the hash of the sudo program changed, and the alert names the host, the old hash, and the new hash. The core switch cannot run that software. Each night a job logs in from the management network on port 22 and records that a remote admin service is enabled, plus the account that logged in. On the server, the evidence is an event from software running on that host. On the switch, the evidence is a remote login, or an API query, from an address you recognize, using an account reserved for checks. If that account can log in from anywhere, the check's own path has become the problem.\n\nDefenders keep an inventory of three piles: hosts that have the agent, devices checked from outside, and machines that have neither. They alarm when an agent stops reporting, because silence can mean the software was removed. For outside checks they record the account, the port or the API, and the source addresses allowed to use them. They store that account in the vault. They do not remove the agent during an incident before they have saved the last report.\n\nStudents call a remote login an agent that lives somewhere else, and they call the check language an agent as well. Software running on the host is the agent. A login from another machine, or a query to an API, is a check with no software installed on the host. The shared language for baseline results is not a firewall and is not host software. The agreed settings list is not host software either. What separates them is where the software runs. If nothing was installed on the host, there is no agent, however clever the remote query looks.",
        'r0': 'Install an agent, which is software on the host, where you need a local fact such as a file hash. The payroll-01 report at 03:14, naming the sudo program and both the old hash and the new hash, is that case. For a switch that cannot run the software, log in remotely on port 22 from the management network, or query the device API, and record the account used. Alarm when an agent stops reporting. Store the remote account in a vault, and do not allow that login from the whole network. Do not remove the agent during an incident before you save the last report. The shared check language is not host software. A benchmark is the settings list. The agent is the software on the host.',
        'r1': 'An agent is software running on the host.\nThe software can see local files and programs.\nThe software sends a report of what it finds.\nA check with no installed software stays outside.\nThat outside check logs in from another machine.\nThe same check can ask a service the device offers.\nOutside checks see less than a local agent.\nThe login path has to stay locked down.\nSome devices cannot run extra software.\nCheck those devices from the outside anyway.\nAlarm if an agent stops sending reports.\nSave the last report before you remove anything.\nA result language is not software on the host.',
        'r2': "A resident inspector lives in the equipment room, sees the machines up close, and writes a note when a cover is opened. A visitor inspector does not live in that room. The visitor is let in, looks only at what the doorway shows, and then leaves. Some rooms have no place for a resident to stay, so a visitor is the only way to look. The visitor's badge is a risk of its own and should open only that one room. A shared inspection form, and the agreed list of what safe means, are not the person standing in the room.\n\nThe resident is an agent, which is software on the host. The visitor is a check that logs in from outside or asks the device a question, and that path has to stay locked.",
        'r4': "Look at one host that should be running an agent. Write down the service name and the time of the last report. Do not uninstall that software yet. Pick one device that cannot run it. Write down whether the check logs in remotely or queries an API, and which account it uses. Do not widen that account's network access yet. Note the source addresses allowed to use the account. Save any report you are relying on before you plan a removal.",
        'exam': 'The trap is to call a remote login an agent, or to call the check language an agent. An agent is software on the host and can see local facts such as a changed file hash. A check with no software on the host logs in remotely or queries an API. That check sees less, and it depends on a path that must stay locked. The shared language describes baseline results. It is not a firewall and it is not host software. Where the software runs is the fact that separates them.',
    },
}
